059,"JUST FOR | DEFENDING Sa | WOMEN’S RIGHTS. Ba | AMNESTY | INTERNATIONAL | LOCKED UP FOR DEFENDING WOMEN’S | RIGHT TO CHOOSE HOW THEY DRESS. | NOW NASRIN NEEDS YOUR HELP. | Nasrin at home with her son Nima, who is | now 12 years old. Nima is now facing being | Dear Friend, left without his mum and dad. | “Il am sending you my tears of love, hoping they make the injustice more tolerable.” | These are Nasrin Sotoudeh’s words from prison in Iran, to her two children. She has been wrenched | apart from them, just for standing up for what’s right. Now she faces a brutal sentence of 148 lashes | and 38 years in prison in Tehran. | Nasrin is a human rights lawyer in Iran. It’s an incredibly risky job, but she has dedicated her life to | defending the rights of others. Whether that means fighting to end the death penalty, or battling laws | that punish women and girls unless they cover themselves in public. | Her commitment to justice has set Iran’s authorities against her. They have thrown her in prison twice: | once in 2010 and again in 2019. She remains in prison today. Nasrin is being held in Shahr-e Rey | prison, a notorious women’s prison, where torture and mistreatment are rife. | Will you donate £20 to Amnesty International UK Section Charitable Trust today? Your gift could help | us Campaign for Nasrin’s freedom and help reunite her with her family. | Right now, Amnesty International is working to protect human rights across the globe. We are | committed to shining a light on injustice, and standing up for people who stand up for others — just | like Nasrin. | In Iran, women and girls above a certain age are required by law not to leave their homes unless they | cover their hair, arms and legs. Women that don’t, face prison, flogging or a fine. | In 2018, a movement against the forced veiling law erupted when one woman staged a solo act of | resistance. She removed her headscarf and silently waved it on the end of a stick. Countless women | across Iran joined her, and became known as the ‘Girls of Revolution Street’. | Nasrin defended some of these women in court, but was then herself arrested. By going after her, the | lranian authorities are attacking women’s rights. All women should be free to choose what they wear, | and lawyers should be free to defend their right to do so. | A gift of £20 could help us publicise the facts of Nasrin’s case, to increase the pressure on the | lranian authorities. | After two grossly unjust trials, Nasrin now faces 148 lashes and 38 years in prison — the harshest | sentence against a human rights defender in Iran for years. Charges against her include “inciting | corruption and prostitution”. | It’s part of a huge crackdown on free speech and dissent in Iran. More and more people like Nasrin | are locked up, tortured, intimidated and more, just for speaking up for human rights. The authorities | carry out floggings and have executed hundreds of people. | Amnesty International United Kingdom Section Charitable Trust, The Human Rights Action Centre, 17-25 New Inn Yard, London EC2A 3EA | Telephone +44 (0)20 7033 1500 Facsimile +44 (0)20 7033 1503 www.amnesty.org.uk | A charity registered in England and Wales (1051681) and Scotland (SCO39534). Registered office 17-25 New Inn Yard London EC2A 3EA | Please turn over | Nasrin has had to cope with having the coronavirus too, on top of other health problems. | In November 2020, Nasrin was briefly released from prison on temporary leave. She was suffering | serious ill health following a 46 day hunger strike: a protest against the unfair conditions her and | other prisoners were being held in. During her release Nasrin tested positive for the coronavirus, which | her husband says she caught in prison. | Nasrin was cruelly returned to prison on 2 December 2020, leaving her desperate family behind. This | was done against the recommendation of health professionals who said she remained infectious with | the disease. | We are now very worried for Nasrin’s health inside a dangerous, unhygienic and overcrowded prison. | Prisoners can’t social distance and receive limited health care. Nasrin is among the hundreds | of wrongly imprisoned people in Iran. No one should spend a single day in prison for peacefully | exercising their rights. | Your gift today could help Amnesty International lobby governments around the world to intervene in | cases like Nasrin’s. | We know this works. With the help of people like you, Amnesty International has secured the release | of lawyers, journalists and photographers the world over. Your gifts mean we can investigate abuse, | hold people to account, and push decision-makers to take action in defence of human rights. | The injustice of Nasrin’s case has already touched the hearts of hundreds of thousands of people. | Now we need to do even more — so the authorities in Iran feel compelled to act. | Together we can show Iran that humanity always wins. | Thank you | Rosie Leatherland | Campaigns Manager - Individuals at Risk | PS Your donation today could be critical if Nasrin is to walk free. Your support could help us pressure | the Iranian government, so Nasrin can be reunited with her family. | TOGETHER WE CAN FREE NASRIN | SHAWKAN, EGYPT TRAN THI NGA, VIETNAM | Jailed for photographing Jailed for peacefully protesting | a protest. against a deadly chemical spill. | E NASRIN SOTOUDEH, IRAN | Locked up for defending women’s right to choose how they dress. | How do | tell you where | am when you are | so innocent and too young to comprehend | the meaning of words such as prison, arrest, | sentence, trial and injustice? | You asked me, “Mummy, are you coming | home with us today?” and | was forced to | respond in plain view of the security agents: | “My work is going to take a while so I’Il | come home later.” | It was then that you nodded as if to say you | understand and took my hand, giving it a | sweet, childlike kiss with your small lips. | | once told you: “My daughter | hope you | never think that | was not thinking of you | or that it was my actions that deserved such | punishment... Everything | have done is | legal and within the framework of the law.” | It was then that you lovingly caressed my | face with your small hands and replied: | “I know, Mummy, | know.” | Here’s how you can help | £15 could help Amnesty International campaign to set Nasrin and other wrongly imprisoned | people free so they can be reunited with their families once again. | £20 could help fund Amnesty International’s research and investigations to tell the world how | people like Nasrin are suffering at the hands of oppressive governments. | £40 could help us to lobby Iran and other governments around the world that seek to silence | people like Nasrin for defending human rights. | To make your donation, fill in the form and return it in the Freepost envelope, or | visit www.amnesty.org.uk/nasrinfree | TOGETHER WE CAN FREE NASRIN | YES, | WILL HELP FREE NASRIN | coulel help firanesty Indernational campaign fr sa | i) MY DONATION Ne ntamiinmenapel | Please accept my gift of £15 [ ] €20V) £40 [| OR | prefer to give £ | CHEQUE [_| | enclose a cheque/postal order/CAF voucher | payable to: Amnesty International (UK Section) Charitable Trust | DEBIT/CREDIT CARD Yat sode | Please debit my Mastercard |_| Visa{_] Maestro |_| CAF [_] | cadno: LT PILE ET ICE EC) CTT cotaesteo oniy | Valid from: LL / LL Expiry date: CLV Issue no: L (Maestro only) | Signature: Date: Cv /LA | ONLINE | Visit www.amnesty.org.uk/nasrinfree to make an online donation. | 2 YOUR DETAILS: (We can't process your donation without this information) | Tite == First Name oe 7 | Last Name | __ Postcode | If you are under 18, please tell us your date of birth so that | 5 I | II | | a | I is || | | é : DI/DI—-|MI|MI~-| YU} YU YIUY | we can send you information that is relevant to you. a _IL— a | WE TAKE YOUR PRIVACY SERIOUSLY The information that you provide will be heid by Amnesty International UK Section and Amnesty | International UK Section Charitable Trust (AIUK). We need this to process your donations. | We will also write to you to keep you updated about AIUK’s campaigns, fundraising appeals, events and other ways you can help.If you do | not want us to keep you updated by post please tick here [_] | If you would also like to hear about this work by email and/or telephone, please provide your preferred email address and/or telephone | number below. | | retophone al | You can opt out of communications at any time by clicking on the unsubscribe button in our emails, or emailing sct@amnesty.org.uk or | writing to us at Amnesty International UK, Human Rights Action Centre, 17-25 New Inn Yard, London EC2A 3EA. For more information | about how AIUK processes personal data, please visit: www.amnesty.org.uk/privacy | 3) GIFT AID DECLARATION | If you are a UK taxpayer we can claim back the tax on your donations — that’s an extra 25p on every £1 you give. ; nid Fr | (_] | would like Amnesty International (UK Section) Charitable Trust to treat all donations that | have made in | the past 4 years and all donations that | make from the date of this declaration as Gift Aid donations. | NB. You must pay an amount of UK Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax in each tax year (6th April to 5th April) at least equal to | the tax that all the charities or Community Amateur Sports Clubs that you donate to will reclaim on your donations for that tax year | (25p on every £1 you give). | Please be aware that you will be responsible for any tax shortfall if you have paid insufficient tax to cover the Gift Aid claimed. | We are committed to the highest standards in fundraising. Read our promise to you: www.amnesty.org.uk/promise | Amnesty International works globally to protect human rights and stand up for the rights of individuals. We focus on the issues | and people most in need at any time, so your donation may be used to fund other areas of our work. | Please return this completed