084,"ETHIOPIA EMERGENCY APPEAL | MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES | DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | Albert Vilas, MSF emergency | coordinator in Tigray, Ethiopia | Hundreds of thousands | of people have been | forced to leave their | homes in the Tigray region of | northern Ethiopia after fighting | broke out in November last year. | MSF teams have been providing medical care to | affected people since mid-December. Albert Viiias | is MSF’s emergency coordinator in Tigray. | “After several attempts, we finally entered the capital of | Tigray, Mekele, more than a month after the violence | started. The city was quiet. There was electricity, but no | basic supplies. The local hospital was running at 30 to | 40 per cent, with little medication. There were almost | no patients, which is always a very bad sign. | In Adigrat, the situation was very tense and the hospital | was in a terrible condition. Most of the staff had left, | there were hardly any medicines and no food and water. | Some patients were malnourished. | We supplied the hospital with medicines and bought | emergency food in the markets that were still open. | Together with the remaining staff, we cleaned the | building and organised waste collection. Little by little | we rehabilitated the hospital so that it could function. | NO TELEPHONES | Beyond the hospitals, around 80 or 90 per cent of the | health centres we visited were not functional. | One woman had been in labour for seven days without | being able to give birth. Her life was saved because | MSF teams distribute essential items to families displaced by the fighting. | Photograph © Claudia Blume/MSF | we were able to transport her to Mekele. I saw people | arrive at hospital on bicycles carrying a patient from | 30 km away. | The impact of the violence is visible in the buildings | and cars with bullet holes. We saw a population | locked in their homes and living in fear. Everyone | gave us pieces of paper with phone numbers written | on them and asked us to convey messages to their | families. People don't even know if their relatives and | loved ones are okay because in many places there are | still no telephones or telecommunications. | Were very concerned about what may be happening | in rural areas. Our mobile medical teams are visiting | areas outside the main cities and we've reopened some | health centres. You feel that you offer people some | hope: the feeling that things can improve after two | months without good news.’ | Hospitals, healthcare centres and clinics across | Ethiopia’s Tigray region have been looted, | vandalised and destroyed. | MSF medical teams are working to refurbish hospitals | and provide urgent medical care to people caught | up in this crisis. We are providing medical support in | emergency rooms, maternity wards, and outpatient | departments and are running mobile clinics in rural | towns and villages. We need your help now more | than ever. | MSF relies on donations | from people like you. | £40 _ could provide pain relief to 29 patients | could provide dressings kits for 95 | £115 wounded patients | could cover the costs of a doctor working | £228 in remote clinics for three days | £866 could pay for a portable ultrasound | device to help medical teams detect | underlying health issues in patients | Please give what you can to our medical | teams — they’ll give everything they can | in return. | THANK YOU | DONATE NOW | CALL 0800 055 7985 | or make your donation online at: | msf.org.uk/tigray | Scan to donate | Please give what you can to our medical teams — they’ll give everything they can in return | | WOULD LIKE TO MAKE A DONATION OF £ | __ | Title CET ET LT] Forename [TTT TTT ETT I | f ; rr Sumame Sg Poe FR ee a Ge Ha G2 Da sO ae | ease make your cheque/charity voucher ins Sans Frontiéres | Tae ES, Tease oe i Address ARBRE BES RGSS | es ee ec conrimba sale peg feet dtl de ale bt Dok pdb cb ded | Cardholder name | | | | | | | | | | | if | ae LI | | Postcode RBRESSScr SERRE RBRERER