G.UK/DONATE YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | Bentiu <r | s { The hospital is run by more than | 500 MSF staff, most of whom | Protection | -=-= | of Civilians | camp | Bentiu Protection of Civilians camp | in South Sudan is home to some | 100,000 people who have sought | refuge from conflict. Residents live in | temporary shelters made of corrugated | metal, plant stalks, mud and plastic | sheeting. These offer limited protection | from the elements, especially during | the rainy season when walkways | become lanes of mud. People’s medical | needs are enormous. | MSF operates the main hospital in the | UN-run camp, which provides general | medical care, surgery, treatment for malaria, | malnutrition and diseases linked to poor | water and along with | for tuberculosis and HIV. | _ Na! Ne | RA \ EES | “MSF is operating one of the only hospitals | 4 a t & | thar has surgical services — not only in Bentiu, | but in the wider region,” says MSF surgeon | John Buckles, who has recurned to Bentiu | five times to conduct surgeries. “Working | here, you're faced with a broad spectrum | of conditions, ranging from tropical diseases | to surgical conditions that occur in any | society where there are abdominal issues, | hrough to gency All of | these conditions have continued during | the COVID-19 pandemic.” | Each ward of the 120-bed b> $ | hospital is housed in a white Le | tent connected by covered q Weter tanks_ _ ‘kk | walkways. “I was working wy —* | 12 to 15 hours a day for seven — | Russtration Jere Padery | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | Sj LA | FS ge om | “From my point of view as a surgeon, | Bentiu and operated in a tent with a | small air-conditioning unit dripping onto | the floor. | was very happy with the | surgical outcomes this latest trip. We | had a lot of very sick patients, but we | didn't lose any of them.” | isn't replaceable. If MSF wasn't there, _ | pleased that you've been able to heip, | while also knowing that, if it wasn’t | thousands of people would be without | the medical care and lifesaving | surgery that they need. | “A little bit of money goes a long | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | AROUND THE WORLD | Around the | world with MSF | WE’RE PROUD OF HOW WE SPEND | YOUR CONTRIBUTIONS | (MSF UK Annual Report 2019) | 85% 13% | ON OUR HUMANITARIAN WORK ON FUNDRAISING (WE RAISE | £8.24 FOR EVERY £1 WE SPEND) | ON OFFICE MANAGEMENT | Photograph © Avra | Fiatas/MSF | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE | PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | ITALY | Over winter, hundreds of mi | and refugees travelled the ‘Balkan | route’ on foot to reach Italy. The | Helmand \ | Photograph © Andrew Quilty | 4 SOUTH SUDAN | MSF physiotherapist Birgit | Schénharting plays with | five-year-old Anyar and helps | him regain the use of his leg | after he was bitten by a snake | SESE ESE while playing outside his house. | Photograph © Damaris Giulana/MSF | MOZAMBIQUE | Photograph © MSF | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE | PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE",Newspaper,NA,NA
155,"want | @ UNICEF Pan) | Here is my gift of: [_]£35[_]£50[_]£75[_]£100 | | prefer to give £ Bees | Please make your cheque / postal order payable to UNICEF or please debit | my MasterCard / Visa / Delta / CAF card (delete as appropriate) | AS COVID-19 | Card number | SBEE SPR eRe Re | vot rom: LVL T]} espiry sore: LLL] | SURGES, INDIA. S@.~ | IS G AS PIN G * Jee Spal “x : t F | re | FOR BREATH gc Gee ® I | Full name | Address | Postcode | if you are a UK taxpayer please tick the box below so we can claim | back 25p for every £1 you give at no extra cost to you. | (1 Yes, | would like all donations | have made to UK Committee for UNICEF | for the four years prior to this year and all donations | make in the future, | to be treated as Gift Aid donations* | *| am a UK taxpayer and understand that if | pay less Income tax and/or capital | Gains Tax in the current tax year than the amount of Gift Aid claimed on all my | donations it is my responsibility to pay any difference. Please remember to notify | A deadly wave of COVID-19 is surging through India. Over 20 | million cases have been reported as the virus sweeps across | the nation — and more children are falling ill than ever before. | us if you want to cancel this declaration, change your name or home address or | if you no longer pay sufficient tax on your income and/or capital gains | UNICEF would like to keep you up to date with our work and | other ways you Can sypport us by post. If you would prefer | Hospitals across India are overwhelmed, and many are having to hBianbe contacted’ please tik hecst) | turn families away without medical help. Please donate today and | help provide life-saving access to oxygen, medical treatment and | vaccines to meet the critical need in India and around the world. £50 | If you'd like to change how you hear from us or to discuss | your donation, please email supportercare@unicef.org.uk | or call us on 0300 330 5580. Our Privacy Policy sets out | how we collect, use and store your personal information. | You can read it at unicef.uk/privacy | could help | provide an oxygen | concentrator | #GiveTheWorldAShot | Registered with | Your gift will support UNICEF's life-saving work to bring an end to the | Covid-19 pandemic in India and around the world. In the unlikely event | that the funds raised exceed UNICEF's funding needs for this appeal, | your donation will go to our Children’s Emergency Fund INDRAISIN | REGULATOR | Registered Charity no. 1072612 (England & Wales) SC043677 (Scotland) NDRPLO3003 | ll elt ee ee eee",Newspaper,NA,NA
156,"YEMEN EMERGENCY APPEAL 4 Secrexozzz | MSF anaesthetist Lenk | in Dhi As-Sufal hospital in | ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | MIS AN MSF NURSE WHO | WORKED IN MARIB | The conflict in Yemen is now in its sixth year. In Marib | in central Yemen, nearly three million people have been | displaced from their homes and are living in makeshift | camps close to the frontline. Pregnant women and | children are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, we found the local hospitals | overwhelmed by the sheer number of people and by an | influx of war-wounded. Around 2.7 million people are | in the province now, many of them displaced from their | homes elsewhere and looking for a safe haven. | People were living in makeshift shelters made of plastic | and metal sheets. There were no latrines, just pits dug in | the ground outside the tents. People were very worried | about their health, about the risk of disease, about not | having enough food. We were very concerned about the | health of mothers and children, and pregnant women | were a major worry too. | Over the next month, our small team worked around. | the clock to set up clinics providing basic healthcare | and to supply hospitals that were struggling. Getting | medicines to this part of Yemen wasn't easy. Supplies | often had to cross frontlines. One of our trucks carrying | antibiotics, medicines and other supplies was hijacked. | It was an incredibly difficult environment to work in. | Along with general healthcare, we provided routine | vaccinations for children and offered antenatal and | postnatal care. We saw a lot of pregnant women and | children and were able to refer pregnant women | with complications to larger hospitals for emergency | caesareans. It made a real difference. | A DESPERATE SITUATION | The day we started working in a rural hospital near the | frontline, a woman came in with a breech delivery who | was having real difficulty. Our midwife assisted and | everything went well. But who knows what would have | happened to that woman if the hospital wasn't there. | Since I left, many more people have come to the area. | But Marib is no longer a safe place to be. In recent | weeks, the frontlines have moved into the province, | causing large numbers of injuries and forcing thousands | more people to flee their homes. As the frontline | approaches Marib city, we are concerned that people | sheltering in the area may find themselves with no | place else to go. | After six years of conflict, the situation is desperate. | These people need our help now more than ever.” | WHAT IS HAPPENING IN YEMEN? | Yemen is the midst of civil war. After six years of conflict, the | medical and humanitarian situation in Yemen continues to | deteriorate. Poverty is widespread, malnutrition is on the rise | and people are confronted by the daily threat of violence. | Pregnant women struggle to access medical care. | Infant and maternal mortality are on the rise as | mothers and children bear the brunt of an increasingly | brutal conflict. | MSF teams are providing urgent medical care to people | caught up in this crisis. We work in 12 hospitals and health | centres and support more than 20 hospitals and health | facilities across the country. | We provide medical support in emergency rooms, | maternity wards and outpatient departments, conduct | surgeries, and run mobile clinics in rural towns and | villages. The situation remains desperate. We need | your help now more than ever | THANK YOU | could pay for three safe delivery kits | to assist women giving birth. | could provide an oxygen kit to help care for | premature babies with underdeveloped lungs. | could cover the cost of a midwife working | in a field hospital for three days. | could pay for a portable ultrasound device | to monitor the health of pregnant women | and their foetuses. | DONATE NOW | CALL 0800 055 79 84 | Or search ‘MSF NURSE’ | to make an online donation | YES, | would like to support MSF's medical teams in Yemen | | WOULD LIKE TO MAKE A DONATION OF £ | | Please make your cheque/charity voucher payable to Médecins Sans Frontiéres UK Cl | OR Es Please charge my VISA/Mastercard/Amex/CAF card: | cv ee CETTE EEE | Cardnumber|_| | | | | | | | | | | Tt I | | oe” LET / EI | ARE YOU A UK TAXPAYER? | If so, you can make your gift worth 25% | | | more at no extra cost. Please tick the box below. | | wish Médecins Sans Frontiéres (MSF) to treat all gifts in the last 4 years, this gift | and all future gifts as Gift Aid donations. | am a UK taxpayer and understand that if | | pay less Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax than the amount of Gift Aid claimed | on all my donations in that tax year it is my responsibility to pay any difference. | date TO/O0/OO | NB: Please let us know if your name, address or tax status changes, or if you would like to cancel | giftaid | Signature Today’s date Zs | a re | | / | | | this declaration, so that we can update our records. | Title | | Forename(s) | | _ RESPECTING YOU AND YOUR PERSONAL DATA | Your support is vital to our work and we would like to keep you informed with first-hand accounts | Surname from our staff and patients about the lifesaving impact your support is having, from combatting | epidemics to providing emergency surgery. | Address | | We won't allow other organisations to have access to your personal data for marketing purposes | and we won't bombard you with appeals. | | | Postcode : F ‘ : : gh wo | By supporting MSF, you will receive our quarterly magazine Dispatches, event invitations,and ~ | Telephone | occasional emergency appeals with requests for donations by post. You can change how you 3 | hear from MSF UK by emailing uk.fundraising@london.msf.org or calling 020 7404 6600. J | Email | Visit our privacy notice for more: msf.org.uk/privacy. | HEAR FROM MSF BY EMAIL. Sign up to our monthly email, Frontline, which Please fill in this form, place in an envelope and return postage | provides first-hand accounts of Sin work. You will receive Frontline, occasional [] : _ _ free to: FREEPOST RUBA-GYZY-YXST, Médecins Sans Frontiéres, | emergency appeals, requests for donations and event invitations. Opt mein to email Bumpers Way, Bumpers Farm, Chippenham SN14 6NG. Altematively — | Charity Registration Number 1026588 you can phone 0800 055 79 84. MSFRO0048",Newspaper,NA,NA
157,""" MAKE A WI LL ¥ 7 | TSAVE THE WORLD. | wr oe ; | Ce aaald ee | Pere | What will happen to our world in the future? One thing is certain, Greenpeace will need to be there. | You can count on us to defend the natural world and do the right thing for our living planet. We will | protect our oceans, forests and climate, for people and all the earth’s natural wonders. | Greenpeace is vital to saving our planet and a gift in your Will makes sure we're there. 1 in 6 of our | campaigns only happen because of donations left to us in people's Wills. | So, stand up for what you believe in. | Make a Will. Save the world. Thank you. | Search greenpeace.org.uk/save",Newspaper,NA,NA
158,"£9L000-Wv) | Luz O8S | dOSHYOM | AWed BstidsazuZ poom uoQueYW We have 7 days to make a huge | auzUs) UOLIN}OS eed P41 stand with families like Rose’s, and | Ply ueL}sStuyy millions more people across Kenya | XSNN-3VAV-GHLY SN}dq 3SOdeeau4 whose lives are threatened daily | by storms, flash floods and drought. | Let's stand together against climate | chaos. Let's work with communities | to help build reliable water systems | ( III | Ill - and better futures. | G Every drop of water that falls is | precious. So there needs to be a | simple, sustainable and ingenious | way to successfully collect it. Earth | : é R dams do just that. And your gift | Help a community like Rose's build | aries eas eae Galle ete, | a life-saving dam. | Stand against . | _the elements. — | These huge basins are dug in the | ground to collect and store rain | water. It’s then piped into taps | for people like Rose to draw from. | You only have to look at Florence | and her community in the image | opposite to see the difference | an earth dam can make. | Crops grow, land can be farmed, | and families no longer go hungry. | People stand strong and are | Duy 2 35 3 better prepared for whatever | pair of taps Fle 350k¢g communal water point life throws at them. a eae | whi cement }% r © taps, situate very one sh 10uld ibe :. | —iopardedes ve. = | Ise © j1v ive a gift today, ‘a | LE hies | Will you stand against | climate chaos bebe | seople in Kenya: | : 2 he a , <> | Sag, ~~"" — ef : x | | Kitui County in Eastern Kenya was already in | the grip of a climate emergency. Severe drought | followed by extreme and unpredictable changes | in weather has destroyed crops, killed livestock, | and continues to threaten livelihoods. | Then the pandemic hit. | We know ourselves that water | and washing hands has been | vital in helping to save lives | during the pandemic. But what | if there isn’t any? What if the | nearest water source is miles | away, by foot? What then? This | climate crisis and th