re independently fu | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | with 99.4 per cent of our funding coming from individual or private donors, like you | Bentiu <r | s { The hospital is run by more than | 500 MSF staff, most of whom | Protection | -=-= | of Civilians | camp | Bentiu Protection of Civilians camp | in South Sudan is home to some | 100,000 people who have sought | refuge from conflict. Residents live in | temporary shelters made of corrugated | metal, plant stalks, mud and plastic | sheeting. These offer limited protection | from the elements, especially during | the rainy season when walkways | become lanes of mud. People’s medical | needs are enormous. | MSF operates the main hospital in the | UN-run camp, which provides general | medical care, surgery, treatment for malaria, | malnutrition and diseases linked to poor | water and along with | for tuberculosis and HIV. | _ Na! Ne | RA \ EES | “MSF is operating one of the only hospitals | 4 a t & | thar has surgical services — not only in Bentiu, | but in the wider region,” says MSF surgeon | John Buckles, who has recurned to Bentiu | five times to conduct surgeries. “Working | here, you're faced with a broad spectrum | of conditions, ranging from tropical diseases | to surgical conditions that occur in any | society where there are abdominal issues, | hrough to gency All of | these conditions have continued during | the COVID-19 pandemic.” | Each ward of the 120-bed b> $ | hospital is housed in a white Le | tent connected by covered q Weter tanks_ _ ‘kk | walkways. “I was working wy —* | 12 to 15 hours a day for seven — | Russtration Jere Padery | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | Sj LA | FS ge om | “From my point of view as a surgeon, | Bentiu and operated in a tent with a | small air-conditioning unit dripping onto | the floor. | was very happy with the | surgical outcomes this latest trip. We | had a lot of very sick patients, but we | didn't lose any of them.” | isn't replaceable. If MSF wasn't there, _ | pleased that you've been able to heip, | while also knowing that, if it wasn’t | thousands of people would be without | the medical care and lifesaving | surgery that they need. | “A little bit of money goes a long | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | AROUND THE WORLD | Around the | world with MSF | WE’RE PROUD OF HOW WE SPEND | YOUR CONTRIBUTIONS | (MSF UK Annual Report 2019) | 85% 13% | ON OUR HUMANITARIAN WORK ON FUNDRAISING (WE RAISE | £8.24 FOR EVERY £1 WE SPEND) | ON OFFICE MANAGEMENT | Photograph © Avra | Fiatas/MSF | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE | PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | ITALY | Over winter, hundreds of mi | and refugees travelled the ‘Balkan | route’ on foot to reach Italy. The | Helmand \ | Photograph © Andrew Quilty | 4 SOUTH SUDAN | MSF physiotherapist Birgit | Schénharting plays with | five-year-old Anyar and helps | him regain the use of his leg | after he was bitten by a snake | SESE ESE while playing outside his house. | Photograph © Damaris Giulana/MSF | MOZAMBIQUE | Photograph © MSF | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE | PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE",Newspaper,NA,NA
183,"URGENT ACTION IRAN | HELP FREE MY HUSBAND | FROM AN IRANIAN PRISON | My name is Sherry Izadi and for the last four My husband is a retired engineer, a dual British- | years, my 67-year-old husband Anoosheh lranian national and a proud father of two. Like | Ashoori has been arbitrarily detained in Iran’s Nazanin Zaghari-Ratcliffe, he has been falsely | notorious Evin prison. accused of spying by the Iranian authorities. | In August 2017, Anoosheh was arrested Anoosheh has recently battled against the | during a regular visit to see his elderly mother. Coronavirus inside an unhygienic and overcrowded | He was later tortured into making a confession prison cell with very little medical help or protection. | to crimes he never committed. After an unfair ; | 4 | am desperate to get him back to our family | trial, he was sentenced to jail terms of two | home in London. | and 10 years. Text FREE2 to 70505 with | WHAT You CAN DO your FIRST and LAST name | to sign our urgent petition* | Please join Amnesty International UK’s urgent petition calling on the UK Government to | take action now to help release Anoosheh, including giving him full diplomatic protection. _www.amnesty.org.uk/anoosheh | *Texts to 70505 are charged at standard network rate, no more than 10p. Please ask bill-payer’s permission. You must be | 14 years or older. Your details will be held securely by Amnesty International United Kingdom Section — a limited company | registered in England (01735872), and Amnesty International UK Section Charitable Trust — a charity registered in England | & Wales (1051681) and in Scotland (SC039534). AMNESTY | Amnesty UK will add your name to this petition and we will also call you about supporting our work with a financial gift and/or INTERNATIONAL | by joining our campaigns. Text NOCALL to 70505 if you prefer not to be called or email us at sct@amnesty.org.uk. Amnesty | UK take your privacy seriously. For full terms and conditions see amnesty.org.uk/smsterms & www.amnesty.org.uk/privacy.",Newspaper,NA,NA
184,"YEMEN EMERGENCY APPEAL | anaesthetist Lenka Cervenova and her team care for a | in Dhi As-Sufal hospital in Yemen's Ibb province. Photograph | a fot | year-old boy after surgery | Majd Aljunaid/MSF | ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | MSF NURSE | The conflict in Yemen is now in its sixth year. In Marib, in central | Yemen, nearly three million people have been displaced from their | homes and are living in makeshift camps close to the frontline. | Pregnant women and children are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, | we found the local hospitals | overwhelmed by the sheer number | of people and by an influx of | war-wounded. Around 2.7 million | people are in the province now, | many of them displaced from | their homes elsewhere and | looking for a safe haven. | People were living in makeshift | shelters made of plastic and metal | sheets. There were no latrines, | just pits dug in the ground | outside the tents. People were | very worried about their health, | about the risk of disease, about | not having enough food. We were | very concerned about the health | of mothers and children, and | pregnant women were a major | worry too. | Over the next month, our small | team worked around the clock | to set up clinics providing basic | healthcare and to supply hospitals | that were struggling. Getting | medicines to this part of Yemen | wasnt easy. Supplies often had to | cross frontlines. One of our trucks | carrying antibiotics, medicines | and other supplies was hijacked. | It was an incredibly difficult | environment to work in. | “As the frontline approaches | Marib city, people sheltering in | the area may find themselves | with no place else to go.” | Along with general healthcare, | we provided routine vaccinations | for children and offered antenatal | and postnatal care. We saw a | lot of pregnant women and | children and were able to | refer pregnant women with | complications to larger hospitals | for emergency caesareans. | It made a real difference. | The day we started working in a | rural hospital near the frontline, | a woman came in with a breech | DONATE NOW | Call: 0800 055 79 81 | seven days a week | Or search | ‘MSF ALI’ | to make an online donation | delivery who was having real | difficulty. Our midwife assisted | and everything went well. But who | knows what would have happened | to that woman if the hospital | wasn't there. | A DESPERATE SITUATION | Since I left, many more people | have come to the area. But Marib | is no longer a safe place to be. In | recent weeks, the frontlines have | moved into the province, causing | large numbers of injuries and | forcing thousands more people to | flee their homes. As the frontline | approaches Marib city, we are | concerned that people sheltering | in the area may find themselves | with no place else to go. | After six years of conflict, the | situation is desperate. These | people need our help now more | than ever.” | Charity Registration Number 1026588 | MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES | DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | WHAT IS HAPPENING | IN YEMEN? | Yemen is in the midst of civil war. After six years of conflict, | the medical and humanitarian situation in Yemen continues | to deteriorate. Poverty is widespread, malnutrition is on the | rise and people are confronted by the daily threat of violence. | Pregnant women struggle to access medical care. | Infant and maternal mortality are on the rise as mothers | and children bear the brunt of an increasingly brutal conflict. | MSF teams are providing urgent medical care to people | caught up in this crisis. We work in 12 hospitals and health | centres and support more than 20 hospitals and health | facilities across the country. | We provide medical support in emergency rooms, maternity | wards and outpatient departments, conduct surgeries, and | run mobile clinics in rural towns and villages. The situation | remains desperate. We need your help now more than ever. | Thank you. | WE CAN'T DOIT | WITHOUT YOU | could pay for three safe delivery | kits to assist women giving birth. | £130 | could provide an oxygen kit to | help care for premature babies | with underdeveloped lungs. | @ £228 | Y. > could cover the cost of a | : } midwife working in a field | hospital for three days. | £866 | could pay for a portable | ultrasound device to monitor | the health of pregnant women | and their foetuses.",Newspaper,NA,NA
185,"ceria Here’s why the gorillas urgently need your help the | organization gorilla | MMT All Kahuzi Biega, Congo: organization | Rangers were tracking a family of Grauer’s gorillas when they | observed a baby male with a crude wire snare cutting into his wrist. | Another gorilla had broken the snare free from its pole, but it was | creating an open, bleeding | wound. Rangers and vets | sedated both baby and | mother, then carefully | removed the snare, and | cleaned and stitched up | the wound. Soon baby was | cuddling his mother again. | Without urgent treatment, | he'd certainly have lost his | hand, and probably his life. | Freepost Plus RTKS-ZCXS-HSBT | The Gorilla Organization | 126 Fairlie Road | SLOUGH | SL1 4PY | Bahoze, Congo: | When baby mountain gorilla Bahoze was trapped t lw a sware, her | mother Magania tried to remove it. But this only tightened the | sware around Bahoze’s | wrist. Silverback Lulengo | was also clearly visibly | distressed. He started to | charge the vets. Rangers | managed to calm | him while Bahoze was | successfully treated. One | more life caea thanks | to the bravery and sel-f- | sacrifice of our rangers. | Tlease a hort + let this be | , | IL | ouv las: F goodbye | What's a govilla?” | A personal appeal from Bill Oddie | Crrecycle | the. | gorilla — | organization | Dear Friend, | When my grandchildren were little, they | used to ask me questions no grown-up ever | would. Like ‘what's a gorilla?’ | | told them that gorillas are amazing | creatures — our closest living relatives. What | | didn’t say was that gorillas will soon | only exist in zoos or movies — unless we “You and | have the power | protect them right now. olanapdinne etal | Bill Oddie, Patron | Gorillas are teetering on the brink of | extinction. They're under more pressure than ever before. Just | thinking about that brings tears to my eyes. | That's why I'm asking for your help today. Will you give just £20 to | save our last precious gorillas before it’s too late? | Because if we don’t do more to protect gorillas right now, we’re | going to have to explain to the next generation how we let them go. | It's over 50 years since Dian Fossey started her amazing work to save | the gorillas —- perhaps you remember her story from the film ‘Gorillas | in the Mist’. Dian dedicated her life to saving gorillas. When she was | savagely murdered by poachers, the Gorilla Organization carried on | her mission. Their unique approach really works. That's why I’m so | proud to help them. | | won't rest until we have saved | the gorillas from extinction. Will | you stand side by side with me? | Anything you give will help: | This tiny baby gorilla is as fragile | as one of our own babies | * provide ranger patrols to protect | vulnerable gorilla families from | poachers — often at terrible risk | to their own lives | * our rangers form a ‘human | shield’ to keep gorillas safe | from the current epidemic | * remove the increasing number | of snares being found in the gorillas’ habitat and | rescue any baby gorillas from traps before its too late. | Mountain gorillas are incredibly rare. There are just over a thousand | left in the wild — ranging across Rwanda, Uganda and the Democratic | Republic of the Congo (DRC). | Tragically, the Grauer’s gorilla has seen a 78 per cent drop from | 17,000 to just 3,800 individuals in just a single generation. | Gorillas urgently need your help today. | Rangers are prepared to give up their own lives to save the gorillas | At least 200 rangers working alongside the Gorilla Organization in the | DRC have given their lives to save their gorilla ‘brothers and sisters’. | We have recently received reports that six more brave men have | been killed by armed militias in the DRC while protecting gorillas. | Please don't let their bravery have been in vain. | Many more rangers are deep in the forest right now, far from their | homes and loved ones. They never question whether the risks are | worth it. They know the gorillas need their help. They're ready to risk | everything to keep a baby gorilla out of the poachers’ hands, or to | rescue a youngster from a cruel snare. | But they cannot do it alone. The gorillas need your help today too | If everyone who loves animals works together, we can save the | gorillas we all care about so much. So please make a gift today — | and make sure we never have to explain why we let the gorillas go. | Yours sincerely | Bill Oddie | Patron and conservationist | The Gorilla Organization | PS. Extinction is for ever. So please make a gift of £20 to save the | gorillas before it’s too late. | The Gorilla Organization, 110 Gk A . London NW1 BHX | Registered Charity No. 1117131. | E-mail: info@gorillas.org Website: www.gorillas.org | germission of Joe McKenna. | Will you please give £20 to save our last gorillas? | We can still save the gorillas, but only if we act now. 99.9% of all gorillas are still | critically endangered, so there’s no time to waste. | Please fill in the form below or if you prefer you can give by phone 0300 330 0141 | or securely online at www.gorillas.org/donate | Here is my gift to support The Gorilla Organization's work: | | (please circle) | £15 £20 £30 | Or my preferred gift of: | £ | How to make your gift | [ | enclose a cheque/PO payable to: The Gorilla Organization | OR please debit my Mastercard/Visa/Maestro/CAF Card (delete as appropriate) | Account Number Maestro ONLY | oT 1 T y T T T | Card Expires ; | Your details ils Title! First name tin full) | Surname | Address. | Post code | Make every gift worth 25% more | YES | am a UK taxpayer* | INO sorry | do not pay tax DATE _| / | )] | *| would like The Gorilla Organization to treat this and all future donations A fii dit | | make from the date of this declaration as Gift Aid donations. | am a UK F Lal | and understand that if | pay less Income Tax and/or Capital | Gains Tax than the amount of Gift Aid claimed on all my donations in that tax year, | it is my responsibility to pay any difference. | | will notify The Gorilla Organization if | wish to cancel this declaration, change my | name or home address or | no longer pay sufficient Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax. | Can we stay in touch? | Your support makes a huge difference to our work, and we would like to stay in touch | with you to let you know how you are helping and how you could help save gorillas from | extinction. We promise we will never sell or share your details to any other organisation and | will always keep them secure. We will only process your data if we have legal grounds to do | so. This will mainly be by your consent to process your data, or under the rules of legitimate | interest. For full information please read our Privacy Policy at www.gorillas.org/privacy. If you | wish to update these preferences at any time, please email us at info@gorillas.org. Please tick | below if you DO NOT wish to hear from The Gorilla Organization by post. | Please fold and stick this form and return to: Freepost Plus RTKS-ZCXS-HSBT, | The Gorilla Organization, 126 Fairlie Road, SLOUGH SL1 4PY Thank you | Gon0Ve3",Newspaper,NA,NA
186,"Dad’ | dementia | killing | me | | Feelings of Confusion, desperation and | isolation don’t just affect people with | dementia, they're symptoms the people | closest to them experience too. | Right now, the broken social care system | means thatin the UK, nearly 1 million | people with dementia and their families | are struggling to get the support and | care that they need. Until things change, | a dementia diagnosis will continue to | claim more than one life. Make a stand | this Dementia Action Week, 17-23 May. | Because while dementia isn’t curable | yet, the care system is. | Sign the petition to reform social care. | Search: Cure the Care System | Alzheimers Dementia Action Week | 2 > Supported by «> Santander",Newspaper,NA,NA
187,"URGENT ACTION IRAN | HELP FREE MY HUSBAND | FROM AN IRANIAN PRISON | My name is Sherry Izadi and for the last four My husband is a retired engineer, a dual British- | years, my 67-year-old husband Anoosheh lranian national and a proud father of two. Like | Ashoori has been arbitrarily detained in Iran's Nazanin Zaghari-Ratcliffe, he has been falsely | notorious Evin prison. accused of spying by the Iranian authorities. | In August 2017, Anoosheh was arrested Anoosheh has recently battled against the | during a regular visit to see his elderly mother. Coronavirus inside an unhygienic and overcrowded | He was later tortured into making a confession prison cell with very little medical help or protection. | to crimes he never committed. After an unfair . ; | | am desperate to get him back to our family | trial, he was sentenced to jail terms of two | home in London. | ee Text FREE1 to 70505 with | to sign our urgent petition” | Please join Amnesty International UK’s urgent petition calling on the UK Government to | take action now to help release Anoosheh, including giving him full diplomatic protection. www.amnesty.org.uk/anoosheh | *Texts to 70505 are charged at standard network rate, no more than 10p. Please ask bill-payer’s permission. You must be | 14 years or older. Your details will be held securely by Amnesty International United Kingdom Section — a limited company | registered in England (01735872), and Amnesty International UK Section Charitable Trust — a charity registered in England | & Wales (1051681) and in Scotland (SC039534). AMNESTY | Amnesty UK will add your name to this petition and we will also call you about supporting our work with a financial gift and/or INTERNATIONAL | by joining our campaigns. Text NOCALL to 70505 if you prefer not to be called or email us at sct@amnesty.org.uk. Amnesty | UK take your privacy seriously. For full terms and conditions see amnesty.org.uk/smsterms & www.amnesty.org.uk/privacy.",Newspaper,NA,NA
