200,"ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | IS AN MSF NURSE WHO | WORKED IN MARIB | The conflict in Yemen is now in its sixth year. In Marib | in central Yemen, nearly three million people have been | displaced from their homes and are living in makeshift | camps close to the frontline. Pregnant women and | children are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, we found the local hospitals | overwhelmed by the sheer number of people and by an | influx of war-wounded. Around 2.7 million people are | in the province now, many of them displaced from their | homes elsewhere and looking for a safe haven. | People were living in makeshift shelters made of plastic | and metal sheets. There were no latrines, just pits dug in | the ground outside the tents. People were very worried | about their health, about the risk of disease, about not | having enough food. We were very concerned about the | health of mothers and children, and pregnant women | were a major worry too. | Over the next month, our small team worked around | the clock to set up clinics providing basic healthcare | and to supply hospitals that were struggling. Getting | medicines to this part of Yemen wasn't easy. Supplies | often had to cross frontlines. One of our trucks carrying | antibiotics, medicines and other supplies was hijacked. | It was an incredibly difficult environment to work in. | Along with general healthcare, we provided routine | vaccinations for children and offered antenatal and | postnatal care. We saw a lot of pregnant women and | children and were able to refer pregnant women | with complications to larger hospitals for emergency | caesareans. It made a real difference. | A DESPERATE SITUATION | The day we started working in a rural hospital near the | frontline, a woman came in with a breech delivery who | was having real difficulty. Our midwife assisted and | everything went well. But who knows what would have | happened to that woman if the hospital wasn't there. | Since I left, many more people have come to the area. | But Marib is no longer a safe place to be. In recent | weeks, the frontlines have moved into the province, | causing large numbers of injuries and forcing thousands | more people to flee their homes. As the frontline | approaches Marib city, we are concerned that people | sheltering in the area may find themselves with no | place else to go. | After six years of conflict, the situation is desperate. | These people need our help now more than ever.” | WHAT IS HAPPENING IN YEMEN? | Yemen is in the midst of civil war. After six years of conflict, | the medical and humanitarian situation in Yemen continues to | deteriorate. Poverty is widespread, malnutrition is on the rise | and people are confronted by the daily threat of violence. | Pregnant women struggle to access medical care. | Infant and maternal mortality are on the rise as | mothers and children bear the brunt of an increasingly | brutal conflict. | MSF teams are providing urgent medical care to people | caught up in this crisis. We work in 12 hospitals and health | -centres and support more than 20 hospitals and health | facilities across the country. | We provide medical support in emergency rooms, | maternity wards and outpatient departments, conduct | surgeries, and run mobile clinics in rural towns and | villages. The situation remains desperate. We need | your help now more than ever. | THANK YOU | could pay for three safe delivery kits | to assist women giving birth. | could provide an oxygen kit to help care for | premature babies with underdeveloped lungs. | could cover the cost of a midwife working | £228 in a field hospital for three days. | £866 could pay for a portable ultrasound device | to monitor the health of pregnant women | and their foetuses. | CALL 0800 055 79 84 | Or search ‘MSF NURSE’ | to make an online donation | YES, | would like to support MSF's medical teams in Yemen | | WOULD LIKE TO MAKE A DONATION OF £ | | Please make your cheque/charity voucher payable to Médecins Sans Frontiéres UK | OR C] Please charge my VISA/Mastercard/Amex/CAF card: | Cardholdername| [| | [| [ | | tt ttt tt te ttt i tt tt | Cardnumber{_ [| [| [| [ | | | | tt tt i tt | Expiry | date” LT] /LLI | Signature | Today’s date | | Er /Ce | ARE YOU A UK TAXPAYER? | If so, you can make your gift worth 25% | more at no extra cost. Please tick the box below. | | wish Médecins Sans Frontiéres (MSF) to treat all gifts in the last 4 years, this gift | and all future gifts as Gift Aid donations. | am a UK taxpayer and understand that if | | pay less Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax than the amount of Gift Aid claimed | on all my donations in that tax year it is my responsibility to pay any difference. | pate QO/O0/OO | NB: Please let us know if your name, address or tax status changes, or if you would like to cancel | this deciaration, so that we can update our records. | gift nid wv | Title | Forename(s) | | Surname | RESPECTING YOU AND YOUR PERSONAL DATA | Your support is vital to our work and we would like to keep you informed with first-hand accounts | from our staff and patients about the lifesaving impact your support is having, from combatting | Address | | epidemics to providing emergency surgery. | We won't allow other organisations to have access to your personal data for marketing purposes | | Postcode | | Telephone | | Email | | HEAR FROM MSF BY EMAIL. Sign up to our monthly email, Frontline, which | provides first-hand accounts of our work. You will receive Frontline, occasional | emergency appeals, requests for donations and event invitations. | Charity Registration Number 1026588 | [ | Opt me in to email | By supporting MSF, you will receive our quarterly magazine Dispatches, event invitations,and | occasional emergency appeals with requests for donations by post. You can change how you | hear from MSF UK by emailing uk.fundraising@london.msf.org or calling 020 7404 6600. | ] and we won't bombard you with appeals. | Visit our privacy notice for more: msf.org.uk/privacy. | 100534 | Please fill in this form, place in an envelope and return postage | free to: FREEPOST RUBA-GYZY-YXST, Médecins Sans Frontiéres, | Bumpers Way, Bumpers Farm, Chippenham SN14 6NG. Alternatively | you can phone 0800 055 79 84. | MSFRO048",Newspaper,NA,NA
201,"MOTHER FACES | 38 YEARS | 148 LASHES. | j Sef | tite | . 4 : | AMNESTY | | INTERNATIONAL | LOCKED UP FOR DEFENDING WOMEN’S | RIGHT TO CHOOSE HOW THEY DRESS. | NOW NASRIN NEEDS YOUR HELP. | Dear Friend, left without his mum and dad. | “I am sending you my tears of love, hoping they make the injustice more tolerable.” | These are Nasrin Sotoudeh’s words from prison in Iran, to her two children. She has been wrenched | apart from them, just for standing up for what’s right. Now she faces a brutal sentence of 148 lashes | and 38 years in prison in Tehran. | Nasrin is a human rights lawyer in Iran. It’s an incredibly risky job, but she has dedicated her life to | defending the rights of others. Whether that means fighting to end the death penalty, or battling laws | that punish women and girls unless they cover themselves in public. | Her commitment to justice has set Iran’s authorities against her. They have thrown her in prison twice: | once in 2010 and again in 2019. She remains in prison today. Nasrin is being held in Shahr-e Rey | prison, a notorious women’s prison, where torture and mistreatment are rife. | Will you support Amnesty International UK Section Charitable Trust today with a monthly donation | of £5? Your gift could help us campaign for Nasrin’s freedom and help reunite her with her family. | Right now, Amnesty International is working to protect human rights across the globe. We are | committed to shining a light on injustice, and standing up for people who stand up for others — just | like Nasrin. | In Iran, women and girls above a certain age are required by law not to leave their homes unless they | cover their hair, arms and legs. Women who don’t, face prison, flogging or a fine. | In 2018, a movement against the forced veiling law erupted when one woman staged a solo act of | resistance. She removed her headscarf and silently waved it on the end of a stick. Countless women | across Iran joined her, and became known as the ‘Girls of Revolution Street’. | Nasrin defended some of these women in court, but was then herself arrested. By going after her, the | lranian authorities are attacking women’s rights. All women should be free to choose what they wear, | and lawyers should be free to defend their right to do so. | A gift of £5 a month could help us publicise the facts of Nasrin’s case, to increase the pressure on | the Iranian authorities. | After two grossly unjust trials, Nasrin now faces 148 lashes and 38 years in prison — the harshest | sentence against a human rights defender in Iran for years. Charges against her include “inciting | corruption and prostitution”. | It’s part of a huge crackdown on free speech and dissent in Iran. More and more people like Nasrin | are locked up, tortured, intimidated and more, just for speaking up for human rights. The authorities | carry out floggings and have executed hundreds of people. | Amnesty International United Kingdom Section Charitable Trust, The Human Rights Action Centre, 17-25 New Inn Yard, London EC2A 3EA | Telephone +44 (0)20 7033 1500 _ Facsimile +44 (0)20 7033 1503 www.amnesty.org.uk | A charity registered in England and Wales (1051681) and Scotland (SCO039534). Registered office 17-25 New Inn Yard London EC2A 3EA Please turn over | Last year, Nasrin had to cope with having the coronavirus too, on top of other health problems. | In January, she was diagnosed with a heart condition which makes her liable to angina and chest | pain. It is made worse by stress or physical exertion. Her cardiologist said she should avoid being | stressed and be held in well-ventilated spaces. We are now very worried for Nasrin’s health inside | a dangerous, unhygienic and overcrowded prison. | Nasrin is among the hundreds of wrongly imprisoned people in Iran. No one should spend a single | day in prison for peacefully exercising their rights. | Your gift today of £5 a month could help Amnesty International lobby governments around the world | to intervene in cases like Nasrin’s. | We know this works. With the help of people like you, Amnesty International has secured the release | of lawyers, journalists and photographers the world over. Your gifts mean we can investigate abuse, | hold people to account, and push decision-makers to take action in defence of human rights. | The injustice of Nasrin’s case has already touched the hearts of hundreds of thousands of people. | Now we need to do even more — so the authorities in Iran feel compelled to act. | Together we can show Iran that humanity always wins. | Thank you | Rosie Leatherland | Campaigns Manager - Individuals at Risk | PS Your donation today could be critical if Nasrin is to walk free. Your support could help us pressure | the Iranian government, so Nasrin can be reunited with her family. | TOGETHER WE CAN FREE NASRIN | PAING PHYO MIN, MYANMAR NARGES MOHAMMADI, | Jailed after using poetry to criticise the IRAN | country’s military. Jailed for her work to protect | = ; human rights. | Kazemi/Getty Images | xy Kaveh | NASRIN SOTOUDEH,|RAN | Locked up for defending women’s right to choose how they dress. | IN PRISON | ies fora TM Moy bet Nin | How do | tell you where | am when you are | e so innocent and too young to comprehend | the meaning of words such as prison, arrest, | sentence, trial and injustice? | You asked me, “Mummy, are you coming | home with us today?” and | was forced to | Cc respond in plain view of the security agents: | “My work is going to take a while so I'll | come home later.” | It was then that you nodded as if to say you | | understand and took my hand, giving it a | ' sweet, childlike kiss with your small lips. | | once told you: “My daughter | hope you | never think that | was not thinking of you | or that it was my actions that deserved such | punishment... Everything | have done is | legal and within the framework of the law.” | It was then that you lovingly caressed my | face with your small hands and replied: | “I know, Mummy, | know.” | Here’s how you can help | £2 amonth could help Amnesty International campaign to set Nasrin and other wrongly | imprisoned people free so they can be reunited with their families once again. | £5 a month could help fund Amnesty International’s research and investigations to tell the | world how people like Nasrin are suffering at the hands of oppressive governments. | £10 a month could help us to lobby Iran and other governments around the world that seek | to silence people like Nasrin for defending human rights. | To make your donation, fill in the form and return it in the Freepost envelope, | or visit www.amnesty.org.uk/freenasrin | TOGETHER WE CAN FREE NASRIN | YES, | WILL HELP FREE NASRIN | : ad to set Nasrin and | could help faranesty ldernatianal carnpoig | | 7) MY DONATION ro bbe frst en be na se rslen eee eg | Here's my direct debit of: £2 (_] £10{_] Other £[____]amonth 2 | fp INSTRUCTION TO YOUR BANK/BUILDING SOCIETY TO PAY BY DIRECT DEBIT | Name and full postal address of your Bank or Building Society branch | To The Manager of Bank/Building Society | Address | Postcode | Name(s) of account holder(s) | Bank/Building Society Account Number Branch Sort Code Service User No. | 8/5/4/4/8)] 5 ASTRA | Official use only | Instructions to your Bank or Building Society. Please pay Amnesty International Direct Debits from the account detailed in this instruction subject to the | safeguards assured by the Direct Debit Guarantee. | understand that this instruction may remain with Amnesty International and, if so, details will be passed | electronically to my Bank/Building Society. Banks and Building Societies may not accept Direct Debits for some types of accounts. | Signature(s) Date / / | Alternatively, visit www.amnesty.org.uk/freenasrin to make your monthly donation online. | 2 YOUR DETAILS: (We can’t process your donation without this information) | Title First Name Last Name | Address | Postcode | Are you aged 18 or over? YES []_ NO (J | If you are under 18, please tell us your date of birth so that | 0 | | D = | we can send you information that is relevant to you. No | | WE TAKE YOUR PRIVACY SERIOUSLY The information that you provide will be held by Amnesty International UK Section and Amnesty | International UK Section Charitable Trust (AIUK). We need this to process your donations. | We will also write to you to keep you updated about AIUK’s campaigns, fundraising appeals, events and other ways you can help. If you | do not want us to keep you updated by post please tick here. (_] | If you would also like to hear about this work by email and/or telephone, please provide your preferred email address and/or telephone | number below. | M Mi! ¥ ¥ Y ng | Email | Telephone | You can opt out of communications at any time by clicking on the unsubscribe button in our emails, or emailing sct@amnesty.org.uk or | writing to us at Amnesty International UK, Human Rights Action Centre, 17-25 New Inn Yard, London EC2A 3EA. For more information | about how AIUK processes personal data, please visit: www.amnesty.org.uk/privacy | 3 GIFT AID DECLARATION | If you are a UK taxpayer we can claim back the tax on your donations — that’s an extra 25p on every £1 you give. : aid it | gift | [-] | would like Amnesty International (UK Section) Charitable Trust to treat all donations that | have made in | the past 4 years and all donations that | make from the date of this declaration as Gift Aid donations. | NB. You must pay an amount of UK Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax in each tax year (6th April to 5th April) at least equal to | the tax that all the charities or Community Amateur Sports Clubs that you donate to will reclaim on your donations for that tax year | (25p on every £1 you give). | Please be aware that you will be responsible for any tax shortfall if you have paid insufficient tax to cover the Gift Aid claimed. | We are committed to the highest standards in fundraising. Read our promise to you: www.amnesty.org.uk/promise | Amnesty International works globally to protect human rights and stand up for the rights of individuals. We focus on the issues | and people most in need at any time, so your donation may be used to fund other areas of our work. | Please return this completed form in the FREEPOST envelope provided. | Amnesty International United Kingdom Section Charitable Trust, The Human Rights Action Centre, 17-25 New Inn Yard, London EC2A 3EA | Telephone +44 (0)20 7033 1500 Facsimile +44 (0)20 7033 1503 www.amnesty.org.uk | Registered with | A charity registered in England and Wales (1051681) and Scotland (SC039534). FUNDRAISING | Registered office 17-25 New Inn Yard London EC2A 3EA | REGULATOR | Affixing | postage stamp | here will | help reduce | our costs. II | Z | AMNESTY | INTERNATIONAL =<{F- | Freepost RSHR-JYYA-UGXC | Nasrin Sotoudeh RG Appeal MAI3104T | Amnesty International UK | PO Box 36 | Sheffield | S98 1DB | Me eel ell",Newspaper,NA,NA
