209,"Ethiopia-Tigray Emergency Appeal | GIVE SHELTER | £159 could provide a shelter kit — giving safety to families | fleeing the dreadful conflict. | ‘Violence in Ethiopia’s Tigray region has seen more than two million people forced to flee their homes since the conflict started in | November 2020. Over 63,000 have fled into neighbouring Sudan, while those displaced inside Ethiopia include around 100,000 | refugees from Eritrea. | The region remains a tinderbox. There have been reports | of hundreds of people killed, of atrocities, including forced | military recruitment of men and boys, and of widespread | sexual violence against women. | Displaced families urgently need shelter. People have been | sleeping in the open air, with no access to water or food. | UNHCR teams on the ground are doing all they can to protect | people, but with funds running desperately low, they urgently | need your support. | £159 could pay for a shelter kit. As the diagram shows, each | kit contains bamboo poles, a bamboo floor mat, roof beams, | and other local building materials with which a family shelter | can be built. With the rainy season fast approaching, the shelter | you help provide could be lifesaving. And it will do more than | protect families from the elements; it will allow people to socially | distance to protect themselves from COVID-19, and give women | and girls a safe haven. | Without shelter, they are intensely vulnerable. UN Special | Representative of the Secretary-General on Sexual Violence | WOODEN GABLE FRAME SHELTER | approx. | 1.7m | — Am | a a <3 | *Pproy Pe ern : Ven - — op | ¢ The kit you can help provide builds a shelter like | this, using locally available materials. | e Each kit contains a roof beam, wall beams, bamboo | poles and sticks, rope, a bamboo mat for flooring | and straw mesh to line the walls. | Example shelter kit from Sudan. Content and structure vary depending on | location and materials available. | in Conflict, Pramila Patten, has said: “In the remote, mountainous regions of North and Central Tigray, women and girls are being | subjected to sexual violence with a level of cruelty beyond comprehension.” | RIGHT NOW, SHELTER WILL SAVE PEOPLE’S LIVES. | Families have been forced to flee the terrifying violence and sought safety in other parts of Ethiopia’s Tigray region or in Sudan. | And every day a family spends without shelter, is a day when they are more at risk of contracting COVID-19, and a day when | women and girls remain more vulnerable to sexual violence. Please give £159, or whatever you can afford, today — there is nota | moment to lose. | £159 COULD PROVIDE A SHELTER KIT | cive now at UNrefugees.org.uk/tigraycrisis | Call 0800 029 3883. or fill in and return the form below | | _|ess9 | |e318 | | Others | |] | enclose a cheque made payable to UK for UNHCR OR | | would like to give: | Z £79 | (_}1 would like to make a donation by debit/credit card | Catan! ra Expiry date__ | SE De SRG ee oe Sie | Se | [Signature SS See eae = Dae Date | ge RS aE A I va) hexane a eel | _] Tick here, if you don’t want to receive an acknowledgement by post | Title Firstname Last name | Address) ee, capes | a (ai Sd TY Postcode _ G | Email igs | Phone | Please tell us if you are happy to hear about UNHCR’s work: C] By email C]py phone | Registered with | FUNDRAISING | REGULATOR | Your gift will help families displaced by the Ethiopia Emergency, | funding shelter and other interventions, depending on greatest need. | United Kingdom for UNHCR Is a registered charity in England and Wales (1183415). | Please return this form along with your cheque to: | Freepost UK FOR UNHCR (you don’t need a stamp) | Increase your donation by 25p for every £1 you donate | giftasd we | [Ji would like UK for UNHCR to claim Gift Aid on this donation (currently 25p | for each £1 donated), as well as any donations | make in the future and have made in the past four | years to UK for UNHCR, until | notify you otherwise. | confirm | am a UK taxpayer and | understand | that if | pay less Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax than the amount of Gift Aid claimed on all my | donations in that tax year it is my responsibility to pay any difference. | understand that council tax | and VAT do not qualify. | If your circumstances change or you have any questions, please contact our Supporter Care Team | on 0800 029 3883 or email supportercare@unrefugees.org.uk | Respecting your personal data: We will use your details to process your | donation and to keep you up to date with our work, fundraising activities and | other events. You can read more about how we use your data in our Privacy Policy | unrefugees.org.uk/privacy-policy You can opt out of any communications at any | time by contacting our Supporter Care Team using the details above. | - (UNHCR | Scan this The UN Refugee Agency | bal 4 | QR code to United Kingdom for UNHCR | donate online | P21ETOBA2",Newspaper,NA,NA
210,"YEMEN EMERGENCY APPEAL Gers: | MSF anaesthetist Lenka | in Dhi As-Sufai hospital in Yemen's Ibb | uel) : | | ervenova ant | *hotograph | are for a four-year-old boy after surgery | Majd Aljunaid/MSI | ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | MSF NURSE | The conflict in Yemen is now in its sixth year. In Marib, in central | Yemen, nearly three million people have been displaced from their | homes and are living in makeshift camps close to the frontline. | Pregnant women and children are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, | we found the local hospitals | overwhelmed by the sheer number | of people and by an influx of | war-wounded. Around 2.7 million | people are in the province now, | many of them displaced from | their homes elsewhere and | looking for a safe haven. | People were living in makeshift | shelters made of plastic and metal | sheets. There were no latrines, | just pits dug in the ground | outside the tents. People were | very worried about their health, | about the risk of disease, about | not having enough food. We were | very concerned about the health | of mothers and children, and | pregnant women were a major | worry too. | Over the next month, our small | team worked around the clock | to set up clinics providing basic | healthcare and to supply hospitals | that were struggling. Getting | medicines to this part of Yemen | wasn‘ easy. Supplies often had to | cross frontlines. One of our trucks | carrying antibiotics, medicines | and other supplies was hijacked. | It was an incredibly difficult | environment to work in. | “As the frontline approaches | Marib city, people sheltering in | the area may find themselves | with no place else to go.” | Along with general healthcare, | we provided routine vaccinations | for children and offered antenatal | and postnatal care. We saw a | lot of pregnant women and | children and were able to | refer pregnant women with | complications to larger hospitals | for emergency caesareans. | It made a real difference. | The day we started working in a | rural hospital near the frontline, | a woman came in with a breech | DONATE NOW | Call: 0800 055 79 81 | seven days a week | Or search ‘MSF ALI’ | to make an online donation | delivery who was having real | difficulty. Our midwife assisted | and everything went well. But who | knows what would have happened | to that woman if the hospital | wasn't there. | A DESPERATE SITUATION | Since I left, many more people | have come to the area. But Marib | is no longer a safe place to be. In | recent weeks, the frontlines have | moved into the province, causing | large numbers of injuries and | forcing thousands more people to | flee their homes. As the frontline | approaches Marib city, we are | concerned that people sheltering | in the area may find themselves | with no place else to go. | After six years of conflict, the | situation is desperate. These | people need our help now more | than ever.” | Charity Registration Number 1026588 | WHAT IS HAPPENING | IN YEMEN? | Yemen is in the midst of civil war. After six years of conflict, | the medical and humanitarian situation in Yemen continues | to deteriorate. Poverty is widespread, malnutrition is on the | rise and people are confronted by the daily threat of violence. | Pregnant women struggle to access medical care. | Infant and maternal mortality are on the rise as mothers | and children bear the brunt of an increasingly brutal conflict. | MSF teams are providing urgent medical care to people | caught up in this crisis. We work in 12 hospitals and health | centres and support more than 20 hospitals and health | facilities across the country. | We provide medical support in emergency rooms, maternity | wards and outpatient departments, conduct surgeries, and | run mobile clinics in rural towns and villages. The situation | remains desperate. We need your help now more than ever. | Thank you. | WE CAN’T DO IT | WITHOUT YOU | could pay for three safe delivery | kits to assist women giving birth. | £130 | could provide an oxygen kit to | help care for premature babies | with sink aicsi lungs. | = £298 | | o> could cover the cost of a | wae midwife working in a field | hospital for three ee | £866 | could pay for a portable | ultrasound device to monitor | > the health of pregnant women | and their foetuses. | 100547",Newspaper,NA,NA
211,"MAKE A WILL. | What will happen to our world in the future? One thing is certain, Greenpeace will Raa | need to be there. You can count on us to defend the natural world anddotheright = == | thing for our living planet. We will protect our oceans, forests and climate, for | people and all the earth’s natural wonders. | Greenpeace is vital to saving our planet and a gift in your Will makes sure we're | there. 1 in 6 of our campaigns only happen because of donations left to.us in | people’s Wills. | So, stand up for what you believe in. | Make a Will. Save the world. Thank you. | Search greenpeace.org.uk/save | Pty mauriber CADIS Greenpeace, regttersd Comparty Fai",Newspaper,NA,NA
212,"YEMEN EMERGENCY APPEAL | ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | MSF NURSE | The conflict in Yemen is now in its sixth year. In Marib, in central | Yemen, nearly three million people have been displaced from their | homes and are living in makeshift camps close to the frontline. | Pregnant women and children are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, | we found the local hospitals | overwhelmed by the sheer number | of people and by an influx of | war-wounded. Around 2.7 million | people are in the province now, | many of them displaced from | their homes elsewhere and | looking for a safe haven. | People were living in makeshift | shelters made of plastic and metal | sheets. There were no latrines, | just pits dug in the ground | outside the tents. People were | very worried about their health, | about the risk of disease, about | not having enough food. We were | very concerned about the health | of mothers and children, and | pregnant women were a major | worry too. | Over the next month, our small | team worked around the clock | to set up clinics providing basic | healthcare and to supply hospitals | that were struggling. Getting | medicines to this part of Yemen | wasnt easy. Supplies often had to | cross frontlines. One of our trucks | carrying antibiotics, medicines | and other supplies was hijacked. | It was an incredibly difficult | environment to work in. | “As the frontline approaches | Marib city, people sheltering in | the area may find themselves | with no place else to go.” | Along with general healthcare, | we provided routine vaccinations | for children and offered antenatal | and postnatal care. We saw a | lot of pregnant women and | children and were able to | refer pregnant women with | complications to larger hospitals | for emergency caesareans. | It made a real difference. | The day we started working in a | rural hospital near the frontline, | a woman came in with a breech | DONATE NOW | Call: 0800 055 79 81 | seven days a week | Or search ‘MSF ALI’ | to make an online donation | delivery who was having real | difficulty. Our midwife assisted | and everything went well. But who | knows what would have happened | to that woman if the hospital | wasnt there. | A DESPERATE SITUATION | Since I left, many more people | have come to the area. But Marib | is no longer a safe place to be. In | recent weeks, the frontlines have | moved into the province, causing | large numbers of injuries and | forcing thousands more people to | flee their homes. As the frontline | approaches Marib city, we are | concerned that people sheltering | in the area may find themselves | with no place else to go. | After six years of conflict, the | situation is desperate. These | people need our help now more | than ever.” | scan to donate | Charity Registration Number 1026588 | MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES | DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | WHAT IS HAPPENING | IN YEMEN? | Yemen is in the midst of civil war. After six years of conflict, | the medical and humanitarian situation in Yemen continues | to deteriorate. Poverty is widespread, malnutrition is on the | rise and people are confronted by the daily threat of violence. | Prégnant women struggle to access medical care. | infant and maternal mortality are on the rise as mothers | and children bear the brunt of an increasingly brutal conflict. | MSF teams are providing urgent medical care to people | caught up in this crisis. We work in 12 hospitals and health | centres and support more than 20 hospitals and health | facilities across the country. | We provide medical support in emergency rooms, maternity | wards and outpatient departments, conduct surgeries, and | run mobile clinics in rural towns and villages. The situation | remains desperate. We need your help now more than ever. | Thank you. | WE CAN’T DOIT | WITHOUT YOU | could pay for three safe delivery | kits to assist women giving birth. | £130 | could provide an oxygen kit to | help care for premature babies | with underdeveloped lungs. | @ £228 | could cover the cost of a | midwife working in a field | en"" hospital for three days. | Thank you | £866 | could pay for a portable | ultrasound device to monitor | the health of pregnant women | and their foetuses. | 100602",Newspaper,NA,NA
213,"Ethiopia-Tigray Emergency Appeal | GIVE SHELTER | £159 could provide a shelter kit — giving safety to families | fleeing the dreadful conflict. | Violence in Ethiopia’s Tigray region has seen more than two million people forced to flee their homes since the conflict started in | November 2020. Over 63,000 have fled into neighbouring Sudan, while those displaced inside Ethiopia include around 100,000 | refugees from Eritrea. | The region remains a tinderbox. There have been reports WOODEN GABLE FRAME SHELTER | of hundreds of people killed, of atrocities, including forced | military recruitment of men and boys, and of widespread | sexual violence against women. | Displaced families urgently need shelter. People have been | sleeping in the open air, with no access to water or food. | UNHCR teams on the ground are doing all they can to protect | people, but with funds running desperately low, they urgently | need your support. | £159 could pay for a shelter kit. As the diagram shows, each Ps iy a | kit contains bamboo poles, a bamboo floor mat, roof beams, PC Pan | and other local building materials with which a family shelter wiWhe kit-yolkean-heip provide boillds.a‘shelter like | can be built. With the rainy season fast approaching, the shelter this, using locally available materials. | you help provide could be lifesaving. And it will do more than os Reet ees eoce Bhan. wall.beans,.baxboo | protect families from the elements; it will allow people to socially poles and sticks, rope, a bamboo mat for flooring | distance to protect themselves from COVID-19, and give women and-straw mesh to line the walls. | and girls a safe haven. : | Example shelter kit from Sudan. Content and structure vary depending on | Without shelter, they are intensely vulnerable. UN Special location and materials available. | Representative of the Secretary-General on Sexual Violence | in Conflict, Pramila Patten, has said: “In the remote, mountainous regions of North and Central Tigray, women and girls are being | subjected to sexual violence with a level of cruelty beyond comprehension.” | RIGHT NOW, SHELTER WILL SAVE PEOPLE’S LIVES. | Families have been forced to flee the terrifying violence and sought safety in other parts of Ethiopia’s Tigray region or in Sudan. | And every day a family spends without shelter, is a day when they are more at risk of contracting COVID-19, and a day when | women and girls remain more vulnerable to sexual violence. Please give £159, or whatever you can afford, today — there is nota | moment to lose. | £159 COULD PROVIDE A SHELTER KIT | ive now at UNrefugees.org.uk/tigraycrisis | Call 0800 029 3883. or fill in and return the form below | | would like to give: _ | £79 |_| £159 EJ £318 LJ Other £ = Se a Se oO ean Gece your cheque to: | (_]1 enclose a cheque made payable to UK for UNHCR OR reepo st aunt need a stampy | (_] | would like to make a donation by debit/credit card Increase your donation by 25p for every £1 you donate ftaid it | Card no. Expiry date (Ji would like UK for UNHCR to claim Gift Aid on this donation (currently 25p F | | a Se Sr ieee | TT} Sqn"" coca oe ts Tae for each £1 donated), as well as any donations | make in the future and have made in the past four | | [ | | | sl [ af | 3 | i [ [3 / = years to UK for UNHCR, until | notify you otherwise. | confirm | am a UK taxpayer and | understand | : UTS et : aie = - that if! pay less Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax than the amount of Gift Aid claimed on all my | | Signature Date donations in that tax year it is my responsibility to pay any difference. | understand that council tax | | / / and VAT do not qualify. | | eT. = ba a If your circumstances change or you have any questions, please contact our Supperter Care Team | (_] Tick here, if you don’t want to receive an acknowledgement by post on 0800 029 3883 or email supportercare@unrefugees.org.uk | Title __ First name _______Last name “_—. Respecting your personal data: We will use your details to process your | Address _ 2 ae donation and to keep you up to date with our work, fundraising activities and | other events. You can read more about how we use your data in our Privacy Policy | —— unrefugees.org.uk/privacy-policy You can opt out of any communications at any | time by contacting our Supporter Care Team using the details above. | mm  QUNHCR | OR codeto =—-~> The UN Refugee Agency | donate online | Postcode | Email. aa | Phone. | Please tell us if you are happy to hear about UNHCR's work: CIBy email CIpy phone | Registered with Your gift will help families displaced by the Ethiopia Emergency, | FUNDRAISING funding shelter and other interventions, depending on greatest need. | REGULATO | United Kingdom for UNHCR is a registered charity in England and Wales (1183415). P21ETGUA2",Newspaper,NA,NA
214,"YEMEN EMERGENCY APPEAL 4 Sevexczzz | MSF anaesthetist Lenke | in Dhi As-Sufal hospital in | ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | IS AN MSF NURSE WHO | WORKED IN MARIB | The conflict in Yemen is now in its sixth year. In Marib | in central Yemen, nearly three million people have been | displaced from their homes and are living in makeshift | camps close to the frontline. Pregnant women and | children are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, we found the local hospitals | overwhelmed by the sheer number of people and by an | influx of war-wounded. Around 2.7 million people are | in the province now, many of them displaced from their | homes elsewhere and looking for a safe haven. | People were living in makeshift shelters made of plastic | and metal sheets. There were no latrines, just pits dug in | the ground outside the tents. People were very worried | about their health, about the risk of disease, about not | having enough food. We were very concerned about the | health of mothers and children, and pregnant women | were a major worry too. | Over the next month, our small team worked around | the clock to set up clinics providing basic healthcare | and to supply hospitals that were struggling. Getting | medicines to this part of Yemen wasn't easy. Supplies | often had to cross frontlines. One of our trucks carrying | antibiotics, medicines and other supplies was hijacked. | It was an incredibly difficult environment to work in. | Along with general healthcare, we provided routine | vaccinations for children and offered antenatal and | postnatal care. We saw a lot of pregnant women and | children and were able to refer pregnant women | with complications to larger hospitals for emergency | caesareans. It made a real difference. | A DESPERATE SITUATION | The day we started working in a rural hospital near the | frontline, a woman came in with a breech delivery who | was having real difficulty. Our midwife assisted and | everything went well. But who knows what would have | happened to that woman if the hospital wasn’t there. | Since I left, many more people have come to the area. | But Marib is no longer a safe place to be. In recent | weeks, the frontlines have moved into the province, | causing large numbers of injuries and forcing thousands | more people to flee their homes. As the frontline | approaches Marib city, we are concerned that people | sheltering in the area may find themselves with no | place else to go. | After six years of conflict, the situation is desperate. | These people need our help now more than ever.” | WHAT IS HAPPENING IN YEMEN? | Yemen is in the midst of civil war. After six years of conflict, | the medical and humanitarian situation in Yemen continues to | deteriorate. Poverty is widespread, malnutrition is on the rise | and people are confronted by the daily threat of violence. | Pregnant women struggle to access medical care. | infant and maternal mortality are on the rise as | mothers and children bear the brunt of an increasingly | brutal conflict. | MSF teams are providing urgent medical care to people | caught up in this crisis. We work in 12 hospitals and health | centres and support more than 20 hospitals and health | facilities across the country. | We provide medical support in emergency rooms, | maternity wards and outpatient departments, conduct | surgeries, and run mobile clinics in rural towns and | villages. The situation remains desperate. We need | your help now more than ever. | THANK YOU | could pay for three safe delivery kits | to assist women giving birth. | £130 | could provide an oxygen kit to help care for | premature babies with underdeveloped lungs. | could cover the cost of a midwife working | in a field hospital for three days. | £228 | £866 | could pay for a portable ultrasound device | to monitor the health of pregnant women | and their foetuses. | CALL 0800 055 79 84 | Or search ‘MSF NURSE’ | to make an online donation | YES, | would like to support MSF's medical teams in Yemen | | WOULD LIKE TO MAKE A DONATION OF £ | | Please make your cheque/charity voucher payable to Médecins Sans Frontieres UK TC] | OR C] Please charge my VISA/Mastercard/Amex/CAF card: | Cardholder name | | | | | | | | | a | | Cardnumber|_{ [| | | | | | | | | tT tt | Lg ier LR | axe” LL1/CO) | ARE YOU A UK TAXPAYER? | If so, you can make your gift worth 25% | | | more at no extra cost. Please tick the box below. | | wish Médecins Sans Frontiéres (MSF) to treat all gifts in the last 4 years, this gift | and all future gifts as Gift Aid donations. | am a UK taxpayer and understand that if | | pay less Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax than the amount of Gift Aid claimed | on all my donations in that tax year it is my responsibility to pay any difference. | pate NO/OO/OO0 | NB: Please let us know if your name, address or tax status changes, or if you would like to cancel | this declaration, so that we can update our records. | giftasd uw | RESPECTING YOU AND YOUR PERSONAL DATA | Your support is vital to our work and we would like to keep you informed with first-hand accounts | from our staff and patients about the lifesaving impact your support is having, from combatting | Signature Today’s date | | 4 ij. | | / | L | | Title} Forename(s) | | Surname | | Address | | | | Postcode | | Telephone | | Email fre | | epidemics to providing emetgency surgery. | We won't allow other organisations to have access to your personal data for marketing purposes | and we won't bombard you with appeals. | By supporting MSF, you will receive our quarterly magazine Dispatches, event invitations,and | occasional emergency appeals with requests for donations by post. You can change how you | hear from MSF UK by emailing uk.fundraising@london.msf.org or calling 020 7404 6600. | Visit our privacy notice for more: msf.org.uk/privacy. | 100593 | HEAR FROM MSF BY EMAIL. Sign up to our monthly email, Frontline, which | provides first-hand accounts of our work. You will receive Frontline, occasional | emergency appeals, requests for donations and event invitations. | Charity Registration Number 1026588 | [| Opt me in to email | Please fill in this form, place in an envelope and return postage | free to: FREEPOST RUBA-GYZY-YXST, Médecins Sans Frontiéres, | Bumpers Way, Bumpers Farm, Chippenham SN14 6NG. Alternatively | you can phone 0800 055 79 84.",Newspaper,NA,NA
215,"‘Saving our planet | begins with you opening this envelope. | * Registered with Friends of the Earth Limited; Company number 1012357. | Friends of FUNDRAISING Registered in England and Wales. | the Earth REGULATOR Our registered office is: Ist Floor, The Printworks, 139 Clapham Road, SW9 OHP. | To view our Privacy Policy please visit: | friendsoftheearth.uk/about-us/privacy-policy. | | Our paper is totally recycled and our printers hold 1S014001 | recyc e which means they care about the environment. May 2021. | eee Becomepartof | something bigg | a ‘ > | If we stand together today, we can make Friends of | the government act tosavethisplanet. -  ~ the Earth | Dear friend. | Thank you for opening this letter. The fact you’ve done so proves we have something in common - we | both want to save our planet. Today, I’m hoping you'll join me and thousands of others to do just that. | The undeniable impact of climate change can be seen all around us - extreme flooding, heatwaves and | storms. It’s getting worse all the time. Climate change has become a climate crisis. | And while people like you and | watch in frustration and horror, the government is failing to do what's | needed to protect our planet and those of us who call it home. | We're running out of time to prevent the worst impacts of this climate crisis. | But it can be done. And it will be done by people like us. | By working together, we can be a movement with the passion, expertise and sheer numbers to show | the government they have no choice but to act. | Will you pl r part, and help protect n r planet oming a Frien f the Earth | member for £5 a month? | The government has made many positive statements | about leading the world in tackling the climate crisis. But | behind closed doors they’re still making harmful decisions. | Recently they have given the green light to more oil and | gas wells in the North Sea. They’ve also cancelled the | Green Homes Grant - an important solution to help people | improve energy efficiency in their homes, like better | insulation or double glazing. We’re members | That’s why we must each take action right now, to demand “I donate to Friends of the | they change the path they're leading us down. If we do that, Earth because they hold | we'll each be able to look back in 5 or 10 years and say with the government to | pride that we were on the right side of history. account over the | It’s easy to feel powerless as an individual, but nothing could climate crisis. | be further from the truth. Jan, Friends of the | Earth Member | By becoming a Friends of the Earth member today you | Please turn over... | can join a growing network of people around the globe, | determined to stand up and make those who have the | power to protect people and planet, act. | What makes us different is that we’re not simply | demanding change, we’re actively working to make | change happen and holding the government to account. | We're campaigners, lawyers, and climate experts. We’re | members, volunteers and local activists too. And the work | we do is making a real difference. | Recent successes include helping to get a planned | opencast coal mine in Northumberland stopped, and | launching a legal challenge against the government’s | investment in a huge gas plant in Mozambique. | Our members give us the strength and funding to | challenge the government on cases like these on the | streets, in courtrooms and in the corridors of power. | If you become a member too, you'll make our movement | stronger until we’re simply impossible to ignore. | You could look back in 5 or 10 years and say you were on | the right side of history, you took action for the planet. | Will you become a member today? | Thank you. | “ORNS Femaudso | Denis Fernando | Friends of the Earth Campaigner | PS Stand with us today and you can look back | 5 or 10 years and say you took action for the | planet, you were on the right side of history. | Please become a member for £5 a month now, | or anything you can afford. io fy | Use the form enclosed, visit “ | foe.uk/climate-member-21, | or scan the QR code. [a] — | Friends of the Earth Limited; Company number 1012357. Registered in England and Wales. | Our registered office is: lst Floor, The Printworks, 139 Clapham Road, SW9 OHP. | To view our Privacy Policy please visit: friendsoftheearth.uk/about-us/privacy-policy. | May 2021. | Our paper is totally recycled and our printers hold 1ISO14001 | which means they care about the environment. | C¥recycle | We’re campaigners | “I support community groups | across the UK to secure | ambitious Climate | Action Plans | from their | local leaders.” | Catriona Currie, | Friends of the Earth | Campaigner | We’re lawyers | “I’m fighting for climate justice | on high-profile cases like | Heathrow airport expansion, | and supporting | communities | in challenging | fossil fuel | developments | in the UK.” | Katie de Kauwe, Friends | of the Earth Lawyer | We’re experts | “We find the evidence | and solutions to show the | government what can be | done to stop the | impacts of the | climate crisis.” | Mike Childs, Friends | of the Earth Head of | Science, Policy and | Research | Here’s what we can achieve | when we stand together. : | Protecting | endangered | species | We helped draft | the Endangered Species | Act 1976 to protect 5,800 | species of animals across | the globe. | 1971 | Glass | bottle | recycling | action | In our founding year we | returned thousands of | used glass bottles outside | the Schweppes HQ to | promote reuse. | SERCO R REECE HEE E EEE E EEE ERE EE ETE EEE EEE EEE EEE SEE EHHHEESHEHESESEEEESEHEEEEHESE EEE EEE TEES | {2008 | | Climate | Change Act | -aclimate | world first | Along with nearly 200,000 | supporters we persuaded | the government to adopt | the first climate change law | inthe world - the Climate | a a elped 6) the | Welsh | | sOQVvernm nt | unch the w Te) td's | on shee sa tea | Change Act. de puto sinth vere | | 2020 Why j