216,"YEMEN | EMERGENCY APPEAL | | ry MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES | DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | Ch SIA EE ek SRO icah ieao | Bag od | ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | IS AN MSF NURSE WHO | WORKED IN MARIB | The conflict in Yemen is now in its sixth year. In Marib | in central Yemen, nearly three million people have been | displaced from their homes and are living in makeshift | camps close to the frontline. Pregnant women and | children are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, we found the local hospitals | overwhelmed by the sheer number of people and by an | influx of war-wounded. Around 2.7 million people are | in the province now, many of them displaced from their | homes elsewhere and looking for a safe haven. | People were living in makeshift shelters made of plastic | and metal sheets. There were no latrines, just pits dug in | the ground outside the tents. People were very worried | about their health, about the risk of disease, about not | having enough food. We were very concerned about the | health of mothers and children, and pregnant women | were a major worry too. | Over the next month, our small team worked around | the clock to set up clinics providing basic healthcare | and to supply hospitals that were struggling. Getting | medicines to this part of Yemen wasn't easy. Supplies | often had to cross frontlines. One of our trucks carrying | antibiotics, medicines and other supplies was hijacked. | It was an incredibly difficult environment to work in. | Along with general healthcare, we provided routine | vaccinations for children and offered antenatal and | postnatal care. We saw a lot of pregnant women and | children and were able to refer pregnant women | with complications to larger hospitals for emergency | caesareans. It made a real difference. | A DESPERATE SITUATION | The day we started working in a rural hospital near the | frontline, a woman came in with a breech delivery who | was having real difficulty. Our midwife assisted and | everything went well. But who knows what would have | happened to that woman if the hospital wasn't there. | Since I left, many more people have come to the area. | But Marib is no longer a safe place to be. In recent | weeks, the frontlines have moved into the province, | causing large numbers of injuries and forcing thousands | more people to flee their homes. As the frontline | approaches Marib city, we are concerned that people | sheltering in the area may find themselves with no | place else to go. | After six years of conflict, the situation is desperate. | These people need our help now more than ever” | WHAT IS HAPPENING IN YEMEN? | Yemen is in the midst of civil war. After six years of conflict, | the medical and humanitarian situation in Yemen continues to | deteriorate. Poverty is widespread, malnutrition is on the rise | and people are confronted by the daily threat of violence. | Pregnant women struggle to access medical care. | Infant and maternal mortality are on the rise as | mothers and children bear the brunt of an increasingly | brutal conflict. | MSF teams are providing urgent medical care to people | caught up in this crisis. We work in 12 hospitals and health | centres and support more than 20 hospitals and health | facilities across the country. | We provide medical support in emergency rooms, | maternity wards and outpatient departments, conduct | surgeries, and run mobile clinics in rural towns and | villages. The situation remains desperate. We need | your help now more than ever. | THANK YOU | could pay for three safe delivery kits | to assist women giving birth. | could provide an oxygen kit to help care for | premature babies with underdeveloped lungs. | could cover the cost of a midwife working | in a field hospital for three days. | could pay for a portable ultrasound device | to monitor the health of pregnant women | and their foetuses. | DONATE NOW [elzsro | CALL 0800 055 79 84 | ux - | Or search ‘MSF NURSE’ | to make an online donation | YES, | would like to support MSF's medical teams in Yemen | | WOULD LIKE TO MAKE A DONATION OF £ | | Please make your cheque/charity voucher payable to Médecins Sans Frontiéres UK C] | OR C] Please charge my VISA/Mastercard/Amex/CAF card: | Cardholder name | | LI | | | | | | | I | | | eV RRRe SS | cardnumber(_T T TT TTT TTT ce LtI/CLLI | Today's date | | 1/| | 1/| | | ARE YOU A UK TAXPAYER? | If so, you can make your gift worth 25% | | | more at no extra cost. Please tick the box below. | | wish Médecins Sans Frontiéres (MSF) to treat all gifts in the last 4 years, this gift | and all future gifts as Gift Aid donations. | am a UK taxpayer and understand that if | | pay less Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax than the amount of Gift Aid claimed | on all my donations in that tax year it is my responsibility to pay any difference. | pate 0O/OO/O0 | NB: Please let us know if your name, address or tax status changes, or if you would like to cancel | this declaration, so that we can update our records. | giftaid | Signature | Title { | Forename(s) | | _ RESPECTING YOU AND YOUR PERSONAL DATA | Your support is vital to our work and we wouid like to keep you informed with first-hand accounts | Surname from our staff and patients about the lifesaving impact your support is having, from combatting | epidemics to providing emergency surgery. | ‘Address | | We won't allow other organisations to have access to your personal data for marketing purposes | and we won't bombard you with appeals. | | | Postcode BI 2 : : . A aah Be roe) | supporting MSF, you will receive our quarterly magazine Dispatches, event invitations,and fo) | Telephone | occasional emergency appeals with requests for donations by post. You can change how you 8 | hear from MSF UK by emailing uk.fundraising@london.msf.org or calling 020 7404 6600. & | Email | Visit our privacy notice for more: msf.org.uk/privacy. | HEAR FROM MSF BY EMAIL. sign up to our monthly email, Frontline, which Pisane 1 in this form, place in. an envelope and retum postage | provides first-hand accounts of our work. You will receive Frontline, occasional 4 O : _ free to: FREEPOST RUBA-GYZY-YXST, Médecins Sans Frontiéres, | emergency appeals, requests for donations and event invitations. ptmeintoemail Bumpers Way, Bumpers Farm, Chippenham SN14 6NG. Alternatively po | Charity Registration Number 1026588 | you can phone 0800 055 79 84.",Newspaper,NA,NA
217,"Ethiopia-Tigray Emergency Appeal | GIVE SHELTER | £159 could provide a shelter kit — giving safety to | families fleeing the dreadful conflict. | Violence in Ethiopia’s Tigray region has | seen more than two million people forced to | flee their homes since the conflict started in | November 2020. Over 63,000 have fled into | neighbouring Sudan, while those displaced | inside Ethiopia include around 100,000 | refugees from Eritrea. | The region remains a tinderbox. There | have been reports of hundreds of people | killed, of atrocities, including forced military | recruitment of men and boys, and of | widespread sexual violence against women. | Displaced families urgently need shelter. | People have been sleeping in the open air, | with no access to water or food. UNHCR | teams on the ground are doing all they | can to protect people, but they urgently | need your support. | £159 could pay for a shelter kit. As the | diagram shows, each kit contains local | WOODEN GABLE FRAME SHELTER | es 4 | appr, Ox a ag | ¢ The kit you can help provide builds | a shelter like this, using locally | available materials. | ¢ Each kit contains a roof beam, wall | beams, bamboo poles and sticks, rope, | a bamboo mat for flooring and straw | mesh to line the walls. | Example shelter kit from Sudan. Content and | structure vary depending on location and | materials available. | to sexual violence with a level of cruelty | building materials with which a family shelter beyond comprehension.” | can be built. The shelter you help provide | could be lifesaving; it will allow people to | socially distance to protect themselves | from COVID-19, and give women and girls | a safe haven. | Without shelter, they are intensely vulnerable. | UN Special Representative of the Secretary- | General on Sexual Violence in Conflict, | Pramila Patten, has said: “In the remote, | mountainous regions of North and Central | Tigray, women and girls are being subjected | * RIGHT NOW, SHELTER WILL SAVE | PEOPLE’S LIVES. | Every day a family spends without shelter, | is a day when they are more at risk of | contracting COVID-19, and a day when | women and girls remain more vulnerable | to sexual violence. | Please give £159, or whatever you | can afford, today — there is nota | moment to lose. | £4159 couLp PROVIDE A SHELTER KIT | Give now at | unrefugees.org.uk/tigraycrisis | Call 0800 029 3883, or fill in and return the form below | I would like to give: | | £79 | | £159| | £318| | Otherge | [_] | enclose a cheque made payable to UK for UNHCR OR | (_] | would like to make a donation by debit/credit card | Expiry date | ee ee | | Date . | | (_] Tick here, if you don't want to receive an acknowledgement by post | Card no. | oS Se SS eae | Signature | Title First name Last name | Address | Postcode | Email “SG = ¢ es | Phone | Please tell us if you are happy to hear about UNHCR’s work: CJ By email C] By phone | Please return this form along with your cheque to: | Freepost UK FOR UNHCR (you don’t need a stamp) | Increase your donation by 25p for every £1 you donate | a} | would like UK for UNHCR to claim Gift Aid on this donation (currently 25p for each giftaid aw | £1 donated), as well as any donations | make in the future and have made in the past four | years to UK for UNHCR, until | notify you otherwise. | confirm | am a UK taxpayer and | understand that if] pay less? | Income Tax and/or Capital.Gains Tax than the amount of Gift Aid claimed on all my donations in that tax year itis | my responsibility to pay any difference. | understand that council tax and VAT do not qualify. | If your circumstances change or you have any questions, please contact our Supporter Care Team on | 0800 029 3883 or email supportercare@unrefugees.org.uk | Respecting your personal data: We will use your details to process your | donation and to keep you up to date with our work, fundraising activities and | other events. You can read more about how we use-your data in our Privacy Policy | unrefugees.org.uk/privacy-policy You can opt out of any communications af any | time by contacting our Supporter Care Team using the details above. | : #0) Scan this Registered with | Gee eee, @ inoue (UNHCR | 7 ee va donate online REGULATOR UNR rs | ter * Your gift will help families displaced by the Ethiopia vei Hath United nadene ows be say | : Emergency, funding shelter and other interventions, | » depending on greatest need. | United Kingdom for UNHCR is a registered charity in England and Wales (1183415). P21ETOBA1",Newspaper,NA,NA
218,"YEMEN EMERGENCY APPEAL | ry MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES | DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | MSF anaesthetist Lenka Cervefiova and her t | in Dhi As-Sufal hospital in Yemen's Ibb provin | ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | IS AN MSF NURSE WHO | — WORKED IN MARIB | The conflict in Yemen is now in its sixth year. In Marib | in central Yemen, nearly three million people have been | displaced from their homes and are living in makeshift | camps close to the frontline. Pregnant women and | children are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, we found the local hospitals | overwhelmed by the sheer number of people and by an | influx of war-wounded. Around 2.7 million people are | in the province now, many of them displaced from their | homes elsewhere and looking for a safe haven. | People were living in makeshift shelters made of plastic | and metal sheets. There were no latrines, just pits dug in | the ground outside the tents. People were very worried | about their health, about the risk of disease, about not | having enough food. We were very concerned about the | health of mothers and children, and pregnant women | were a major worry too. | Over the next month, our small team worked around | the clock to set up clinics providing basic healthcare | and to supply hospitals that were struggling. Getting | medicines to this part of Yemen wasn't easy. Supplies | often had to cross frontlines. One of our trucks carrying | antibiotics, medicines and other supplies was hijacked. | It was an incredibly difficult environment to work in, | Along with general healthcare, we provided routine | vaccinations for children and offered antenatal and | postnatal care. We saw a lot of pregnant women and | children and were able to refer pregnant women | with complications to larger hospitals for emergency | caesareans. It made a real difference. | A DESPERATE SITUATION | The day we started working in a rural hospital near the | frontline, a woman came in with a breech delivery who | was having real difficulty. Qur midwife assisted and | everything went well. But who knows what would have | happened to that woman if the hospital wasn't there. | Since I left, many more people have come to the area. | But Marib is no longer a safe place to be. In recent | weeks, the frontlines have moved into the province, | causing large numbers of injuries and forcing thousands | more people to flee their homes. As the frontline | approaches Marib city, we are concerned that people | sheltering in the area may find themselves with no | place else to go. | After six years of conflict, the situation is desperate. | These people need our help now more than ever.” | WHAT IS HAPPENING IN YEMEN? | Yemen is in the midst of civil war. After six years of conflict, | the medical and humanitarian situation in Yemen continues to | deteriorate. Poverty is widespread, mainutrition is on the rise | and people are confronted by the daily threat of violence. | Pregnant women struggle to access medical care. | Infant and maternal mortality are on the rise as | mothers and children bear the brunt of an increasingly | brutal conflict. | MSF teams are providing urgent medical care to people | caught up in this crisis. We work in 12 hospitals and health | centres and support more than 20 hospitals and health | facilities across the country. | We provide medical support in emergency rooms, | maternity wards and outpatient departments, conduct | surgeries, and run mobile clinics in rural towns and | villages. The situation remains desperate. We need | your help now more than ever. | THANK YOU | could pay for three safe delivery kits | to assist women giving birth. | could provide an oxygen kit to help care for | premature babies with underdeveloped lungs. | could cover the cost of a midwife working | in a field hospital for three days. | could pay for a portable ultrasound device | to monitor the health of pregnant women | and their foetuses. | DONATE NOW | CALL 0800 055 79 84 | Or search ‘MSF NURSE’ | to make an online donation | YES, | would like to support MSF's medical teams in Yemen | | WOULD LIKE TO MAKE A DONATION OF £ | | Please make your cheque/charity voucher payable to Médecins Sans Frontiéres UK ba | OR Ea Please charge my VISA/Mastercard/Amex/CAF card: | Cardholdername|_| | | | | | | |} | |} TT ETT Et tt tt | card number {TTT TTT TTT LL TE Ts oe ee | Signature | Today’s date|_ |_|} Aas oe | ARE YOU A UK TAXPAYER? | If so, you can make your gift worth 25% | 4 more at no extra cost. Please tick the box below. | | wish Médecins Sans Frontiéres (MSF) to treat all gifts in the last 4 years, this gift | and all future gifts as Gift Aid donations. | am a UK taxpayer and understand that if | | pay less Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax than the amount of Gift Aid claimed | on all my donations in that tax year it is my responsibility to pay any difference. | date NO/OO0/O0 | NB: Please let us know if your name, address or tax status changes, or if you would like to cancel | this declaration, so that we can update our records. | giftasd it | Title | Forename(s) | | Surname | RESPECTING YOU AND YOUR PERSONAL DATA | Your support is vital to our work and we would like to keep you informed with first-hand accounts | from our staff and patients about the lifesaving impact your support is having, from combatting | Address & | epidemics to providing emergency surgery. | We won't allow other organisations to have access to your personal data for marketing purposes | | Postcode | Telephone | | Email | | HEAR FROM MSF BY EMAIL. Sign up to our monthly email, Frontline, which | provides first-hand accounts of our work. You will receive Frontline, occasional | emergency appeals, requests for donations and event invitations. | Charity Registration Number 1026588 | iz Opt me in to email | By supporting MSF, you will receive our quarterly magazine Dispatches, event invitations,and | occasional emergency appeals with requests for donations by post. You can change how you | hear from MSF UK by emailing uk.fundraising@london.msf.org or calling 020 7404 6600. | | and we won't bombard you with appeals. | Visit our privacy notice for more: msf.org.uk/privacy. | 100596 | Please fill in this form, place in an envelope and return postage | free to: FREEPOST RUBA-GYZY-YXST, Médecins Sans Frontiéres, | Bumpers Way, Bumpers Farm, Chippenham SN14 6NG. Alternatively | you can phone 0800 055 79 84. | MSFRO0048",Newspaper,NA,NA
219,"eee rae | eee ee ate ment | a -ge ‘ Yay"" toy | Y 2 a = . | - - "" Pave as | ""fe me bby | a! ¥ | MOTHER FACES | 148 LASHES. | AMNESTY . | INTERNATIONAL | LOCKED UP FOR DEFENDING WOMEN’S | RIGHT TO CHOOSE HOW THEY DRESS. | NOW NASRIN NEEDS YOUR HELP. | Dear Friend, left without his mum and dad. | =  aenet | “I am sending you my tears of love, hoping they make the injustice more tolerable.” | These are Nasrin Sotoudeh’s words from prison in Iran, to her two children. She has been wrenched | apart from them, just for standing up for what’s right. Now she faces a brutal sentence of 148 lashes | and 38 years in prison in Tehran. | Nasrin is a human rights lawyer in Iran. It’s an incredibly risky job, but she has dedicated her life to | defending the rights of others. Whether that means fighting to end the death penalty, or battling laws | that punish women and girls unless they cover themselves in public. | Her commitment to justice has set Iran’s authorities against her. They have thrown her in prison twice: | once in 2010 and again in 2019. She remains in prison today. Nasrin is being held in Shahr-e Rey | prison, a notorious women’s prison, where torture and mistreatment are rife. | Will you support Amnesty International UK Section Charitable Trust today with a monthly donation | of £5? Your gift could help us campaign for Nasrin’s freedom and help reunite her with her family. | Right now, Amnesty International is working to protect human rights across the globe. We are | committed to shining a light on injustice, and standing up for people who stand up for others — just | like Nasrin. | In Iran, women and girls above a certain age are required by law not to leave their homes unless they | cover their hair, arms and legs. Women who don’t, face prison, flogging or a fine. | In 2018, a movement against the forced veiling law erupted when one woman staged a solo act of | resistance. She removed her headscarf and silently waved it on the end of a stick. Countless women | across Iran joined her, and became known as the ‘Girls of Revolution Street’. | Nasrin defended some of these women in court, but was then herself arrested. By going after her, the | lranian authorities are attacking women’s rights. All women should be free to choose what they wear, | and lawyers should be free to defend their right to do so. | A gift of £5 a month could help us publicise the facts of Nasrin’s case, to increase the pressure on | the Iranian authorities. | After two grossly unjust trials, Nasrin now faces 148 lashes and 38 years in prison — the harshest | sentence against a human rights defender in Iran for years. Charges against her include “inciting | corruption and prostitution”. | It’s part of a huge crackdown on free speech and dissent in Iran. More and more people like Nasrin | are locked up, tortured, intimidated and more, just for speaking up for human rights. The authorities | carry out floggings and have executed hundreds of people. | Amnesty International United Kingdom Section Charitable Trust, The Human Rights Action Centre, 17-25 New Inn Yard, London EC2A 3EA | Telephone +44 (0)20 7033 1500 _ Facsimile +44 (0)20 7033 1503 www.amnesty.org.uk | A charity registered in England and Wales (1051681) and Scotland (SCO39534). Registered office 17-25 New Inn Yard London EC2A 3EA Please turn over | Last year, Nasrin had to cope with having the coronavirus too, on top of other health problems. | In January, she was diagnosed with a heart condition which makes her liable to angina and chest | pain. It is made worse by stress or physical exertion. Her cardiologist said she should avoid being | stressed and be held in well-ventilated spaces. We are now very worried for Nasrin’s health inside | a dangerous, unhygienic and overcrowded prison. | Nasrin is among the hundreds of wrongly imprisoned people in Iran. No one should spend a single | day in prison for peacefully exercising their rights. | Your gift today of £5 a month could help Amnesty International lobby governments around the world | to intervene in cases like Nasrin’s. | We know this works. With the help of people like you, Amnesty International has secured the release | of lawyers, journalists and photographers the world over. Your gifts mean we can investigate abuse, | hold people to account, and push decision-makers to take action in defence of human rights. | The injustice of Nasrin’s case has already touched the hearts of hundreds of thousands of people. | Now we need to do even more — so the authorities in Iran feel compelled to act. | Together we can show Iran that humanity always wins. | Thank you | Rosie Leatherland | Campaigns Manager - Individuals at Risk | PS Your donation today could be critical if Nasrin is to walk free. Your support could help us pressure | the Iranian government, so Nasrin can be reunited with her family. | TOGETHER WE CAN FREE NASRIN | PAING PHYO MIN, MYANMAR NARGES MOHAMMADI, | Jailed after using poetry to criticise the IRAN | country’s military. Jailed for her work to protect | : ; human rights. | NASRIN SOTOUDEH,|RAN | Locked up for defending women’s right to choose how they dress. | IN PRISON | Photo by Kaveh Kazemi/Getty Images | hae pron a coe 2. Migr denoer sn, Ninn | How do | tell you where | am when you are | so innocent and too young to comprehend | the meaning of words such as prison, arrest, | sentence, trial and injustice? | You asked me, “Mummy, are you coming | home with us today?” and | was forced to | respond in plain view of the security agents: | “My work is going to take a while so I’II | come home later.” | It was then that you nodded as if to say you | understand and took my hand, giving it a | sweet, childlike kiss with your small lips. | | once told you: “My daughter | hope you | never think that | was not thinking of you | or that it was my actions that deserved such | punishment... Everything | have done is | legal and within the framework of the law.” | It was then that you lovingly caressed my | face with your small hands and replied: | “| know, Mummy, | know.” | Here’s how you can help | £2 amonth could help Amnesty International campaign to set Nasrin and other wrongly | imprisoned people free so they can be reunited with their families once again. | £5 a month could help fund Amnesty International’s research and investigations to tell the | world how people like Nasrin are suffering at the hands of oppressive governments. | £10 a month could help us to lobby Iran and other governments around the world that seek | to silence people like Nasrin for defending human rights. | To make your donation, fill in the form and return it in the Freepost envelope, | or visit www.amnesty.org.uk/freenasrin | TOGETHER WE CAN FREE NASRIN | YES, | WILL HELP FREE NASRIN | : ad to set Nasrin and | could help Paranesty ldernatianal carnpaig | | 1 MY DONATION “dre ke her fees thea bev har furs mae nga ; | Here’s my direct debit of: £2 [_] £10[_] Other£[__]amonth 2 | fp INSTRUCTION TO YOUR BANK/BUILDING SOCIETY TO PAY BY DIRECT DEBIT | Name and full postal address of your Bank or Building Society branch | To The Manager of Bank/Building Society | Address | Postcode | Name(s) of account holder(s) | Bank/Building Society Account Number Branch Sort Code Service User No. | 81/5|4/4/8) 5 ASTRA | Official use only | Instructions to your Bank or Building Society. Please pay Amnesty International Direct Debits from the account detailed in this instruction subject to the | safeguards assured by the Direct Debit Guarantee. | understand that this instruction may remain with Amnesty International and, if so, details will be passed | electronically to my Bank/Building Society. Banks and Building Societies may not accept Direct Debits for some types of accounts. | Signature(s) Date / / | Alternatively, visit www.amnesty.org.uk/freenasrin to make your monthly donation online. | 2 YOUR DETAILS: (We can’t process your donation without this information) | ‘Title First Name Last Name | Address | Postcode | Are you aged 18 or over? YES []_ NO (J | If you are under 18, please tell us your date of birth so that [| a , | D || D|/-|M MI-| Y || Y | we can send you information that is relevant to you. a | WE TAKE YOUR PRIVACY SERIOUSLY The information that you provide will be held by Amnesty International UK Section and Amnesty | International UK Section Charitable Trust (AIUK). We need this to process your donations. | We will also write to you to keep you updated about AIUK’s campaigns, fundraising appeals, events and other ways you can help. If you | do not want us to keep you updated by post please tick here. (_] | If you would also like to hear about this work by email and/or telephone, please provide your preferred email address and/or telephone | number below. | | Email | Telephone | You can opt out of communications at any time by clicking on the unsubscribe button in our emails, or emailing sct@amnesty.org.uk or | writing to us at Amnesty International UK, Human Rights Action Centre, 17-25 New Inn Yard, London EC2A 3EA. For more information | about how AIUK processes personal data, please visit: www.amnesty.org.uk/privacy | 3 GIFT AID DECLARATION | If you are a UK taxpayer we can claim back the tax on your donations — that’s an extra 25p on every £1 you give. . aid it | gift | [] | would like Amnesty International (UK Section) Charitable Trust to treat all donations that | have made in | the past 4 years and all donations that | make from the date of this declaration as Gift Aid donations. | NB. You must pay an amount of UK Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax in each tax year (6th April to 5th April) at least equal to | the tax that all the charities or Community Amateur Sports Clubs that you donate to will reclaim on your donations for that tax year | (25p on every £1 you give). | Please be aware that you will be responsible for any tax shortfall if you have paid insufficient tax to cover the Gift Aid claimed. | We are committed to the highest standards in fundraising. Read our promise to you: www.amnesty.org.uk/promise | Amnesty International works globally to protect human rights and stand up for the rights of individuals. We focus on the issues | and people most in need at any time, so your donation may be used to fund other areas of our work. | Please return this completed form in the FREEPOST envelope provided. | Amnesty International United Kingdom Section Charitable Trust, The Human Rights Action Centre, 17-25 New Inn Yard, London EC2A 3EA | Telephone +44 (0)20 7033 1500 Facsimile +44 (0)20 7033 1503 www.amnesty.org.uk | A charit tered in England and Wales (1051681) and Scotland (SC039534) pay rg seat ce | Charity registered in England an ales an cotian 5 FUNDRAISING | Registered office 17-25 New Inn Yard London EC2A 3EA | REGULATOR | Affixing | postage stamp | here will | help reduce | our costs. II | Z | AMNESTY | INTERNATIONAL = <{F- | Freepost RSHR-JYYA-UGXC | Nasrin Sotoudeh RG Appeal MAI3104T | Amnesty International UK | PO Box 36 | Sheffield | S98 1DB | Me eel ell",Newspaper,NA,NA
223,"For the working animals of the world | can feed an could prevent buys 14 padded can vaccinate five a | exhausted painful injuries and doughnut donkeys against | working animal sores by providing bandages allowing _ the potentially | for five days. humane equipment wounds to heal fatal disease, | to replace broken while animals tetanus. oe Ais * oa = ‘ te : | parts of aharness. continue working. Eu ae ff ; Fret | YOUR GIFT GAN TURN A MISERABLE EXISTENCE INTO A | BETTER LIFE FOR WORKING ANIMALS. THANK YOU. | For the working animals of the world | Freepost Plus RTSE-TTEZ-CUXK | SPANA | York House | Wetherby Road | Long Marston | YORK | YO26 7NH | \ / ‘ q | _.alintilshé | www.spana.org/malirubbish 0800 082 6217. | Weighed down and weary | Exhausted and starving, animals like Kadi work until they | can’t go on any more | Walking over broken glass and twisted metal, toiling in the heat | and stench with a backbreaking load of rubbish weighing her | down, this exhausted donkey couldn't take any more. Her legs | gave way and she collapsed in the mud. Life doesn't get any | tougher for a donkey than working on the rubbish dumps of | Bamako, Mali's capital city. Donkeys are collapsing and dying | from exhaustion and dehydration. They are also in real danger | of catching the potentially fatal disease, tetanus, through injuries | inflicted by rusting metal and broken glass. | SPANA, the charity for the working animals of the world, | is helping | We work in poor countries like Mali, Mauritania and Ethiopia, | providing free veterinary treatment and educating owners. When | Kadi collapsed a team from our local veterinary centre were | already at the dump immunising donkeys against tetanus. They | rushed to her aid and lifted her gently to her feet. Our vet gave | Kadi the two things she desperately needed — a drink of water and | some nutritious feed. He then cleaned a nasty wound to her front | fetlock and administered antibiotics and a tetanus shot. | Animals may have to do the job of trucks and tractors in many | countries, but that doesn't mean they have to be overloaded, | overworked and underfed. We work with owners to help them take | better care of the animals that support their livelihoods. Together, | we can build a better future for both animals and communities. | We are the only hope for hundreds of thousands of working | animals around the world. These weighed down and weary | animals need your support. Every gift counts, so please send | whatever you can today. | Remember to complete and return the Gift Aid declaration to | allow us to claim an extra 25 per cent on donations you make | to SPANA at no extra cost to you. Thank you. | FREE VETERINARY GARE | In 2018, SPANA provided over 364,000 free veterinary | treatments to working animals around the world. | Our vision is a world where every working animal lives a | life free from suffering and is treated with compassion. We | urgently need your help to continue our lifesaving work. | Registered charity no: 209015 | £15 will allow 14 | animals to work free | from pain. | SPANA’s mission is to | improve the welfare | of working animals in | the world’s poorest | communities. We do | this through treatment, | training and teaching. | We treat by providing | free veterinary care | when animals suffer | or emergencies strike. | We train by building | expertise among vets | and promoting humane | care by owners. We | teach children to develop | positive beliefs, respect | and compassion | towards animals. | 3Y3H 0104 GNV HOVL3G | 3Y35H NALSIOW | Card no: | Title: | Address: | First name: Surname: | Postcode: | Contact tel. no: Email: | At SPANA, we value your privacy and promise not to sell or trade your details to anyone. | Read our full Privacy Statement at spana.org/privacy | We will use your details to fulfil your request. If you would prefer not to receive fundraising | updates or appeals in the future, please tick here J | If you would like to receive updates and appeals from us via email, text or phone, please let us | know by ticking the relevant boxes: (] Email LJ Text LJ Phone | | enclose: J£15 My preferred amount £ | lenclose a: _/ Cheque L_} Postal Order LJ CAF Voucher | Please make cheques payable to SPANA and ensure that your full postcode is written on the back. | 2106RCI05 | |MasterCard Visa/Visa Debit |CAF Card | COOOL, COOOL | Expiry date: TT VLE (Not CAF) FR) | Cardholder's name: | Your generous donations will be used for projects such as those featured in this appeal and where | they are required the most to help animals in need. | Registered with | Signature: | Hel ven m wort nimals at xt ost to you iftaid be | Gift Aid is reclaimed by SPANA from the tax you pay for the current tax year. 3 | Taxes such as VAT and Council Tax do not qualify. | YES (| want to Gift Aid any donations | make today, or in the future, or have made in the | past four years to SPANA. | am a UK taxpayer and understand that if | pay less Income Tax | and/or Capital Gains Tax than the amount of Gift Aid claimed on all my donations in that tax | year it is my responsibility to pay any difference. | Please notify us if you want to cancel this declaration, change your name or home address | and/or no longer pay sufficient tax on your income and/or capital gains. If you pay Income | Tax at the higher or additional rate and want to receive the additional tax relief due to you, | you must include all your Gift Aid donations on your Self-Assessment tax return or ask | HM Revenue and Customs to adjust your tax code. By completing this declaration you | are confirming your name and address are correct. These details are | needed to identify you as a current UK taxpayer. OR: | NOC)! am not currently a UK taxpayer. Date: | MOISTEN HERE | REGULATOR | 3Y3H 0104",Newspaper,NA,NA
224,"YEMEN EMERGENCY APPEAL | ARR. | BE” ss. Welee a? | ses BP! t | MSF anaesthetist Lenka Cervefiova and her team care for a four-year-old boy after surgery | in Dhi As-Sufal hospital in Yemen's Ibb province. Photograph © Majd Aljunaid/MSF | ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | MSF NURSE | The conflict in Yemen is now in its sixth year. In Marib, in central | Yemen, nearly three million people have been displaced from their | homes and are living in makeshift camps close to the frontline. | Pregnant women and children are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, | we found the local hospitals | overwhelmed by the sheer number | of people and by an influx of | war-wounded. Around 2.7 million | people are in the province now, | many of them displaced from | their homes elsewhere and | looking for a safe haven. | People were living in makeshift | shelters made of plastic and metal | sheets. There were no latrines, | just pits dug in the ground | outside the tents. People were | very worried about their health, | about the risk of disease, about | not having enough food. We were | very concerned about the health | of mothers and children, and | pregnant women were a major | worry too. | Over the next month, our small | team worked around the clock | to set up clinics providing basic | healthcare and to supply hospitals | that were struggling. Getting | medicines to this part of Yemen | wasn't easy. Supplies often had to | cross frontlines. One of our trucks | carrying antibiotics, medicines | and other supplies was hijacked. | It was an incredibly difficult | environment to work in. | “As the frontline approaches | Marib city, people sheltering in | the area may find themselves | with no place else to go.” | Along with general healthcare, | we provided routine vaccinations | for children and offered antenatal | and postnatal care. We saw a | lot of pregnant women and | children and were able to | refer pregnant women with | complications to larger hospitals | for emergency caesareans. | It made a real difference. | The day we started working in a | rural hospital near the frontline, | a woman came in with a breech | DONATE NOW | Call: 0800 055 79 81 | seven days a week | Or search ‘MSF ALI’ | to make an online donation | delivery who was having real | difficulty. Our midwife assisted | and everything went well. But who _ | knows what would have happened | to that woman if the hospital | - wasn't there. | A DESPERATE SITUATION | Since I left, many more people | have come to the area. But Marib | is no longer a safe place to be. In | recent weeks, the frontlines have | moved into the province, causing | large numbers of injuries and | forcing thousands more people to | flee their homes. As the frontline | approaches Marib city, we are | concerned that people sheltering | in the area may find themselves | _ with no place else to go. | After six years of conflict, the | situation is desperate. These | people need our help now more | than ever.’ | scan to donate | MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES | DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | i WHAT IS HAPPENING | IN YEMEN? | Yemen is in the midst of civil war. After six years of conflict, | the medical and humanitarian situation in Yemen continues | to deteriorate. Poverty is widespread, malnutrition is on the | rise and people are confronted by the daily threat of violence. | Pregnant women struggle to access medical care. | Infant and maternal mortality are on the rise as mothers | and children bear the brunt of an increasingly brutal conflict. | MSF teams are providing urgent medical care to people | caught up in this crisis. We work in 12 hospitals and health | centres and support more than 20 hospitals and health | facilities across the country. | We provide medical support in emergency rooms, maternity | wards and outpatient departments, conduct surgeries, and | run mobile clinics in rural towns and villages. The situation | remains desperate. We need your help now more than ever. | Thank you. | WE CAN'T DOIT | WITHOUT YOU | could pay for three safe delivery | kits to assist women giving birth. | could provide an oxygen kit to | help care for premature babies | with underdeveloped lungs. | . £228 | could cover the cost of a | midwife working in a field | hospital for three days. | could pay for a portable | ultrasound device to monitor | - the health of pregnant women | and their foetuses. | Thank you | Charity Registration Number 1026588",Newspaper,NA,NA
225,"What will happen to our world in the future? One thing is certain, Greenpeace will | need to be there. You can count on us to defend the natural world and do the right | thing for our living planet. We will protect our oceans, forests and climate, for | people and all the earth’s natural wonders. | Greenpeace is vital to saving our planet and a gift in your Will makes sure we're _ | there. 1 in 6 of our campaigns only happen because of donations left to us in | people's Wills. | So, stand up for what you believe in. | Make a will, Save the world. Thank you. | Search greenpeace.org.uk/save",Newspaper,NA,NA
226,"> = pera ae > | Sightsavers : Sightsavers | 4 7) we | Nalukena geteeW x j N | cae | | SJ@ADS]YBIS | nowwn | zacc- | a+ 3 3 0 | -Fvuv0vTS | UO ® et | anor a8 | 2 aun ® | oz < | on=t oo | Soo 4 | I< OW | Will you Sian . | help protect | alukena | children fro wy bs | | Just £5 from you) | could save her sight. | could save her sight. | www.sightsavers.org/closer | | www.sightsavers.org/ p's www.sightsavers.org/closer | Trachoma steals sight with every blink... | Trachoma is an infection that's both agonising, and blinding. | At first, it makes your eyes itchy and sore. But if left | untreated, it develops into the more severe trichiasis - | which steals your sight blink by painful blink... | Nalukena had suffered from trachoma since birth - and | at just two years old, it was clear she was in real pain. | Her devastated parents did all they could to help their | little girl, but they didn't understand what was wrong. | They just knew it was getting worse. | Without help, | sight altogether. | You can save children from a life of pain | That’s why your support today is so vital. Together, we | can reach a whole class of children like Nalukena with | sight-saving treatment - and one day eliminate this cruel | infection for good. | From painful trachoma... | £5 could protect a class of children | Thankfully, we could help Nalukena with an antibiotic called | Zithromax* - which costs just 15p per treatment, and has the | power to stop trachoma in its tracks. | Now, Nalukena no longer has to live in pain, and can look forward | to a happy future. But there are still millions of children across | Africa who are moving closer to blindness each day. It's vital | we can treat them, before they lose their sight forever. | Your gift will save children’s sight | Just £5 from you is all it takes to provide Zithromax” for | a class of 33 children - saving them from blindness, and | moving us closer to our goal of eliminating trachoma by 2025. | To make a donation, simply complete the form | opposite and return it in the attached envelope, | give online, or call O800 298 2200 today. | little Nalukena was at risk of losing her | ...to blinding trichiasis | Trachoma starts with a | bite from an infected fly. @ | Will you send £5 today and save a class of children from blinding trachoma? | The eyes start to itch and weep, @ <p The eyelids then turn inwards, causing 4 <p> This condition is trichiasis, | and become very sore in sunlight. the eyelashes to scrape the eye. which eventually steals sight. | 4& DETACH HERE & | & DETACH HERE & | Yes, | want to give £5 to stop trachoma and save the sight of children like Nalukena | Title First name Surname | Full home address | Postcode | Phone number | Email | Are you happy to receive updates and find out how you can help by email or telephone? | If so, please fill in your details above. | We want you to be really proud of the work you're helping make ; | happen and will be in touch from time to time with updates about ThroughGiftAid,any dit | how your support is changing lives. To do this, we'll use the details donation you give will gifte | you've provided but promise never to sell or share your data. be worth 25% more. | If you'd rather not hear from us, please let us know by emailing tam a UK taxpayer and | would like | info@sightsavers.org or calling 01444 446600. Sightsavers to reclaim the tax paid on all my | past, present and future. | understand | [ 7 P : | | would like to make a donation of el [TT J-TT) that if | pay less Income Tax and/or Capital Gains | Tax in the current tax year than the amount | Please make cheque/postal order payable to Sightsavers tase poner of Gift Aid claimed on my donations, it is my | Or debit my: O MasterCard OVisa 0 CAF SEND CASH responsibility to pay any difference. | SC LILI ET LICE Lt (CJ I'd like Sightsavers to claim | Gift Aid on my donations. | eC LIC) be CLC | [Name on card | s! | [Signature | Date | Your donation will be used to support our work wherever the need is greatest. gS | Please detach this form and return it, with your gift, in the FREEPOST envelope attached. | Alternatively, visit www.sightsavers.org/closer or call 0800 298 2200. Thank you. | 207544 and $CO38110 | & DETACH HERE | 4 DETACH HERE & | MOISTEN HERE AND SEAL | JNDRAIS! | Registered with | REGULATOR | iS) Sightsavers | Every penny counts | and for every pound we receive” | 91% goes on vital charity work | goes on growing awareness | and our funding | Thank you for your support | Registered charity numbers 207544 and $CO38110",Newspaper,NA,NA
