231,"YEMEN EMERGENCY APPEAL 4 Sersxox | MSF anaesthetist Lenka Cerveiiova and her team care for a four-year-old boy after surgery | in Dhi As-Sufal hospital in Yemen's Ibb province. Photograph © Majd Aljunaid/MSF | ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | MSF NURSE | The conflict in Yemen is now in its sixth year. In Marib, in central | Yemen, nearly three million people have been displaced from their | homes and are living in makeshift camps close to the frontline. | Pregnant women and children are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, | we found the local hospitals | overwhelmed by the sheer number | of people and by an influx of | war-wounded. Around 2:7 million | people are in the province now, | many of them displaced from | their homes elsewhere and | looking for a safe haven. | People were living in makeshift | shelters made of plastic and metal | sheets. There were no latrines, | just pits dug in the ground | outside the tents. People were | very worried about their health, | about the risk of disease, about | not having enough food. We were | very concerned about the health | of mothers and children, and | pregnant women were a major | worry too. | Over the next month, our small | team worked around the clock | to set up clinics providing basic | healthcare and to supply hospitals | that were struggling. Getting | medicines to this part of Yemen | wasnt easy. Supplies often had to | cross frontlines. One of our trucks | carrying antibiotics, medicines | and other supplies was hijacked. | It was an incredibly difficult | environment to work in. | “As the frontline approaches | Marib city, people sheltering in | the area may find themselves | with no place else to go.” | Along with general healthcare, | we provided routine vaccinations | for children and offered antenatal | and postnatal care. We saw a | lot of pregnant women and | children and were able to | refer pregnant women with | complications to larger hospitals | for emergency caesareans. | It made a real difference. | The day we started working in a | rural hospital near the frontline, | a woman came in with a breech | delivery who was having real | difficulty. Our midwife assisted | and everything went well. But who | knows what would have happened | to that woman if the hospital | wasnt there. | A DESPERATE SITUATION | Since I left, many more people | have come to the area. But Marib | is no longer a safe place to be. In | recent weeks, the frontlines have | moved into the province, causing | large numbers of injuries and | forcing thousands more people to | flee their homes. As the frontline | approaches Marib city, we are | concerned that people sheltering | in the area may find themselves | with no place else to go. | After six years of conflict, the | situation is desperate. These | people need our help now more | than ever” | scan to donate | WHAT IS HAPPENING | IN YEMEN? | Yemen is in the midst of civil war. After six years of conflict, | the medical and humanitarian situation in Yemen continues | to deteriorate. Poverty is widespread, malnutrition is on the | rise and people are confronted by the daily threat of violence. | Pregnant women struggle to access medical care. | Infant and maternal mortality are on the rise as mothers | and children bear the brunt of an increasingly brutal conflict. | MSF teams are providing urgent medical care to people | caught up in this crisis. We work in 12 hospitals and health | centres and support more than 20 hospitals and health | facilities across the country. | We provide medical support in emergency rooms, maternity | wards and outpatient departments, conduct surgeries, and | run mobile clinics in rural towns and villages. The situation | remains desperate. We need your help now more than ever. | Thank you. | WE CAN’T DOIT | WITHOUT YOU | could pay for three safe delivery | kits to assist women “gi birth. | £130 | could provide an oxygen kit to | help care for premature babies | with underdeveloped lungs. | £228 | o> could cover the cost of a | midwife working in a field | hospital for three days. | £866 | could pay for a portable | ultrasound device to monitor | >> the health of pregnant women | and their foetuses. | DONATE NOW | Call: 0800 055 79 81 | Thank you . | seven days a week | Or search ‘MSF ALI’ | to make an online donation | Charity Registration Number 1026588",Newspaper,NA,NA
232,"Ethiopia-Tigray Emergency Appeal | GIVE SHELTER | £159 could provide a shelter kit — giving safety to families | fleeing the dreadful conflict. | Violence in Ethiopia’s Tigray region has seen more than two million people forced to flee their homes since the conflict started in | November 2020. Over 63,000 have fled into neighbouring Sudan, while those displaced inside Ethiopia include around 100,000 | refugees from Eritrea. | The region remains a tinderbox. There have been reports | of hundreds of people killed, of atrocities, including forced | military recruitment of men and boys, and of widespread | sexual violence against women. | Displaced families urgently need shelter. People have been | sleeping in the open air, with no access to water or food. | UNHCR teams on the ground are doing all they can to protect | people, but with funds running desperately low, they urgently | need your support. | £159 could pay for a shelter kit. As the diagram shows, each | kit contains bamboo poles, a bamboo floor mat, roof beams, | and other local building materials with which a family shelter | can be built. With the rainy season fast approaching, the shelter | you help provide could be lifesaving. And it will do more than | protect families from the elements; it will allow people to socially | distance to protect themselves from COVID-19, and give women | and girls a safe haven. | Without shelter, they are intensely vulnerable. UNHCR’s Country | Representative, Ann Encontre, said: “/ am deeply concerned by | WOODEN GABLE FRAME SHELTER | tess poe — ys | *PProx. 3 ——~ 2's —— == apr” | ¢ The kit you can help provide builds a shelter like | this, using locally available materials. | e Each kit contains a roof beam, wall beams, bamboo | poles and sticks, rope, a bamboo mat for flooring | and straw mesh to line the walls. | Example shelter kit from Sudan. Content and structure vary depending on | location and materials available. | the widespread allegations of sexual violence against women and girls. The level of cruelty being reported defies belief.” | RIGHT NOW, SHELTER WILL SAVE PEOPLE’S LIVES. | Families have been forced to flee the terrifying violence and sought safety in other parts of Ethiopia’s Tigray region or in Sudan. | And every day a family spends without shelter, is a day when they are more at risk of contracting COVID-19, and a day when | women and girls remain more vulnerable to sexual violence. Please give £159, or whatever you can afford, today — there is nota | moment to lose. | £159 COULD PROVIDE A SHELTER KIT | ive now at UNrefugees.org.uk/tigraycrisis | Call 0800 029 3883. or fill in and return the form below | | would like to give: |_| £79 |_| 2159 |_| 318 |_| Other | Cl enclose a cheque made payable to UK for UNHCR OR | _] | would like to make a donation by debit/credit card | . Expirydate | = | ees) | Date | | Card no. *E ‘eo: s ar pig! 3 | SR eee ee Sei Seay) = | | Signature | Oni Tick here, if you don’t want to receive an acksbenbecpictient by post | Title First name Last name | Address _ arnt ewe, ee é a eee | RES Postcode = 5 | Email | Phone | Please tell us if you are happy to hear about UNHCR’s work: O By email oO By phone | Registered with | FUNDRAISING | REGULATOR | Your gift will help families displaced by the Ethiopia Emergency, | funding shelter and other interventions, depending on greatest need. | United Kingdom for UNHCR is a registered charity in England and Wales (1183415). | Please return this form along with your cheque to: | Freepost UK FOR UNHCR (you don’t need a stamp) | Increase your donation by 25p for every £1 you donate | giftaid ue | (1 would like UK for UNHCR to claim Gift Aid on this donation (currently 25p | for each £1 donated), as well as any donations | make in the future and have made in the past four | years to UK for UNHCR, until | notify you otherwise. | confirm | am a UK taxpayer and | understand | that if | pay less Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax than the amount of Gift Aid claimed on all my | donations in that tax year it is my responsibility to pay any difference. | understand that council tax | and VAT do not qualify. | If your circumstances change or you have any questions, please contact our Supporter Care Team | on 0800 029 3883 or email supportercare@unrefugees.org.uk | Respecting your personal data: We will use your details to process your | donation and to keep you up to date with our work, fundraising activities and | other events. You can read more about how we use your data in our Privacy Policy | unrefugees.org.uk/privacy-policy You can opt out of any communications at any | time by contacting our Supporter Care Team using the details above. | (4) UNHCR | Scan this | QR code to & . “a The UN Refugee Agency | donate online United Kingdom for UNHCR | P21ETOBA2",Newspaper,NA,NA
233,"me not | Appea | ll you show your | support for people | affected by dementia? | Thank you so much if you already Forget Me Not Appeal | support Alzheimer’s Society, and please | | . feel free to pass on this le iend, | “ely | p tter toa friend ee recycle | | ~~) ai | Again | Dementia q | Give today and you'll | receive a limited | edition Forget Me | Not pin badge. | Who will you wear | yours for? | Your Forget Me Not | stickers are inside | Registered charity no. 296645. Alzheimer’s Society operates in England, Wales and Northern Ireland. FMNOU04 | Forget | me not | Appeal | Dementia | Alzheimer’s Society | Scott Lodge | This summer, will you Show your sccttreaa | Plymouth | love for somebody affected by —»zs0u | T 0330 333 0804 | dementia and support our seule | alzheimers.org.uk | Forget Me Not Appeal? this arena | Dear friend, | This last year hasn't been an easy time for any of us, and if you or someone you know is affected | by dementia, you'll understand just how isolating lockdowns and social distancing have been. | But we've got through it by pulling together, by acting as a community even when we've been | physically apart. | Alzheimer’s Society has been supporting people living with | dementia and their loved ones for over 40 years. And since | March 2020 our vital services; providing advice, guidance | and emotional support to people affected by dementia, | have been needed like never before. People have been | through so much and told us they’ve been feeling | forgotten, and that they want an opportunity to remember | and pay tribute to their loved ones. That’s why we have | launched our new Forget Me Not Appeal and why I’m | asking you to be part of it. It would be wonderful if you | could give a gift, to help fund vital support services for | people affected by dementia. | Please, will you give £20 to our Forget Me Not Appeal in | honour of someone you know affected by dementia? | To give now, you can fill in and return the enclosed form, visit alzheimers.org.uk/forgetmenotappeal | or call 0330 333 0804. | If you do give, we'll send you a Forget Me Not pin badge. Do you know who you will wear it for? |’|| be | wearing my badge and supporting the appeal for my mum, who has late-stage dementia and is | currently in a care home. She doesn’t know me now, but | know she doesn’t love me any less. | As you can imagine, the visiting restrictions this past year have been really tough. | can only | pom hope Mum found comfort as Dad and me waved and shouted that we loved her through | her window. | | hope you'll join me and support the appeal in solidarity with | somebody you know who's affected by dementia, and all the | challenges they face. Even though dementia is so common | across the UK, it’s widely misunderstood and people still face | alot of stigma. So if we all support the appeal and wear a | Please turn over... | FMNLETO3 | badge it will be a way of raising our voices — of telling the UK | that people living with dementia matter. | But the Forget Me Not Appeal is about more than raising | awareness - it’s about raising funds to give people living with | dementia, and their carers, the practical support they need | right now. | That’s why I hope you'll give £20 so Alzheimer’s Society | can be here for someone like Christine, who cares for her | husband Roy. | Perhaps Christine’s experiences resonate with your own? | ‘The man | love had changed and life seemed upside-down. | | didn’t know how to approach it. He would forget words. Now he can’t communicate with me, and | | have carers to help shower him. They talk about lockdown, but I’ve been in permanent lockdown | since Roy became ill as we're so isolated. Friends don’t come any more as they don’t know what to | say. The man| love is disappearing.’ | if someone you know is affected by dementia, you'll probably recognise the sense of isolation that | Christine mentions. Even in the best of times, isolation has a massive impact on the mental and | physical health of people living with dementia — and this last 15 or 16 months it’s been so much | harder. That’s why the Forget Me Not Appeal is so important — it will fund services like our | Dementia Advisers and Companion Calls, to make sure nobody has to face dementia alone. | For Christine, the Companion Calls service was a lifeline. I’m so grateful that the past kindness | of people like you made it possible to be there for her: | ‘My Companion Caller Mel rings every week and never misses — we have such a laugh. I’m so | involved with Roy’s dementia every day — but Mel makes me feel human again.’ | | don’t think there’s any way to overstate how vital that is — to help someone feel like a person, a | wife, husband, daughter, friend — and not just a carer. So when you send in your gift and then wear | your Forget Me Not badge this summer, | hope you'll think not just of someone who's special to | you, but also of all the people like Christine, who can feel a bit more human thanks to your support. | Thank you so much for getting behind our Forget Me Not Appeal. | Kate Loe ) | Kate Lee | Alzheimer’s Society CEO | ay ok hope you'll Support the Forget Me Not. Appeal to help | provicle much-neecle cl emotional anck practical Support to people | aected by clementia. Ancl =E also hope you'll wear your baclae When | you receive it, in honour of Someone Speccal to you: thank you SO much. | Alzheimer’s Society respects the confidentiality of everyone who uses our services and keeps that | information strictly private. Should this letter reach you at a difficult time, please accept our apologies. | lf you or your family have been affected by dementia we would like to support you in every way we can; | please contact our Dementia Connect support line on 0333 150 3456 or visit our website at | alzheimers.org.uk You can also find out about services available near you at | alzheimers.org.uk/dementiaconnect | our gift will mean compassion, empathy | and support when people need it most. | Please give what you can to the Forget Me Not Appeal | £20 could pay for over an hour’s support | from a Dementia Adviser. | In that hour they could help people cope with | their condition on a day-to-day basis, understand | the benefits they’re entitled to, or look after their | mental health if they’re still isolating at home. | £40 means 250 people can access Talking | Point, our online community. | With Talking Point a trouble shared can become | a trouble halved — people can learn from each | other’s experiences, share their frustrations and | their Knowledge of local services. | £105 supports a Dementia Adviser for | one day. | Dementia Advisers are dementia experts. They | provide information, offer guidance and directly | support people affected by dementia, day in, and | day out. | Will you choose to change the future of dementia? | You're doing something incredible to help thousands | of families affected by dementia. We'd love to | keep in touch with you about our amazing services | LJ Yes, I'd like to receive updates by email, | contact me at: | and research, and how you can get involved with L_] Yes, 'd be happy to speak to someone on the | fundraising, campaigning, or volunteering. It’s totally phone, contact me on: | up to you — you can update what you get at any time | and we'll never sell your details to third parties. [ | Yes, I'd like to receive updates via SMS, | Our work can’t continue without people like you, so contact me on: | please choose to stay in touch using these boxes | and help us beat dementia. If you'd like to change | what you receive from us at any time, please call | LJ Please do NOT contact me with updates | through the post | Customer Care on 0330 333 0804. | | Dear friend, | Your gift to the Forget Me Not Appeal will help change the lives of people | affected by dementia. Your kindness will mean more people living with | dementia and their carers get the practical and emotional support they | desperately need. Thank you. | Kate Lee, Chief Executive Officer | / . Yes, Pll join the Forget Me Not Appeal to | /  Attachyour +‘ ° | | ForgetMeNot | Support people affected by dementia. | stickerhere ; | Do y Don’t forget to ve US Your cle tails So we can Send p | poe * your Forget Me Not pin baclge. ) — | My details: Please complete all fields to make a valid Gift Aid claim. | Title. —s—COsCS<*First Naame Surname | Address | Postcode | 2 | will give: L_] £20 to help pay for urgently needed support services ALZH0230 | L] OR my choice of £ | L | enclose a cheque/postal order made payable to Alzheimer’s Society OR | Please debit my (visa LJMasterCard [JCAF Card with the amount specified. | Card Start Expiry | no. date ~ date im | Signature Today’s | date ~ ai | All donations will go to where they are needed most: to support services, research and campaigning on behalf of people with dementia. | rll be wearing my pin badge in tribute to | Please use this space to tell us why you want to support people affected by dementia. | Sharing real stories is a powerful way to demonstrate the value of supporting people affected by dementia. | By filling in this box you are consenting to us potentially using your story in future updates or appeals. | Please turn every £10 you donate into £12.50 at no extra cost to you , ; it | & teas a | OU lam happy for all gifts of money that | have made to Alzheimer’s Society in the | past four years, and all future gifts of money that | make, to be Gift Aid donations. | |amaUK taxpayer and understand that if | pay less Income Tax & Capital Gains Tax in the current | tax year than the amount of Gift Aid claimed on all of my donations across all charities, it is my | responsibility to pay any difference. Alzheimer’s Society reclaims 25p for every £1 you donate from the | tax you pay for the current tax year. If you change your name, address or tax status, do let us know. | Please return this form in the Freepost envelope provided or post it to: | Freepost Plus RUAT—CJJT-—TEJB, Forget Me Not Appeal, Alzheimer’s Hover your | Society, Bumpers Way, Bumpers Farm, Chippenham SN 14 6NG One Camera | You can also donate online at alzheimers.org.uk/forgetmenotappeal over ths code t© [aah | or call 0330 333 0804 DMC2105INE  V= online NOW > | | | Registered charity no. 296645. A company limited by guarantee and registered in England no. 2115499. Alzheimer’s Society operates in England, Wales and Northern Ireland. FMNOFO4 | | From: see | zhei | From | From: | From: | Youcanwearthe - o> | Forget Me Not — | stickers (below) | | or stick one to | your donation | form to symbolise | your support. | Registered charity no. 296645. Alzheimer’s Society operates in England, | Wales and Northern Ireland. | DMC2 105iAN | 4 Dementia | Please affix | postage | stamp here to | help reduce | our costs. | Freepost Plus RUAT-CJJT-TEJB | Forget Me Not Appeal | Alzheimer's Society | Bumpers Way | Bumpers Farm | Chippenham | SN14 6NG | ————_vo-———_—_—-",Newspaper,NA,NA
234,"MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES | DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | The conflict in Yemen is now | in its sixth year. In Marib, in | central Yemen, nearly three | million people have been | displaced from their homes and | are living in makeshift camps | close to the frontline. Pregnant women and children | are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, we found the local | hospitals overwhelmed by the sheer number of people | and by an influx of war-wounded. Nearly 3 million | people are in the province now, many of them | displaced from elsewhere. | People were living in makeshift shelters made of | plastic and metal sheets. There were no latrines, just | pits dug in the ground. People were worried about | their health, about the risk of disease, about not | having enough food. We were very concerned about | the health of mothers and children, and pregnant | women were also a major worry. | Over the next month, our small team worked | around the clock to set up clinics providing basic | healthcare and to supply hospitals that were | struggling. Getting medicines to this part of Yemen | wasn’t easy. Supplies often had to cross frontlines. | ‘PREGNANT | YEMEN EMERGENCY APPEAL | Dr Oruh2 examines two-month-old | Muhammad Jamat, admitted to the | paediatric ward of Ad-Oahi rurat hospital | afer sutras diarrhoea and ivomiting. | One of our trucks carrying antibiotics, medicines | and other supplies was hijacked. It was an incredibly | difficult environment to work in. | Along with general healthcare, we provided | routine vaccinations for children and offered | antenatal and postnatal care. We saw a lot of pregnant | women and children and were able to refer pregnant | women with complications to larger hospitals for | emergency caesareans. It made a real difference. | The day we started working in a rural hospital | near the frontline, a woman came in with a breech | delivery who was having real difficulty. Our | midwife assisted and everything went well. But | who knows what would have happened if the | hospital wasn’t there. | AN URGENT SITUATION | Since I left, many more people have come to | the area. But Marib is no longer a safe place to | be. In recent weeks, the frontlines have moved | into the province, causing large numbers of | injuries and forcing thousands more people | to flee their homes. | After six years of conflict, the situation is | desperate. These people need our help now | more than ever. | Thank you.” | TO SUPPORT OUR | MEDICAL TEAMS | could pay for three safe delivery kits | to assist women giving birth | £130 | could provide an oxygen kit to help care for | premature babies with underdeveloped lungs | £228 | could cover the the cost of a midwife working | ina field hospital for three days | CALL | SEARCH | OR SE | 100671",Newspaper,NA,NA
235,"Or Druha examines two-month-old | Muhammad Jamal, admitted to the | paediatric ward of Ad-Dahi rural hospital | after suffering diar vomiting. | tooraph © Majd ASE | MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES | >) DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | YEMEN EMERGENCY APPEAL | ‘After six years of conflict, the situation is desperate. Yemeni women | and children need our help now more than ever.” | ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | MSF NURSE | The conflict in Yemen is now | in its sixth year. In Marib, in | central Yemen, nearly three | million people have been | displaced from their homes | and are living in makeshift | camps close to the frontline. Pregnant women and | children are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, we found the local © | hospitals overwhelmed by the sheer number of | people and by an influx of war-wounded. Nearly | 3 million people are in the province now, many | of them displaced from elsewhere. | People were living in makeshift shelters made | of plastic and metal sheets. There were no latrines, | just pits dug in the ground. People were worried | about their health, about the risk of disease, | about not having enough food. We were very | concerned about the health of mothers and children, | and pregnant women were also a major worry. | Over the next month, our small team worked | around the clock to set up clinics providing basic | healthcare and to supply hospitals that were | struggling. Getting medicines to this part of | ""Pregnant women and | children are in desperate | need of medical care’ | Yemen wasn't easy. Supplies often had to cross _ | frontlines. One of our trucks carrying antibiotics, | medicines and other supplies was hijacked. It was | an incredibly difficult environment to work in. | Along with general healthcare, we provided | routine vaccinations for children and offered . | _ antenatal and postnatal care. We saw a lot of | pregnant women and children and were able | to refer pregnant women with complications | to larger hospitals for emergency caesareans. | It made a real difference. | The day we started working in a rural hospital | near the frontline, a woman came in witha | breech delivery who was having real difficulty. | Our midwife assisted and everything went well. | But who knows what would have happened if | the hospital wasn’t there. | AN URGENT SITUATION | Since I left, many more people have come to the | area. But Marib is no longer a safe place to be. In | recent weeks, the frontlines have moved into the | province, causing large numbers of injuries and | forcing thousands more people to flee their homes. | After six years of conflict, the situation is | desperate. These people need our help now | more than ever. | Thank you.” © | DONATE TO SUPPORT OUR MEDICAL TEAMS | £44 COULD PAY FOR THREE SAFE DELIVERY | KITS TO ASSIST WOMEN GIVING BIRTH | CALL 08000557985 | OR SCAN TO DONATE | SEARCH MSFCRISIS = “e855 | 100658",Newspaper,NA,NA
