G.UK/DONATE YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | Bentiu <r | s { The hospital is run by more than | 500 MSF staff, most of whom | Protection | -=-= | of Civilians | camp | Bentiu Protection of Civilians camp | in South Sudan is home to some | 100,000 people who have sought | refuge from conflict. Residents live in | temporary shelters made of corrugated | metal, plant stalks, mud and plastic | sheeting. These offer limited protection | from the elements, especially during | the rainy season when walkways | become lanes of mud. People’s medical | needs are enormous. | MSF operates the main hospital in the | UN-run camp, which provides general | medical care, surgery, treatment for malaria, | malnutrition and diseases linked to poor | water and along with | for tuberculosis and HIV. | _ Na! Ne | RA \ EES | “MSF is operating one of the only hospitals | 4 a t & | thar has surgical services — not only in Bentiu, | but in the wider region,” says MSF surgeon | John Buckles, who has recurned to Bentiu | five times to conduct surgeries. “Working | here, you're faced with a broad spectrum | of conditions, ranging from tropical diseases | to surgical conditions that occur in any | society where there are abdominal issues, | hrough to gency All of | these conditions have continued during | the COVID-19 pandemic.” | Each ward of the 120-bed b> $ | hospital is housed in a white Le | tent connected by covered q Weter tanks_ _ ‘kk | walkways. “I was working wy —* | 12 to 15 hours a day for seven — | Russtration Jere Padery | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | Sj LA | FS ge om | “From my point of view as a surgeon, | Bentiu and operated in a tent with a | small air-conditioning unit dripping onto | the floor. | was very happy with the | surgical outcomes this latest trip. We | had a lot of very sick patients, but we | didn't lose any of them.” | isn't replaceable. If MSF wasn't there, _ | pleased that you've been able to heip, | while also knowing that, if it wasn’t | thousands of people would be without | the medical care and lifesaving | surgery that they need. | “A little bit of money goes a long | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | AROUND THE WORLD | Around the | world with MSF | WE’RE PROUD OF HOW WE SPEND | YOUR CONTRIBUTIONS | (MSF UK Annual Report 2019) | 85% 13% | ON OUR HUMANITARIAN WORK ON FUNDRAISING (WE RAISE | £8.24 FOR EVERY £1 WE SPEND) | ON OFFICE MANAGEMENT | Photograph © Avra | Fiatas/MSF | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE | PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | ITALY | Over winter, hundreds of mi | and refugees travelled the ‘Balkan | route’ on foot to reach Italy. The | Helmand \ | Photograph © Andrew Quilty | 4 SOUTH SUDAN | MSF physiotherapist Birgit | Schénharting plays with | five-year-old Anyar and helps | him regain the use of his leg | after he was bitten by a snake | SESE ESE while playing outside his house. | Photograph © Damaris Giulana/MSF | MOZAMBIQUE | Photograph © MSF | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE | PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE",Newspaper,NA,NA
242,"MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES | EED 0 | EDICA | YEMEN EMERGENCY APPEAL | ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | MSF NURSE : | The conflict in Yemen is now | in its sixth year. In Marib, in | central Yemen, nearly three | million people have been | displaced from their homes and | are living in makeshift camps | close to the frontline. Pregnant women and children | are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, we found the local | hospitals overwhelmed by the sheer number of people | and by an influx of war-wounded. Nearly 3 million | people are in the province now, many of them | displaced from elsewhere. | People were living in makeshift shelters made of | plastic and metal sheets. There were no latrines, just | pits dug in the ground. People were worried about | their health, about the risk of disease, about not | having enough food. We were very concerned about | the health of mothers and children, and pregnant | women were also a major worry. | Over the next month, our small team worked | around the clock to set up clinics providing basic | healthcare and to supply hospitals that were | struggling. Getting medicines to this part of Yemen | wasn't easy. Supplies often had to cross frontlines. | DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | ‘PREGNANT | | WOMEN AND | CHILDREN ARE | IN DESPERATE » | erent | Or Druha examines two-month-old | Muhammad Jamal, admitted to the | paediatric ward of Ad-Dahi rural hospital | after suffering dlerntioee ant vomiting: | One of our trucks carrying antibiotics, medicines | and other supplies was hijacked. It was an incredibly | ~ difficult environment to work in. | Along with general healthcare, we provided | routine vaccinations for children and offered | antenatal and postnatal care. We saw a lot of pregnant | women and children and were able to refer pregnant | women with complications to larger hospitals for | emergency caesareans. It made a real difference. | The day we started working in a rural hospital | near the frontline, a woman came in with a breech | delivery who was having real difficulty. Our | midwife assisted and everything went well. But | who knows what would have happened if the | hospital wasn’t there. | AN URGENT SITUATION j | Since I left, many more people have come to | the area. But Marib is no longer a safe place to | be. In recent weeks, the frontlines have moved | into the province, causing large numbers of | injuries and forcing thousands more people | to flee their homes. | After six years of conflict, the situation is | desperate. These people need our help now | more than ever. | Thank you.” | CALL0800 0557973 | DONATE NOW | TO SUPPORT OUR | MEDICAL TEAMS | could pay for three safe delivery kits | to assist women giving birth | £130 | could provide an oxygen kit to help care for | premature babies with underdeveloped lungs | £228 | could cover the the cost of a midwife working | ina field hospital for three days | SEARCH MSF MEDIC | OR SCAN TO DONATE | 100674",Newspaper,NA,NA
243,"Ethiopia-Tigray Emergency Appeal | GIVE SHELTER | £159 could provide a shelter kit — giving safety to | families fleeing the dreadful conflict. | Violence in Ethiopia’s Tigray region has | seen more than two million people forced | to flee their homes since the conflict | started in November 2020. Over 63,000 | have fled into neighbouring Sudan, while | those displaced inside Ethiopia include | around 100,000 refugees from Eritrea. | The region remains a tinderbox. There | have been reports of hundreds of people | killed, of atrocities, including forced | military recruitment of men and boys, and | of widespread sexual violence against | women. | Displaced families urgently need shelter. | People have been sleeping in the open | air, with no access to water or food. | UNHCR teams on the ground are doing | all they can to protect people, but they | urgently need your support. | £159 could pay for a shelter kit. As the | diagram shows, each kit contains local | building materials with which a family shelter | can be built. The shelter you help provide | could be lifesaving; it will allow people to | socially distance to protect themselves | from COVID-19, and give women and girls | a safe haven. | Without shelter, they are intensely vulnerable. | UNHCR’s Country Representative, Ann | Encontre, said: “/ am deeply concerned by | the widespread allegations of sexual violence | against women and girls. The level of cruelty | WOODEN GABLE FRAME SHELTER | e The kit you can help provide builds | a shelter like this, using locally | available materials. | e Each kit contains a roof beam, wall | beams, bamboo poles and sticks, rope, | a bamboo mat for flooring and straw | mesh to line the walls. | Example shelter kit from Sudan. Content and | structure vary depending on location and | materials available. | being reported defies belief” | RIGHT NOW, SHELTER WILL SAVE | PEOPLE’S LIVES. | Every day a family spends without shelter, | is a day when they are more at risk of | contracting COVID-19, and a day when | women and girls remain more vulnerable | to sexual violence. | Please give £159, or whatever you | can afford, today — there is nota | moment to lose. | £459 couLpD PROVIDE A SHELTER KIT | Give now at. | unrefugees.org.uk/tigraycrisis | Call O800 029 3883, or fill in and return the form below | I would like to give: [_] £79 [_] £159 [_] ¢318 [_] others | (-]1 enclose a cheque made payable to UK for UNHCR OR | (1 would like to make a donation by debit/credit card | Card no. | Expiry date | Zee SR aes Saas | Signature Date ; | Oo Tick here, if you don’t want to receive an acknowledgement by post | Title First name Last name | Address | Postcode | Email | Phone | Please tell us if you are happy to hear about UNHCR’s work: Oo By email | By phone | Please return this form along with your cheque to: | Freepost UK FOR UNHCR (you don’t need a stamp) | crease your donation by 25p for every tyou donate es d ie | 1 would like UK for UNHCR to claim Gift Aid on this donation (currently 25p foreach J fad : | £1 donated), as well as any donations | make in the future and have made in the past four : : | years to UK for UNHCR, until | notify you otherwise. | confirm | am a UK taxpayer and | understand that if! pay less | Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax than the amount of Gift Aid claimed on all my donations in thet tex year itis _ | my responsibility to pay any difference. | understand that council tax and VAT do not qualify. re 5 | if your circumstances change or you have any questions. please contact our Supporter Care Team on | 0800 029 3883 or email supportercare@unrefugees.org.uk | especting your personal data: We will use your details to process your | donation and to keep you up to date with our work, fundraising activities and __ | other events. You can read more about how we use your data in our Privacy Policy | unrefugees.org.uk/privacy-policy You can opt out of any communications at any | time by contacting our Supporter Care Team using the details above. | ; wale) Scan this Registered with | pote, @ mae (UNHCR | a REGULATOR | * donate online oy | % s : The UN Refugee Agency | Your gift will help families displaced by the Ethiopia United Kingdom for UNHCR | Emergency, funding shelter and other interventions, | ~ depending on greatest need. | United Kingdom for UNHCR is a registered charity in England and Wales (1183415). | P21ETIIA1",Newspaper,NA,NA
244,"“I was completely in awe | of these creatures.” | Chief Executive Mark Rose has been in charge at FFI for | over 25 years. Here he speaks about his experiences and | inspirations, and the scale of challenges ahead. | Chief Executive Mark Rose has | been in charge at FFI for over 25 | years. Here the zoologist-turned- | conservationist speaks about his | passion for conservation and a | memorable wildlife encounter. | “I've always loved the natural world, | but for reasons | can’t quite explain | itis reptiles that fascinate me most. | Perhaps it's that they're prehistoric, | like mini-dinosaurs. | vividly | remember my grandfather giving | me an aquarium when | was seven | years old. He told me | could keep | goldfish in it, but | said | would prefer | to put snakes and lizards in there -| | spent quite a lot of my childhood | catching them. | “| was probably destined to work | with wildlife, and with reptiles in | particular, from that very moment. | Sure enough, after studying zoology | at London University and spending | a period in West Africa via the | Sahara, | eventually found myself in | Papua New Guinea. That was where | | really cut my conservation teeth, as | a district wildlife officer working on | crocodiles. It was the conservation | equivalent of the Wild West and I | loved every minute of it. | “It was several decades later that | | first took charge at Fauna & Flora | International, after carving out a | career turning around the fortunes | of another charity working in the | UK. When | arrived, we only had a | handful of active projects, one of | which was the Mountain Gorilla | Project. It actually took me 10 years | to see these amazing great apes | in the flesh, because | was so busy | growing the organisation. | “| eventually joined a trip to | Rwanda at the insistence of one | of our donors. It was a fascinating, | humbling experience. We had | no sooner walked into the forest | on the first day than a group of | gorillas came hurtling towards us; | they were being chased by some | overenthusiastic young males. | | found myself running alongside | them until the chaos subsided, | which was a pretty invigorating | experience, to say the least. | “The next day, we caught up with | one of the largest gorilla groups | and followed them for about an | hour, walking with them until we | -reached a clearing. When the adults | sat down, the youngsters took it | a$ a signal that it was playtime and | You can be one of the heroes saving the planet. Please | turn to your donation form on page 3 and respond today. | Thank you. | showed off their acrobatic skills by | swinging among the trees. It was | pouring down, but the mist and rain | just added to the atmosphere. | “| was completely in awe of these | creatures — their powerful presence, | their obvious intelligence, their | gentle nature — and | don't know | anyone who hasn't been moved by | the experience of spending time | with them. That memory will stay | with me forever. | “Those gorillas were being | protected by the national park | authorities in Rwanda, with support | from FFI. That's the essence of our | whole approach to conservation. | We take the longer, but ultimately | more effective, route to enable | people to protect the wildlife on | their own doorsteps. | “People often ask me about | my vision for FFI. Put simply, | | want us to be redundant. We | have a vision of a world in which | species and habitats can thrive | without our intervention, a world in | which — eventually — our services | are no longer required. But that | day is a long way in the future. In | the meantime, we have a vital role | to play, working with the people | closest to threatened species and | habitats in order to combat the | global crisis that we are facing. | “Thanks to our supporters, we | have consistently defied the odds, | achieving success after success | in the one hundred plus years | since FFI was founded. The scale | of the challenge is enormous. As | | write this, over 28,000 species are | threatened with extinction. Every | year, more species are added to | that list, including some that have | only just been discovered. | urge | anyone who cares about the natural | world to set up a regular gift today | and join our fight for a better planet. | V4 \ FAUNA & FLORA | INTERNATIONAL | Fighting back against | mass extinction | D FAUNA & FLORA | INTERNATIONAL | The Sunda pangolin is one of the most threatened of 28,338 species | dangerously close to disappearing forever. It’s time to act. | Take a good hard look at the | pangolin (pictured above). Blink | and you may never see it again. | This mysterious and barely | understood creature could now | vanish after 65 million years on | the planet. Timid, slow moving and | completely toothless, its defence | against predators is to roll into a | ball and wait for danger to pass. | But it cannot defend itself against | the greatest predator of them all: | humans. Since 2000, more than | one million pangolins have been | The illegal trade that could put us all at risk | llegal wildlife trade creates opportunities for | massacred for their meat, which | ends up in restaurants, and for their | scales, which are falsely believed to | have medicinal properties. Pangolin | foetuses are said to increase | virility and are consumed in soup. | Carcasses are steeped in rice wine | to concoct a ‘health tonic’. Some | restaurants are said to offer to slit the | pangolin's throat at the table in front | of the guest, to ensure its freshness | before it is served and eaten. The | blood is reportedly drained and drunk | as an aphrodisiac. The list goes on. | Avast criminal industry has risen up | around the Sunda pangolin’s slaughter | and trade, with shipments worth tens | of millions of dollars. It is now the | world’s most trafficked wild mammal. | This is precisely why Fauna & Flora | International (FFI) exists: to stop | animals like the Sunda pangolin | becoming extinct. The planet | has reached a tipping point. The | spectre of mass extinction and | habitat destruction is looming. | Scientists are saying (cont. p2) | “Fauna & Flora | International saves | endangered species | and habitats around | the world. I’m proud to | have been a member | for over 50 years.” | Sir David Attenborough | ‘Inside: | Eastern black rhino | numbers plummeted | The mountain gorilla | success story | Start saving wildlife and | habitats today | Poaching, trafficking, forest fires and more - FFI tackles the most urgent threats to our | planet. But your support makes it all possible. Please turn to the form on page 3, go to | www.supportffi.org or call 01223 749019 to make a donation today. Thank you. | (cont. from p1) that | the Doomsday Clock's | hands are now set at two | minutes to midnight. | Saving the Sunda pangolin_ | | and its fragile habitat is | a test case for saving | wildlife globally. In Sumatra, | | we've already helped bring | over 70,000 hectares of | land under the care of | Eastern black rhinos: num | conservationists and the | surrounding communities. | But here, as well as in | many trafficking hotspots | for endangered species, | we need more land under | local management. | Right now, our | investigation teams are | doing painstaking | work, building legal cases | against the traffickers, but | this requires both time and | tremendous rigour. | Debbie Martyr, Technical | Adviser at FFI says, “Any | case we put together | has to be completely | watertight. No evidence, | no case. We need to keep | up the momentum and get | that evidence fast. Arrests | show we can follow | through and get results, | but we can’t let these | criminals regroup. | Today, you can help | us build up our anti- | trafficking network across | the island of Sumatra. | That's the only way the | Sunda pangolin will have | any kind of future.” | DeTS | dropped 90% in three generations | Saving this iconic species would set a global example of effective | conservation. Today, only around 850 remain alive. | Nicolas Tubbs, Senior | Programme Manager | at FFI, talks about | encountering both the | eastern black rhino and | the poachers hunting | down this critically | endangered animal: | “Suddenly coming face | to face with a rhino after | tracking it on foot is a truly | unforgettable moment. | Words that come to mind | are emblematic, threatened | and grumpy! Their | grumpiness has a charm in | itself. You'd never believe | how such a large animal | can camouflage so well - | you could be standing next | to it and never know. Sadly, | they can't hide from | the poachers. | “Confronting a rhino | poacher brings up other | emotions. My feelings | are a combination of | anger and despair — as | well as the determination | to understand why, It's | absolutely important to | reach out. To understand | what would make every | poacher stop and finda | viable and sustainable | alternative for their | own future. | “The whole key, in fact, | to saving species like | the eastern black rhino | is support from local | communities. Without | that, the poaching will | never stop — and poaching | remains the single greatest | threat to an adult rhino. | Fascinating facts about eastern black rhinos | 2 Have two horns, made of | compressed hair-like keratin | strands (like fingernail fibres). | Soak in mud or roll in dust as | protection against sunburn and | insect bites. | Calves are vulnerable to | lions and hyenas, but once | they reach adulthood, even | a lion would struggle to | take one down. | “Primarily due to poaching, | a staggering 96% of black | rhinos died between 1970 | and 1992, leaving only | 2,475. Now a mere 850 | are in existence compared | to 65,000 a century ago. | The eastern black rhino is | under severe threat and its | last remaining stronghold is | in Kenya. | “Kenya is home to around | 80% of eastern black | rhinos. To protect them, FFI | is working with Ol Pejeta | Conservancy, a crucial | sanctuary in Kenya's | Laikipia County that holds | the largest population of | eastern black rhinos in | East Africa. | “FFI has worked with | Ol Pejeta ever since | its establishment in | 2003, assisting with the | transportation of black | rhinos into the sanctuary | and protecting them from | poaching. We have ranger | patrols checking on every | single rhino every four days | to ensure that poachers | are kept off the reserve. | We must stay vigilant. | “To see the eastern black | rhino become extinct | would be devastating. FFI | doesn't shy away from | a challenge. We stick at | it, finding locally driven | solutions, fitting the | local context. | “We work in notoriously | difficult places. But if we | can't conserve a species | as emblematic as a rhino, | then how can we save far | less famous ones?” | Can go five days without water | by obtaining moisture from | succulent plants. | Mountain gorillas are back from | the brink of extinction | Celebrating one of conservation’s greatest success stories | “The first time I visited | mountain gorillas, I was | overwhelmed by a sense | of acceptance. It was very | clear that these incredible | gorillas are entirely aware | of us humans. Some gorillas | allow us to spend time with | them, the mature silverback | males especially.” | Anna Behm Masozera is a | woman who is certain of | her mission. As Director | of the International Gorilla | Conservation Programme | (IGCP), a collaboration | between Fauna & Flora | International and other | partners, she has seena | dramatic change in the | fortunes of the mountain | gorilla. Figures reached a low | in the 1970s with only a few | hundred left, but there are | now over 1,000 — and that | number continues to rise. | So what's the secret? Part of | it has been the intersection | of people and economics. | Anna explains: “Conserving | mountain gorillas means | tackling different things at | the same time. You have to | deal with borders between | Rwanda, the Democratic | Republic of Congo and | Uganda. You have to face | threats like traffickers who | target young gorillas for | private buyers, but also | people who are dependent | on subsistence agriculture, | who put down snares | because they need to eat | the next day. | “Tourism that brings | everyone on board ensures | mountain gorillas living wild | and free in intact habitats | are worth more than any | other option, like poaching | or land conversion. | ""We have to recognise | that mountain gorillas are | revered and hold a special | place in local culture. The | benefits from tourism | reinforce positive attitudes | and empower people to be | gorilla protectors. | “The way a collaboration of | government, civil society, | and private sector is | approaching conservation | is an example to those | fighting to save other | species. We're bringing | people across borders to | focus on something positive | —and this is creating the | trust on which conservation | and prosperity can be built | in the region. | Conserving gorillas is | huge work and we simply | couldn't do it without the | supporters of FFI. | Fascinating facts about the mountain gorillas | Live in groups with a dominant | silverback male, several | females and their offspring. | Predominantly vegetarian, with | a mainly plant-based diet of | stems, shoots and fruit. | Attenborough and FFI to the rescue | You may remember that it was back in 1979 that Sir | David Attenborough opened the eyes of the world | to the plight of mountain gorillas in Rwanda. The | gorillas’ gentle interaction with Attenborough on | national television exposed the absurdity of their | fearsome reputation and endeared them not just | to viewers in the UK, but to a worldwide audience | of 500 million. Attenborough himself described the | experience as “one of the most exciting encounters | of my life”. | However, before the series was even aired, | Attenborough had received the news that poachers | had murdered a young silverback, Digit. His head | and hands had been chopped off - for a bounty | of just £10. As a passionate supporter of Fauna & | Flora International since the 1950s, Attenborough | felt compelled to act and, together, we continue | to collaborate with the Rwandan government to | protect these most impressive and peaceful of | creatures. You can be part of this historic effort | by supporting our work today. | Share over 98% of their DNA | with humans - they're one of ij | our closest genetic cousins. | Global briefing | A snapshot of eight of our 132 | Vietnam | Tonkin snub-nosed monkeys | critically endangered | First described in 1912, and | rediscovered in 1990, this black-and- | projects in more than 40 countries | around the world | Georgia | High alert for the Eurasian | sparrowhawk | At one of the world’s five main | migration spots for raptors, hunters | and falconers are involved in the | illegal killing and trade of many raptor | species. Illegal hunting affects many | endangered raptors such as steppe | eagles, while illegal trade threatens | the survival of species such as the | saker falcon, northern goshawk, | peregrine falcon and Eurasian | sparrowhawk. We are now working | closely with falconers’ associations | and government agencies to regulate | the practice of falconry and stop the | criminal international trade in birds of | prey for good. Your support can help us | urgently address these threats. | Tajikistan | Two species of pear tree | could soon die out | Unsustainable grazing, cutting and | firewood collection are dangerously | fragmenting Central Asia's precious | fruit-and-nut forests. These remote | forests have global significance as | genetic reservoirs, keeping alive | an astonishing array of biodiversity | elsewhere threatened by climate | change. Our project is focused | on two of the country’s three | most valuable forest sites, with | a rich variety of wild fruit and | nut trees (including two critically | endangered pear species) as well | as types of almond and apple | tree. Species like these could be | significant to our future agriculture. | Liberia | Protecting tropical | rainforests | of the world's great tropical | forest ecosystems. Diana | Liberia is home to much of the | Upper Guinean Forest - one | white monkey has a pink nose and lips | and blue patches around the eyes. | One of the rarest primates in the world, | the Tonkin snub-nosed monkey has | been at the heart of our work for over | a decade. Whilst the species is | Still listed as one of the 25 most | endangered primates in the world by | the IUCN Primate Specialist Group, | consistent protection by our partners | has seen the population in | Ha Giang increase more than double | to around 200 individuals. More work is | urgently needed to ensure this species | doesn't disappear. | St Vincent and | the Grenadines | Tiny lizard in jeopardy | | As tiny as a paperclip and | weighing less than a pinch of | salt, the Union Island gecko | was only discovered in 2005 — | monkeys, chimpanzees, pygmy | hippos and other endangered | species rely on this forest for | their survival. But agricultural | expansion, mining and poaching | all pose a risk. You can help stop | this destruction by supporting | training for the next generation | but, without immediate action, | could soon vanish. Named | after an island belonging to St | Vincent and the Grenadines, | it is not known to exist | anywhere else, but numbers | are rapidly declining because | of illegal trading. | Indonesia | Wild Sumatran tigers | down to just 400 | We are conserving tigers in | three Sumatran landscapes: | Aceh province, Riau province | and Kerinci Seblat National | Park. These forests contain | more than 60% of all wild | Sumatran tigers. Populations | are now stabilising in the | areas that our protection | teams regularly patrol. | Myanmar | Protecting karst | ecosystems | Quarrying, destructive | tourism, wildfires and | hunting pose major threats | to unique karst ecosystems | formed of rock worn away | by water to make caves and | towers. Without attention | to these habitats and the | species they harbour, | extinctions are inevitable. | of local conservationists. | South Africa | 6,300 unique floral | species on the brink | Many of the critical habitats | of the Cape flora are | severely threatened by | human development. We | are helping our partners | to protect fynbos habitats | from invasive alien | plants and uncontrolled | fires, while sustainably | harvesting the flowers. | Your monthly support is vital | For more than 100 years, our supporters have been the | absolute bedrock of our conservation projects across the | world. Today, we face the greatest challenge of all: to save | the planet from environmental devastation. | “Lam proud to have been a | member of Fauna & Flora | International since the | late 1950s. This admirable | organisation has kept its | 143/801 U10WN Aled 2ID3ID | To meet this epic challenge, we urgently need more people like | you to support FFI each month. More regular gifts will help us | plan ahead, and respond quickly to any sudden emergencies for | species and habitats. So you'll be helping us work efficiently and | making sure your money is used where it's needed most: today | 94p in every £1 you give is spent directly on conservation. | £20 a month | could help to bring more land under | the control of conservationists and | local communities, protecting the | fragile habitats vital to the mountain | gorilla and other species. | Yes, I will give a regular gift to FFI today - and promise | to protect wildlife and habitats from destruction | | would like to make a monthly contribution of: | [_]e2o [_Je10 [_les | OR my own choice of fl | Taken on the [ | 1st or|_| 15th of the month | PLEASE COMPLETE IN BLOCK CAPITALS | (We will not pass your details to anyone else) | objectives clear and focused, | and has achieved marvellous | Title: Forename: | Surname: | Home Address: | Postcode: | As anew regular donor you will receive a | supporter pack and our annual magazine. | We'll also keep you up to date with our progress, so | we'll send you our Update newsletter three times a | year, special event invites and sometimes ask for your | support when we need it. | £10 a month | could help us gather crucial | evidence to prosecute the criminal | traffickers responsible for the near | extinction of species such as the | Sunda pangolin. | things for conservation | throughout its history.” | Sir David Attenborough | £5 a month | could help equip our frontline | conservation teams to stop | poachers before they kill, | saving species like the | eastern black rhino. | Instruction to your Bank pirecr Make your gift go tenid | or Building society to pay Debit further atno site ma | by Direct Debit extra cost | The Government's Gift Aid scheme | Service User Number:_9 | 1| 6 [8] 9] 0] | Name(s) of Account Holder(s): | allows us to reclaim 25p of tax on every | £1 you give Fauna & Flora International — | at no extra cost to you. All you need to do | is tick below. | Ci ama uk taxpayer and would like to Gift | Aid all donations | have made to Fauna | & Flora International in the last four | years and all donations | make in the | future, until ! notify you otherwise.* | 1am nota UK taxpayer | co, SD | pounce | coco: EEE 2S | To: The Manager oe | Name and Address of Bank/Building Society: | pate O10 |[m[ mL] J | Our promise to you | You make our work possible — so we | want to make sure you feel part of | our team. | We will do this by getting in touch in | Postcode: | the way that suits you to tell you how | what you could help us acheive in | Instruction to your bank or building society: | Please pay FFI Direct Debits from the accounts | detailed in this Instruction subject to the safeguards | assured by the Direct Debit Guarantee. | understand that | this instruction may remain with FFI and, if so, details will | be passed electronicallyto my Bank/Building Society. | the future. | We want to honour your faith in | us —So we won't sell, share or trade | personal data. : | And you can change your choices at | any time by emailing | appeals@fauna-flora.org or calling | your support has had an impact and | If you don't want to hear from us by post, pl tick | this box] | If you would like to receive emails from us, please enter | your email address here: | Email: | Signature(s): | +44 (0)1223 749019. | For more details, find our published | Privacy Statement online: | www.fauna-flora.org/privacy- | statement | pate: 12 J[M| MJiLY | ¥ | | Please fill out and return to Freepost Plus RTKS-ZCXS-HSBT, Fauna & Flora International, | 126 Fairlie Road, Slough, SL1 4PY, UK | Alternatively, you can phone +44 (0) 1223 749019 or make your donation online at supportffi.org | Registered with | (FR) FUNDRAISING | R) REGULATOR | Registered Charity No. 1011102. | Registered company No. 2677068 | SDINS21-DDBIII2 | We are the world’s oldest and most effective conservation charity, established | in 1903, with expert local teams deployed in over 40 countries to protect our | planet. But the clock is ticking: the biodiversity on which all life depends is | being lost at 1,000 times the normal rate. Will you help us avert global disaster? | Saving endangered wildlife and | habitats across the world | “@.i | Will you fight | back against mass | extinction? | Turn to the form | on page 3 and | make a regular | donation today. | Mediterranean | EURASIA monk seal | This region holds many delicate | landscapes where achieving harmony | between people and nature is absolutely | vital. Illegal wildlife trade threatens the | saiga antelope. Unsustainable fishing | Thank you. | goo en threatens the Mediterranean monk | seal. And other animals like the | s grey wolf and brown bear are : | AMERICAS & @ losing their habitats. si | CARIBBEAN wey — | Ranging from rugged f : ‘ | Asian elephant | mountains to lush rainforests | and crystal-clear tropical | seas - a staggering array of | different landscapes and | species are found in this | region. Your support can | help in countries across | this diverse area, protecting | everything from tiny geckos | to mighty jaguars, and from | leatherback turtle nesting | sites on sandy beaches to | parrot nests in dense forests. | Northern white rhino | Sumatran tiger | Grauer's gorilla | AFRICA | You can support our teams | throughout Africa, home to | many iconic creatures such as | lions, rhinos and gorillas. With | your help, we can achieve so | Leatherback | turtle | ASIA-PACIFIC | much - protecting the threatened | rainforests of Liberia and | - Guinea; defending the savannah | grasslands of Kenya; saving | the coral reefs of Tanzania; and | sustaining the unique fynbos wild | Yellow naped A ; | parrot flower landscapes of South Africa. Fynbos wild flowers | Jaguar | image Credits: Zafer Kizilkaya, Jorge Martinez/FF1, Jeremy Holderv/FFI, Juan Pablo Moreiras/FFI, John Parker, Oi Pejeta Conservancy, Alison Motion, Nguyén Van Trudng/FFL Ondrej Prosicay/Adobe Stock, Gareth Golcthorpe/FFI | Many charismatic animals and plants are found in the Asia- ot | Pacific region, such as the Sumatran tiger, Malayan tapir, Asian Ld | elephant and Magnolia grandis. Together, we can safeguard | vital forest, marine and wetland habitats in Myanmar, protect | critically endangered creatures such as the Sunda pangolin from | traffickers in Indonesia and rescue primates like the Delacour's | langur from the brink of extinction.",Newspaper,NA,NA
245,"Dr Druha examines two-month-old | Muhammad Jamal, admitted tothe | paediatric ward of Ad-Dahirurat hospital | after suffering diarrhoea and vomiting. | ‘MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES | |DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | “After six years of conflict, the situation is desperate. Yemeni women | and children need our help now more than ever.’ | ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | The conflict in Yemen is now | in its sixth year. In Marib, in | central Yemen, nearly three | million people have been | displaced from their homes | and are living in makeshift | camps lose to the frontline. Pregnant women and | children are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, we found the local | hospitals overwhelmed by the sheer number of | people and by an influx of war-wounded. Nearly | 3 million people are in the province now, many | of them displaced from elsewhere. | People were living in makeshift shelters made | of plastic and metal sheets. There were no latrines, | just pits dug in the ground. People were worried | about their health, about the risk of disease, | about not having enough food. We were very | concerned about the health of mothers‘and children, | and pregnant women were also a major worry. | Over the next month, our small team worked | around the clock to set up clinics providing basic | healthcare and to supply hospitals that were | struggling. Getting medicines to this part of | ‘Pregnant women and | children are in desperate | need of medical care’ | Yemen wasn't easy. Supplies often had to cross | frontlines. One of our trucks carrying antibiotics, | medicines and other supplies was hijacked. It was | an incredibly difficult environment to work in. | Along with general healthcare, we provided | routine vaccinations for children and offered | antenatal and postnatal care. We saw a lot of | pregnant women and children and were able | to refer pregnant women with complications | to larger hospitals for emergency caesareans. | It made a real difference. | The day we started working in a rural hospital | near the frontline, a woman came in witha | breech delivery who was having real difficulty. | Our midwife assisted and everything went well. | But who knows what would have happened if | the hospital wasn’t there. | AN URGENT SITUATION | Since I left, many more people have come to the | area. But Marib is no longer a safe place to be. In | recent weeks, the frontlines have moved into the | province, causing large numbers of injuries and | forcing thousands more people to flee their homes. | After six years of conflict, the situation is | desperate. These people need our help now | more than ever. | Thank you.” | DONATE TO SUPPORT OUR MEDICAL TEAMS | CALL | SEARCH! | 100661",Newspaper,NA,NA
246,"es —- | MOY} 3SNf },uOp | SSPSId ""SPISUI | 49319] JOSPTOAT | ue S,d10U | | ny XX | ‘\ YY | AY AY | +. ) | = eS | SSeS os | =. SS ee SSeS | The power | of kindness | To donate: | Call 0300 023 0827 | Visit redcross.org.uk/newfund | Text FUND to 70141 to make a £10 donation | By texting you consent to future telephone & SMS marketing contact from British Red Cross. | Text FUND NO to 70141 to give £10 without consenting to calls & texts | Texts cost £10 plus 1 standard message (we receive 100%). For full T&Cs visit redcross.org.uk/mobile | The British Red Cross Society, incorporated by Royal Charter 1908, is a charity registered in England and OQ recycle | Wales (220949), Scotland (SC037738), Isle of Man (0752) and Jersey (430). When you have finished | with this please recycle it | Return address: British Red Cross, Bumpers Way, Bumpers Farm, CHIPPENHAM SN14 6NG | The power | of kindne | No need to post, there are many ways that you can donate: | ( Call: 0300 0230827. [J Visit: redcross.org.uk/newfund | [] Text FUND to 70141 to make a £10 donation | By texting you consent to future telephone & SMS marketing contact | from British Red Cross. Text FUND NO to give £10 without consenting to | Calls & texts. Texts cost £10 plus 1 standard message (we receive 100%). | For full T&Cs visit redcross.org.uk/mobile | The British Red Cross Society, incorporated by Royal Charter 1908, is a charity registered in England and Wales (220949), Scotland (SC037738), Isle of Man (0752) and Jersey (430). | Please affix | postage stamp | | A BritishRedCross ecineon, | ne Bt | Freepost Plus RUCB-JCXU-SRSJ | ROOM Q2SI21 | British Red Cross | Bumpers Way | Bumpers Farm | CHIPPENHAM | SN14 6NG } | ATT y pee eT LT aT | UK Office | 44 Moorfields | BritishRedCross Sea | 0300 456 11 55 | redcross.org.uk | The British Red Cross Society, | incorporated by Royal Charter | 1908, is a charity registered in | England and Wales (220949), | People are in danger. oo Men O62) ard Jetty | But we have a plan. | This is the response of our lifetime. | Dear reader, | I’m sure you'll agree that the recent news coverage from around the world has been bleak. My teams and | are | deeply concerned by what we’re hearing from our colleagues across the global Red Cross movement, too. | Today | wanted to tell you about the new international fund we’ve launched, dedicated to supporting | people overseas, and ask if you can help. | Every year since 1999, extreme poverty levels globally have fallen — but that changed last year. The World Bank | estimates that somewhere between 119-124 million people fell into extreme poverty last year. | That’s 20 years of progress, of communities lifting themselves out of poverty, damaged by the pandemic. It’s clear to | us that we need to act, now. | We've set up a new international fund specifically to reach people whose lives have changed for the worse. Their | reality is a stark contrast to ours here in the UK — because despite the pain of last year, our nation’s vaccine rollout is | going well, and things are opening up. | However, that’s not the case for millions of people worldwide. It’s far from it. That’s why we’re asking if you can | support our new fund with a donation today. | Take the communities we work with in Cox’s Bazar in Bangladesh, for example, who rely on busy trading to earn | money and provide for their families. Worryingly, there’s been more than a 60% drop in income for people there, | due to factors out of their control (travel restrictions, supply chain disruptions, and limited buyers in the market, to | name a few). | For them, there is no plan B. Their livelinoods have been crushed by Covid-19 — causing a spike in food insecurity | (which could turn into a food crisis, if nothing changes). | continued overleaf | Please pledge whatever you can. | | would like to make a donation of: | Gift Aid allows us to claim back ‘ . d ue | @ £5 [| £10 [ | £25 fil £50  Mychoice [fe si 25p of tax for every £1 you give, J (JUM | making your donation go further | Be | enclose a cheque or postal order, made payable to ‘British Red Cross’ OR | am a UK taxpayer and | would like the British Red Cross | Sudasay tue dri amickyean | [__] Please debit my Credit / Debit or CAF Card with the amount specified si rwice Pecan cae | | understand that if | pay less income / capital gains tax | Card No. | | | | | | | | | | than the amount of gift aid claimed on all my donations | in the tax year in which they are received, it is my | ; responsibility to pay any difference. Please remember to Oo | Expiry ae / [ [| let us know of any changes to your tax status including rat | Date changes to your name or address or if you need to 3s | Title (please tick): MrL_] mrsl_] missL_] msL_] other[ ep ee eegeenet = | Name Surname Keeping in touch | fckkoss We're so grateful for your support, thank you. As part | of the Red Cross, we write to you to tell you about Q | Postcode how your gifts help transform lives and how you can 2 | donate and help in other ways, as well as to update = | Telephone* you about our news and services. If you would rather | 8 | Email"" we didn’t communicate with you in this way, you = | can change the way we contact you at any time by BY | “Please only give us your teleohone number/email address if you are happy for us to visiting redcross.org.uk/keepingintouch, e-mailing Q | contact you in this way with updates about how your gifts are helping and how you can mailenquiries@redcross.org.uk writing to FREEPOST = | donate and help in other ways. RED CROSS or calling Freephone 0800 2 800 491. : | We’re seeing a similar dangerous pattern across the world. So that’s why we created this fund - to reach | individuals, families and communities who urgently need help, wherever they are. | Our resilience project in Rakhine, Myanmar, has been running since 2017, and | think it’s another example worth | sharing with you. People face crises on top of crises - climate change and ongoing conflict has led to more frequent | cyclones and floods, household providers losing livelihoods, displacement, and limited access to healthcare. | As |’m sure you'd expect, adding Covid-19 to that list has hugely intensified the situation. People who were | vulnerable before the pandemic are now in an even more fragile position, and our help has never been more critical. | Communities like those in Rakhine need support that’s tailored to these intense, complex needs — that takes into | account every dimension of each crisis they’re facing. | The Red Cross is the organisation that can give that support. But we need your help to deliver it. | We were made for times like these. For context, in 191 countries, you’ll see a Red Cross — a team of dedicated staff | and volunteers, who work alongside vulnerable communities and know exactly what they need. | That’s why our programmes really do work. We’re already part of the communities we help. | To take you back to Cox’s Bazar — before the pandemic, we’d been working with some particularly vulnerable | groups there, to help them become more financially resilient. And in the wake of coronavirus, our reports show that | those groups’ income had dropped less than others who hadn’t had that vital training. Ultimately, they were | more prepared for the worst. | With your help, we can continue to make a real impact and strengthen our programmes around the world, to reach | people whose lives depend on it. | These are people who have no safety net, nothing to fall back on, no savings to see them through. People who may | care for children or elderly relatives, who have no idea how they'll get through the week, who need a lifeline more | than ever right now. | So please donate to our international fund if you can. | | Know you might not have expected my letter to (quite literally) fall into your lap today. | know how easy it is to feel | small when the problem is so big. | know you might be thinking: “there’s no way | can make a difference.” | But, if you’re still reading this letter, | also know that’s simply not true. | The only way we can tackle this is by coming together, taking action, and doing all we can to show that we are not | willing to leave anyone behind, no matter where they are. | It’ll be the response of our lifetime. But it’s what we do, and | hope you'll be a part of it. | Thank you for reading, | Jams Lo | Inmaculada Lopez De La Cova Pena | Director of Programmes and Partnerships, British Red Cross | The power | of Kindness | P.S. | recognise that my letter may have landed at a hard time for you. If you need help from a friendly Red Crosser, | call our support line on 0808 196 3651. It’s open every day between 10am and 6pm. | ie BritishRedCross | No need to post, there are many ways that you can donate: | \, Call: 0300 0230827 [J Visit: redcross.org.uk/newfund | 0 Text FUND to 70141 to make a £10 donation | By texting you consent to future telephone & SMS marketing contact | from British Red Cross. Text FUND NO to give £10 without consenting | to calls & texts. Texts cost £10 plus 1 standard message | (we receive 100%). For full T&Cs visit redcross.org.uk/mobile | The British Red Cross will use your donation to support our work overseas, wherever the | need is greatest. | The British Red Cross Society, incorporated by Royal Charter 1908, is a charity registered in | England and Wales (220949), Scotland (SC037738), Isle of Man (0752) and Jersey (430). | Registered with | (FR) FUNDRAISING | REGULATOR | Your Privacy | The British Red Cross is committed to privacy and | will use personal data for the purpose it was collected | or other legitimate purposes we tell you about: for | example, to provide goods, services or information you | have requested or to administer donations or services | we provide. We may also analyse data we collect | to better understand the people who support us or | those who use or deliver our services. Sometimes this | means us combining that data with information from | reliable public sources. Our research allows us to tailor | communications and services in a more focused and | cost effective way, as well as better meeting your needs | and the needs of others like you. However, we will never | do this in a way that intrudes on personal privacy and | will not use your data for a purpose that conflicts with | previously expressed privacy preferences. For full details | about how we use personal data, our legal basis for | doing so and your privacy rights, please see our privacy | notice online at redcross.org.uk/privacy",Newspaper,NA,NA
