ns WE’RE PROUD OF HOW WE SPEND | YOUR CONTRIBUTIONS | camp | (MSF UK Annual Report 2019) | Bentiu Protection of Civilians camp | 85% 13% 2% | ON OUR HUMANITARIAN WORK one Oe ON OFFICE MANAGEMENT | 100,000 people who have sought ) | £8.24 FOR EVERY | refuge from conflict. Residents live in | temporary shelters made of corrugated | metal, plant stalks, mud and plastic | sheeting. These offer limited protection | from the elements, especially during | the rainy season when walkways | become lanes of mud. People's medical | needs are enormous. | MSF operates the main hospital in the | AMBULANCE | l Helmand . | Photograph © Avra Photograph © Andrew Quilty | UN-run camp, which provides general Fiatas/MSF / | medical care, surgery, treatment for malaria, | malnutrition and diseases linked to poor | | water and sanitation, along with 1 ~~. sessennecane SOUTH SUDAN | for tuberculosis and HIV. MSF physiotherapist Birgit | “MSF is operating one of the only hospitals five-year-old |r saitdios | that has surgical services — not only in Bentiu, him regain the use of his leg | but in the wider region,” says MSF surgeon after he was bitten by a snake | John Buckles, who has recurnedto Bentiu 20 | lll ik lc ON lle 6=—li(ii llc tw I §=— “You see people getbetterandyou'rs © __ereeeeneneeneneenenennecnnatennennenennee tmmR RRR RR REE while playing eutelde his house, | x + gg Photograph © Damaris Giullana/MSF | five times to conduct surgeries. “Working | here, you're faced with a broad spectrum | of conditions, ranging from tropical diseases | to surgical conditions that occur in any | society where there are abdominal issues, | through to emergency caesareans, All of | these conditions have continued during | the COVID-19 pandemic.” | <a : | <—- patients. But what strikes you in a | ir Z| like Bentiu is that the work itself“ | —_ Place | __-_ isn't replaceable. If MSF wasn't there, _ | thousands of people would be without | — | the medical care and lifesaving | MOZAMBIQUE | : check for malnutrition | Each ward of the 120-bed 4 t ss surgery that they need. ne | hospital is housed in a white “From my point of view as a surgeon, ‘ | tent connected by covered q “— the hospital is well planned, with the zz no be | watkways. “I was working sa ""operating theatres close to the a tn a have sought refuge from | 12 to 15 hours a day for seven maternity ward, the emergency room ~<A Serge nee | weeks, doing an average of and the surgical ward. It's an enormous a“ neo | eight surgeries every day, nar | improvement from when | first went to | Bentiu and operated in a tent with a —S | Photograph © MSF | along with a range of local Er | anaesthetic procedures,” says 71 | < small air-conditioning unit dripping onto | Buckles. “I lost count of the “Se the floor. | was very happy with the | number of gunshot wounds 3 surgical outcomes this latest trip. We | we treated.” — ‘ had a lot of very sick patients, but we | rs ZA didn't lose any of them."" | pen Baia ~ : | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE",Newspaper,NA,NA
248,"Help us create | ne es | n Yorkshire | Help.us: transform a bare landscape | crying out for the life trees bring | Please spare a few minutes to read about Snaizeholme, our urgent appeal to | raise funds to buy it, and the amazing rare and threatened wildlife we hope | will make it their home. | Snaizeholme is in the Yorkshire Dales, close to the famous Ribblehead Viaduct. | Right now it’s bleak, windswept and bare - a 550 acre landscape crying out for | the life, colour and beauty trees bring. | And that’s where we come in. You’ve probably heard of the Woodland Trust, | but maybe you don’t know how we are creating new havens for wildlife across | the UK, through protecting and planting woods and trees, and letting them | grow naturally. | As the UK’s largest woodland conservation charity, we know how vital trees | are for our landscapes and our lives. We desperately want to be able to buy | Snaizeholme and transform it with trees into a thriving nature habitat. | Because waiting for us nearby is someone very, very special. | As well as a host of other important wildlife, this part of Yorkshire is one of | the few places in England where the UK’s native red squirrel can be found. By | boosting biodiversity and getting trees in the ground at Snaizeholme, we hope | the rare and precious red squirrel will take up residence there, expanding its | range and hopefully breeding and thriving. | This is an incredible opportunity to boost biodiversity at a time when the | natural world is in crisis. Imagine how wonderful it will be to see woodpeckers | nesting in the native trees we will plant, black grouse on patrol, otters, herons | and kingfishers along the streams and of course, red squirrels darting along | branches high up in the canopy. | There isn’t a moment to lose. We must raise | £3.5 million to buy Snaizeholme and fund | the regeneration and planting the land needs. | Mi | Donations can be made using the | attached form. Or donate online and | see Snaizeholme, plus the wildlife we | want to help in our inspiring short film at | woodlandtrust.org.uk/snaizeholmeappeal. | Every pound you give brings us closer to | buying Snaizehome. | Thank you | For more information about this appeal please email supporters@woodlandtrust.org.uk | 12/90 6S000 S888E0DS ‘ou pud},09g UI pub HHEYEZ ‘OU | $2]0/M pud punj6u3 u! peseysiBau Aiquoyo d si snl puD]poo/y ay | 119 TEON | weyjueuy | Aem uoidwe»y | 3snuL pue,LpoomM sYyL | Leeddy awj_oyezLeus | XCSS-NSUC-VHLY JSOdeeu4 | isnul | GNV1IGOOM | S @.2 0 | eag¢2 88 | 9£°386 | 8633528 | 8&2 35280 | EV 2oR | al @ x | Yes, | want to help boost the UR’s red squirrel population | and help other threatened wildlife in Yorkshire | Please fill in your details below | Title First name | Last name | Address | Postcode | Email address | Daytime Tel No. | The return of the'red | The red squirrel is the UK’s only native squirrel | yet has undergone one of the most drastic | declines of all our mammals - largely due to | the introduction of non-native grey squirrels | inthe early 20th century. The larger greys | out-compete reds, driving them out of their | preferred native broadleaf woodland habitat. | Greys also transmit the deadly squirrelpox | virus which they are immune to, but which | proves fatal to the native red. | Today fewer than 300,000 red squirrels | remain in Great Britain, in isolated woodland | pockets - like woods near Snaizeholme. The | Woodland Trust is working hard to save the | red squirrel by protecting and expanding its | habitat - just as we hope to do, with your | help, at Snaizeholme. | You can also donate online at woodlandtrust.org.uk/snaizeholmeappeal | Registered with | (FR) FUNDRAISING | REGULATOR | Let’s keep in touch! | Without our supporters we wouldn't be able to stand up for woods and trees, and we'd love to | keep in touch with you about the work supporter donations help us achieve. | You'll be kept up to date with our latest news, products, and activities, and hear about more | ways you can get involved such as campaigning, fundraising and events. | We may contact you by post or phone about things we believe will be of interest to you, unless you | tell us not to. | Please let us know below how you're happy to hear from us: | [] Yes please, I’d like [| No thank you, I’d [ | No thank you, I’d | to hear from you prefer not to hear prefer not to hear | by email from you by post from you by phone | You can unsubscribe at any time. Just visit our online permission portal - | woodlandtrust.yourpreferencecentre.com, call 0330 333 3300, or | ernail supporters@woodlandtrust.org.uk. We promise to keep your details safe; | our online Privacy Policy - woodlandtrust.org.uk/privacy-policy - explains all. CS22 | : ‘d it> Make a 25% bigger difference to woodland | Gg! fear A chit aid | Boost your donation by 25p of Gift Aid for every £1 you donate. The Woodland Trust can reclaim the | tax you pay for the current tax year. | L] YES. | confirm that | am a UK taxpayer and | want to Gift Aid my donation, and any donations | | make in the future, or have made in the past 4 years to the Woodland Trust. If | pay less Income Tax and/ | or Capital Gains Tax than the amount of Gift Aid claimed on all my donations in that tax year, it is my | responsibility to pay any difference. Please notify us if you want to cancel this declaration, change your | name or home address, or, if you no longer pay sufficient tax on your income and/or capital gains. | L_] Please tick if you are not a UK taxpayer. | | would like to support the | Woodland Trust’s Snaizeholme Appeal with my gift of: £ L U L L O , O O | Date: L L LU L L a | | enclose a: LJ cheque/postal order made payable to The Woodland Trust. | For cheques please add your account number: U U L U U L L LU | Visa: LO Mastercard: L] Maestro: LJ Amex: L] | ate” OO) O00 smber‘ JOOOOOO0 00000000 | Signed: | A21NP1123 | ’ OR Please debit my Credit or Debit Card: LJ | Tree-planting will improve the | water quality and provide shade at | Snaizeholme, helping the resident | otters and white-clawed crayfish. | Nature is in crisis. | But there is a way you can help... | As well as the devastating effects of | climate change we are facing a global | biodiversity crisis. | Here in the UK woodland wildlife is falling | at an alarming rate and just 7% of native | woods are ina healthy condition. | <CAURIE CAMPBELL/WTML | As a conservation charity we are extremely | concerned over what the future holds for | trees, woods and wildlife if urgent action | isn't taken. Birds like the | lesser redpoll | We protect and restore woods all over the can thrive | UK and create new woodland where it is | needed most, giving threatened species the | chance to survive and thrive. | We can really make a difference to the land | at Snaigeholme, showcasing what we do to | boost nature by getting native trees in the | ground and letting them grow naturally. | There are many wildlife species who will | benefit from what we hope to achieve there, | including of course rare native red squirrels. | Please donate to our urgent appeal | to buy Snaizeholme. | Fill in the attached form or go online to | woodlandtrust.org.uk/snaizeholmeappeal. | Time is running out and your | support is vital. | Kempton Way, Grantham, | Lincolnshire NG31 6LL | in the mixed | woodland we | will create at | Snaizeholme.7 | Woodland birds such | as the lesser spotted | woodpecker (pictured) have | suffered declines of more | than 80% since the 1970s. | Upland scrub woodland will | make an ideal habitat to entice | black grouse from the fringes. | The Woodland Trust is a charity registered | | in England and Wales (No. 294344) and in | Scotland (No. SCO38885). | Map © Crown Copyright 2021. All rights | WO O D L AN D reserved. Ordnance Survey Licence number | 100021607. Cover: Aileen Louden/WTML. | TRUST 6/21 00059",Newspaper,NA,NA
249,"EDICA | YEMEN EMERGENCY APPEAL | ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | MSF NURSE | The conflict in Yemen is now | in its sixth year. In Marib, in | central Yemen, nearly three | million people have been | displaced from their homes and | are living in makeshift camps | close to the frontline. Pregnant women and ence | are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, we found the local | hospitals overwhelmed by the sheer number of people | and by an influx of war-wounded. Nearly 3 million | people are in the province now, many of them | displaced from elsewhere. | People were living in makeshift shelters made of | plastic and metal sheets. There were no latrines, just | pits dug in the ground. People were worried about | their health, about the risk of disease, about not | having enough food. We were very concerned about | the health of mothers and children, and pregnant | women were also a major worry. | Over the next month, our small team worked | around the clock to:set up clinics providing basic | healthcare and to supply hospitals that were - | struggling. Getting medicines to this part of Yemen | wasn’t easy. Supplies often had to cross frontlines. | Be MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES | DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | 'PREGNANT | WOMEN AND | CHILDREN ARE | IN DESPERATE | Or Druha examines two-month-old | Muhammad Jamal, admitted to the | paediatric ward of Ad-Dahi rurat hospital | after suffering diarrhoea and vomiting. | One of our trucks carrying antibiotics, medicines | and other supplies was hijacked. It was an incredibly | difficult environment to work in. | Along with general healthcare, we provided | routine vaccinations for children and offered | antenatal and postnatal care. We saw a lot of pregnant | women and children and were able to refer pregnant | women with complications to larger hospitals for | emergency caesareans. It made a real difference. | The day we started working in a rural hospital | near the frontline, a woman came in with a breech | delivery who was having real difficulty. Our | midwife assisted and everything went well. But | who knows what would have happened if the | hospital wasn’t there. | AN URGENT SITUATION | Since I left, many more people have come to | the area. But Marib is no longer a safe place to | be. In recent weeks, the frontlines have moved | into the province, causing large numbers of | injuries and forcing thousands more people | to flee theirhomes. | After six years of conflict, the situation is | desperate. These people need our help now | more than ever. | Thank you.” | _ could provide an oxygen kit to help care for | DONATE NOW | TO SUPPORT OUR | MEDICAL TEAMS | could pay for three safe delivery kits T @ fj | to assist women giving birth | £130 | premature babies with underdeveloped lungs | £228 | could cover the the cost of a midwife working | in afield hospital for three days | CALL 0800 0557973 coi | SEARCH MSFMEDIC 4 | OR SCAN TO DONATE | 100668",Newspaper,NA,NA
250,"— == | <= — | Se, — — = | SS Se CS = zs | SSS SSS ae SOS OSS See Ss aoes SSS Ses = | | SS = SS SS Sse esses | et = | SSS oS ee oe | YY ft | Ry nine HY | Hl +4, i} | AY + | S25 SSCS | Sasses | Wik ‘ | + +4 | << So | There’s an | important letter | inside. Please | don’t just throw | it away. | The power | of kindness | To donate: | Call O800 023 0827 | Visit redcross.org.uk/newfund | Text FUND to 70141 to make a £10 donation | By texting you consent to future telephone & SMS marketing contact from British Red Cross. | Text FUND NO to 70141 to give £10 without consenting to calls & texts | Texts cost £10 plus 1 standard message (we receive 100%). For full T&Cs visit redcross.org.uk/mobile | The British Red Cross Society, incorporated by Royal Charter 1908, is a charity registered in England and Q recycle | Wales (220949), Scotland (SC037738), Isle of Man (0752) and Jersey (430). When you have finished | with this please recycle it | Return address: British Red Cross, Bumpers Way, Bumpers Farm, CHIPPENHAM SN14 6NG | The power | drekncetierers roa nlite ntti | No need to post, there are many ways that you can donate: | ( Call: 0300 0230827. LI Visit: redcross.org.uk/newfund | i Text FUND to 70141 to make a £10 donation | By texting you consent to future telephone & SMS marketing contact | from British Red Cross. Text FUND NO to give £10 without consenting to | calls & texts. Texts cost £10 plus 1 standard message (we receive 100%). | For full T&Cs visit redcross.org.uk/mobile | The British Red Cross Society, incorporated by Royal Charter 1908, is a charity registered in England and Wales (220949), Scotland (SC037738), Isle of Man (0752) and Jersey (430). | Please affix | postage stamp | | vi BritishRedCross ea | oe |B 0 | | Freepost Plus RUCB-JCXU-SRSJ | ROOM Q2SI21 | British Red Cross | Bumpers Way | Bumpers Farm | CHIPPENHAM | SN14 6NG | | TT lw eos ear La TT | UK Office | 44 Moorfields | BritishRedCross ay | 0300 456 11 55 | redcross.org.uk | The British Red Cross Society, | incorporated by Royal Charter | 1908, is a charity registered in | England and Wales (220949), | People are in danger. of Men 0762) eri Jersey (A | But we have a plan. | This is the response of our lifetime. | Dear reader, | I’m sure you’ll agree that the recent news coverage from around the world has been bleak. My teams and | are | deeply concerned by what we’re hearing from our colleagues across the global Red Cross movement, too. | Today | wanted to tell you about the new international fund we’ve launched, dedicated to supporting | people overseas, and ask if you can help. | Every year since 1999, extreme poverty levels globally have fallen — but that changed last year. The World Bank | estimates that somewhere between 119-124 million people fell into extreme poverty last year. | That’s 20 years of progress, of communities lifting themselves out of poverty, damaged by the pandemic. It’s clear to | us that we need to act, now. | We've set up a new international fund specifically to reach people whose lives have changed for the worse. Their | reality is a stark contrast to ours here in the UK — because despite the pain of last year, our nation’s vaccine rollout is | going well, and things are opening up. | However, that’s not the case for millions of people worldwide. It’s far from it. That’s why we’re asking if you can | support our new fund with a donation today. | Take the communities we work with in Cox’s Bazar in Bangladesh, for example, who rely on busy trading to earn | money and provide for their families. Worryingly, there’s been more than a 60% drop in income for people there, | due to factors out of their control (travel restrictions, supply chain disruptions, and limited buyers in the market, to | name a few). | For them, there is no plan B. Their livelinoods have been crushed by Covid-19 — causing a spike in food insecurity | (which could turn into a food crisis, if nothing changes). | continued overleaf | Please pledge whatever you can. | | would like to make a donation of: | Gift Aid allows us to claim back ‘ . d uw | C] £5 I £10 C] £25 Hq £50  Mychoice ha 25p of tax for every £1 you give, I (JUM | making your donation go further | [ | | enclose a cheque or postal order, made payable to ‘British Red Cross’ OR | am a UK taxpayer and | would like the British Red Cross | to reclaim the tax on all qualifying donations | have made, | [ ] Please debit my Credit / Debit or CAF Card with the amount specified as well as any future donations, until | notify them | otherwise. Please tick to confirm | | understand that if | pay less income / capital gains tax | eee OEE ta ee ice a ae | : responsibility to pay any difference. Please remember to ow | Expiry [ L] LL | let us know of any changes to your tax status including 3a | Date changes to your name or address or if you need to x | Title (please tick): MrL_] mrsL_] missL_] Msl_] other | ac 3 | Name Surname Keeping in touch | Adchoss We're so grateful for your support, thank you. As part | of the Red Cross, we write to you to tell you about Q | Postcode how your gifts help transform lives and how you can 2 | donate and help in other ways, as well as to update = | Teeprens"" you about our news and services. If you would rather | & | Email* we didn’t communicate with you in this way, you = | can change the way we contact you at any time by e | “Please only give us your teleohone number/email address if you are happy for us to visiting redcross.org.uk/keepingintouch, e-mailing B | contact you in this way with updates about how your gifts are helping and how you can mailenquiries@redcross.org.uk writing to FREERPOST = | donate and help in other ways. RED CROSS or calling Freephone 0800 2 800 491. = | We’re seeing a similar dangerous pattern across the world. So that’s why we created this fund - to reach | individuals, families and communities who urgently need help, wherever they are. | Our resilience project in Rakhine, Myanmar, has been running since 2017, and | think it’s another example worth | sharing with you. People face crises on top of crises - climate change and ongoing conflict has led to more frequent | cyclones and floods, household providers losing livelihoods, displacement, and limited access to healthcare. | As |’m sure you'd expect, adding Covid-19 to that list has hugely intensified the situation. People who were | vulnerable before the pandemic are now in an even more fragile position, and our help has never been more critical. | Communities like those in Rakhine need support that’s tailored to these intense, complex needs — that takes into | account every dimension of each crisis they’re facing. | The Red Cross is the organisation that can give that support. But we need your help to deliver it. | We were made for times like these. For context, in 191 countries, you’ll see a Red Cross — a team of dedicated staff | and volunteers, who work alongside vulnerable communities and know exactly what they need. | That’s why our programmes really do work. We’re already part of the communities we help. | To take you back to Cox’s Bazar — before the pandemic, we’d been working with some particularly vulnerable | groups there, to help them become more financially resilient. And in the wake of coronavirus, our reports show that | those groups’ income had dropped less than others who hadn’t had that vital training. Ultimately, they were | more prepared for the worst. | With your help, we can continue to make a real impact and strengthen our programmes around the world, to reach | people whose lives depend on it. | These are people who have no safety net, nothing to fall back on, no savings to see them through. People who may | care for children or elderly relatives, who have no idea how they’ll get through the week, who need a lifeline more | than ever right now. | So please donate to our international fund if you can. | | know you might not have expected my letter to (quite literally) fall into your lap today. | know how easy it is to feel | small when the problem is so big. | know you might be thinking: “there’s no way | can make a difference.” | But, if you’re still reading this letter, | also know that’s simply not true. | The only way we can tackle this is by coming together, taking action, and doing all we can to show that we are not | willing to leave anyone behind, no matter where they are. | It’ll be the response of our lifetime. But it's what we do, and | hope you’ll be a part of it. | Thank you for reading, | Jams Lo | Inmaculada Lopez De La Cova Pena | Director of Programmes and Partnerships, British Red Cross | The power | of kindness | P.S. | recognise that my letter may have landed at a hard time for you. If you need help from a friendly Red Crosser, | call our support line on 0808 196 3651. It’s open every day between 10am and 6pm. | mie BritishRedCross | No need to post, there are many ways that you can donate: | \, Call: 0300 0230827 [J Visit: redcross.org.uk/newfund | (] Text FUND to 70141 to make a £10 donation | By texting you consent to future telephone & SMS marketing contact | from British Red Cross. Text FUND NO to give £10 without consenting | to calls & texts. Texts cost £10 plus 1 standard message | (we receive 100%). For full T&Cs visit redcross.org.uk/mobile | The British Red Cross will use your donation to support our work overseas, wherever the | need is greatest. | The British Red Cross Society, incorporated by Royal Charter 1908, is a charity registered in | England and Wales (220949), Scotland (SC037738), Isle of Man (0752) and Jersey (430). | Registered with | (FR) FUNDRAISING | REGULATOR | Your Privacy | The British Red Cross is committed to privacy and | will use personal data for the purpose it was collected | or other legitimate purposes we tell you about: for | example, to provide goods, services or information you | have requested or to administer donations or services | we provide. We may also analyse data we collect | to better understand the people who support us or | those who use or deliver our services. Sometimes this | means us combining that data with information from | reliable public sources. Our research allows us to tailor | communications and services in a more focused and | cost effective way, as well as better meeting your needs | and the needs of others like you. However, we will never | do this in a way that intrudes on personal privacy and | will not use your data for a purpose that conflicts with | previously expressed privacy preferences. For full details | about how we use personal data, our legal basis for | doing so and your privacy rights, please see our privacy | notice online at redcross.org.uk/privacy",Newspaper,NA,NA
