267,"= os | a me hs | 9 10d M | XNE Of | DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | YEMEN EMERGENCY APPEAL | ALISON CRIADO-PEREZ | MSF NURSE | The conflict in Yemen is now | in its sixth year. In Marib, in | central Yemen, nearly three | million people have been | displaced from their homes and | are living in makeshift camps | close to the frontline. Pregnant women and children | are in desperate need of medical care. | “When we arrived in Marib, we found the local | hospitals overwhelmed by the sheer number of people | and by an influx of war-wounded. Nearly 3 million | people are in the province now, many of them | displaced from elsewhere. | People were living in makeshift shelters made of | plastic and metal sheets. There were no latrines, just | pits dug in the ground. People were worried about | their health, about the risk of disease, about not | having enough food. We were very concerned about | the health of mothers and children, and pregnant | - women were also a major worry. | Over the next month, our small team worked | around the clock to set up clinics providing basic | healthcare and to supply hospitals that were | struggling. Getting medicines to this part of Yemen | wasn't easy. Supplies often had to cross frontlines. | 4 MEDECINS SANS:-FRONTIERES | | ‘PREGNANT © | WOMEN AND | CHILDREN ARE / | SIN DESPERATE | Or Oruha examines two-month-old | Muhammad Jamal, admitted to the | paediatric ward of Ad-Dahi rural hospital | after suffering diarrhoea and vomiting. | One of our trucks carrying antibiotics, medicines | and other supplies was hijacked. It was an incredibly | difficult environment to work in. | Along with general healthcare, we provided | routine vaccinations for children and offered | antenatal and postnatal care. We saw a lot of pregnant | women and children and were able to refer pregnant | women with complications to larger hospitals for | emergency caesareans. It made a real difference. | The day we started working in a rural hospital | near the frontline, a woman came in with a breech | delivery who was having real difficulty. Our | midwife assisted and everything went well. But | who knows what would have happened if the | hospital wasn’t there. | AN URGENT SITUATION | Since I left, many more people have come to | the area. But Marib is no longer a safe place to | be. In recent weeks, the frontlines have moved | into the province, causing large numbers of | injuries and forcing thousands more people | to flee their homes. | After six years of conflict, the situation is | desperate. These people need our help now | more than ever. | Thank you.” | DONATE NOW | TO SUPPORT OUR | MEDICAL TEAMS | could pay for three safe delivery kits | to assist women giving birth | £130 | could provide an oxygen kit to help care for @ | premature babies with underdeveloped lungs | could cover the the cost of a midwife working | (6s) | in afield hospital forthree days | CALL 0800 055 79 73 Se | SEARCH MSF MEDIC : | OR SCAN TO DONATE | 100669",Newspaper,NA,NA
268,"Sightsavers aes a 4 Sightsavers | ""4 é | Nalukena g Nalukena Geise¥ | SJAADS]YBIS | nvowwn | 2z2acc- | =a 33 0 | SFvuv0uvT Ss | UD © ® et | anor 38 | 239"" 4 ® | oz < | on =o | soo 5 | Will you | help protect | children nding, | Se Just £5 from you r , | could save her sight. | ~ Just £5 from you! | could save her sight. | www.sightsavers.org/closer | www.sightsavers.org/< os www.sightsavers.org/closer | Trachoma steals sight with every blink... | Trachoma is an infection that’s both agonising, and blinding. | At first, it makes your eyes itchy and sore. But if left | untreated, it develops into the more severe trichiasis - | which steals your sight blink by painful blink... | Nalukena had suffered from trachoma since birth - and | at just two years old, it was clear she was in real pain. | Her devastated parents did all they could to help their | little girl, but they didn't understand what was wrong. | They just knew it was getting worse. | Without help, little Nalukena was at risk of losing her | sight altogether. | You can save children from a life of pain | That’s why your support today is so vital. Together, we | can reach a whole class of children like Nalukena with | sight-saving treatment - and one day eliminate this cruel | infection for good. | From painful trachoma... | £5 could protect a class of children | Thankfully, we could help Nalukena with an antibiotic called | Zithromax® - which costs just 15p per treatment, and has the | power to stop trachoma in its tracks. | Now, Nalukena no longer has to live in pain, and can look forward | to a happy future. But there are still millions of children across | Africa who are moving closer to blindness each day. It’s vital | we can treat them, before they lose their sight forever. | Your gift will save children’s sight | Just £5 from you is all it takes to provide Zithromax” for | a class of 33 children - saving them from blindness, and | moving us closer to our goal of eliminating trachoma by 2025. | To