the world’s leading emergency medical humanitarian aid organisation. We help people affected by armed hospital wasn't there. MSF | conflict, epidemics and natural or manmade disasters, without discrimination and irrespective of race, religion, creed, gender or political affiliation. We work in more | than 70 countries and go to places where others cannot or choose not to go. We can do this because we are independently funded, | with 99.8 per cent of our funding coming from individual or private donors, like you. \ ‘ PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT | | AROUND THE WORLD | Around the | world with MSF | WE’RE PROUD OF HOW WE SPEND | YOUR CONTRIBUTIONS | 86% 12% | ON OUR HUMANITARIAN WORK ON FUNDRAISING (WE RAISE ON OFFICE MANAGEMENT | £8.11 FOR EVERY £1 WE SPEND) | CENTRAL AFRICAN | REPUBLIC | “a France Beldo is brought to the | “@ recovery room after a surgical | «eq procedure to get her wound | stitched after being wounded | by a stray bullet. | Photograph © Adrienne | Surprenant/MSF | seen ee ee eeee sen eaeeenaeeeeeneeseee | CAMEROON | An MSF nurse plays | with children at Maroua | hospital where MSF | provides nutritional care, | emergency surgery and | mental healthcare. | Photograph © Patrick | Meinharat | An MSF nurse treats a COVID-19 | | patient in an emergency care | unit in Porto Velho, Rondénia. | | Photograph © Diego Baravelli | ETHIOPIA | MSF translator Tedros gives | instructions to women | waiting with their children | for a medical consultation at | a mobile clinic in the village | of Adiftaw, in the northern | Ethiopian region of Tigray. | errrrrrrrr errr rrrrr terres | Photograph © Igor Barbero/MSF | seeeee | AFGHANISTAN | Ahmadullah visits his nephew | Samiullah, 12, who suffered a | gunshot wound to the head in early | 15. 2a2. S. May. Samiullah and his mother had | to travel for two and a half hours and | cross a river to avoid fighting before | reaching the hospital supported by | MSF in Boost, Helmand province. | Photograph © Tom Casey/MSF =a | DEMOCRATIC REPUBLIC | OF CONGO | MSF midwife Laure checks a | newborn baby at the health | centre in Ndu. | Photograph © Alexis Huguet | measures a child’s | ""3 | arm circumference to | . check for malnutrition in | southeast Madagascar | where people are facing | a food crisis. | Randrianarivelo | MADAGASCAR | An MSF staff member | Photograph © iAko M. | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE | PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE | PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | SOUTH SUDAN | Runaway | backpack. | People living in conflict-riven areas | of South Sudan are often forced to | flee new outbreaks of fighting at a | moment's notice. To ensure that | people on the run have access to | a minimum of medical care, MSF | developed the ‘runaway backpac | to provide on-the-go medical | treatment for common ailments 5 | and minor injuries. | Containing a range of supplies, from malaria | test kits through to antibiotics and water | purification tablets, the backpacks are designed | so that community health workers with | minimal medical training can use them | even when they are miles from the nearest | medical facility. | “The concept may seem simple,” says Amor | Chandoul of MSF’s Nairobi Displacement | Unit, which was tasked with redesigning the | backpack. “But crafting a mobile form of | healthcare that could move at the same pace | as those fleeing the conflict’s frontlines and | that was more than just a first-aid kit was a | process of trial and error.” | “At first, we thought each backpack should also | have space for telecommunications equipment,” | says MSF medical coordinator Dr Aziz | Harouna. “However, we soon realised that a | (mame | Each backpack | weighs 8 kg, hasspace | for the wearer's | personalitems andis | designed to provide | basic medical care for | upto 20 people. | Therunaway | backpack has now | been adopted by MSF | teams in Democratic | Republic of Congo | and other countries | where MSF works. | pack with high-tech kit like a satellite phone | might put the wearer at risk of being targeted | by an armed group, so we dropped it.” | While most items of clothing worn by MSF | staff are white, to make them stand out, these | backpacks are plain black with a small MSF | logo. Close up, they are identifiable as MSF, | but without singling out the wearer as a target. | The decision to carry a backpack is totally | voluntary. Volunteers are trained in how to use | all the medicines and supplies. The backpacks | are kept fully stocked, with all the drugs in | date, so they are always ready to grab and go. | The new backpacks were first used successfully | in April 2017 when an outbreak of fighting in | Upper Nile state caused 25,000 people to flee. | Among them were 29 health workers, each | carrying a runaway backpack, who were able to | provide people with emergency medical care. | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE | PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | Illustration: R. Palmer | Thermometer | Portable malaria | test kit | Antimalarial | drugs | Solar charger | Sterile water | Wound dressing | materials and | Paracetamol | (for pain, fever, | and inflammation) | Oral rehydration | solution | Antiseptic wipes | Small MSF reference ZB ; Water purification | guide on proper use tablets | and dosage of drugs, | including a register Antibiotics | to ‘d what h | ca £ | YOUR SUPPORT | £9.10 per month over | a year could pay for a | runaway backpack. | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE | PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | SUPPORTING MSF | We can’t do it without you | “Without regular support from people like you, | MSF wouldn't exist.” | Javid Abdelmoneim, MSF doctor | Join the MSF team today and support our | lifesaving work. Here’s how: | @  cattus tree on: 0800 055 79 82 | Complete the Direct Debit form below, | place it in an envelope and post it to: | FREEPOST RUBA-GYZY-YXST | Médecins Sans Frontiéres (MSF), Bumpers Way, | Bumpers Farm, Chippenham, SN14 6NG | 3] aos Scan to donate | | would like to make a monthly contribution of | £10 [] £20 CJ or my own choice e[[TIIII1) | staringon te_]sn{_J2en(] ot (| VL] | [4 | Alternatively MSF will claim on the next available date. | Please allow four weeks for the Direct Debit to be set up. | Please complete in BLOCK CAPITALS | (We will not pass your details to anyone else) | rie[_____] Foner | | Surname | | Address | | | rostcoce [+d | Telephone [ } | Email [ | | Hear from MSF by email | Sign up to our monthly email, Frontline, which provides first-hand accounts of | our work. You will receive Frontline, occasional emergency appeals, requests for | donations and event invitations. | [ ] Opt me in to email | Are you a UK tax payer? If so, you can make your gifts worth iftaid | 25% more at no extra cost to you. Please tick the box below. 7 | | wish Médecins Sans Frontiéres (MSF) to treat all gifts in the last 4 years, | this gift and all future gifts as Gift Aid donations. | am a UK taxpayer and | understand that if | pay less Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax than the | amount of Gift Aid claimed on all my donations in that tax year it is my | responsibility to pay any difference. | ow (TMT CLIT | NB: Please let us know if your name, address or tax status changes, or if you | would like to cancel this declaration, so that we can update our records. | Your support makes our work | possible — THANK YOU! | | 3 per month over a year | could provide a surgical kit | for an MSF hospital. | £1 a per month over a year | could send a doctor to the | field for three days. | $23 per month over a year | could pay for two folding | scoop stretchers to transport ~ | injured patients. | 100612 | Yes, | will support MSF’s volunteer medical teams Lée ea cues caries | Sovice Userneber[s]o]s]¢[4f2] @DBsEH | MSF UK, Chancery Exchange, 10 Furnival Street, London EC4A 1AB | Instruction to your Bank or Building Society to pay by Direct Debit | Name(s) of Account Holder(s) | | Bank/Bullding Society Account number | | | | | | | | | Branch Sortcode [ | |/{ | ]/[ | | | To: The Manager Name and address of Bank/Building Society | pescado [ | Signature(s) | pate | | |/| | \/| | | |_| | Banks and Building Societies may not accept Direct Debit Instructions for | some types of account. MSF's Direct Debits will show as MSF UK on your | bank statements. | Respecting you and your data | Your support is vital to our work and | we would like to keep you informed | with first-hand accounts from our | Staff and patients about the lifesaving | impact your support is having, from | combating epidemics to providing | emergency surgery. | We won't allow other organisations to | have access to your personal data for | marketing purposes and we won't | bombard you with appeals. | Please return to: | FREEPOST RUBA-GYZY-YXST, Alternatively phone 0800 055 79 82 | Médecins Sans Frontiéres (open 8 am - 10 pm 7 days a week) | Bumpers Way, Bumpers Farm, or make your donation online at | Chippenham, SN14 6NG msf.org.uk/donate | msf.org.uk/donate | By supporting MSF, you will receive You can change how you hear from | our quarterly magazine Dispatches, MSF UK by emailing | event invitations, and occasional uk.fundraising@london.msf.org | emergency appeals with requests for | donations by post. | or calling 020 7404 6600. | Visit our privacy notice for more: | msf.org.uk/privacy | INDIA | VID has | charged me as | a person and | as a doctor’ | The second wave of COVID-19 | in India has been devastating. | The country has now recorded | over 29 million cases and | over 377,000 deaths. At the | height of the crisis in May, | an MSF team reported from | a COVID-19 treatment centre | in Mumbai. | Mabel Morales, MSF medical | coordinator: | “The situation in India and in | Mumbai is very bad. It’s critical | across the country. We're providing | medical support to one of the | COVID treatment centres here | in Mumbai with 1,000 beds. | There are many, many cases and | the last week has seen a real | increase. The health workers are | overwhelmed and exhausted. | There is so much work to do. | The authorities here are well | organised and are adapting to | the situation as it evolves as best | they can. But as the cases increase, | it’s getting harder to find beds. | They're calling from hospital to | hospital trying to refer patients, | but they're struggling.” | Dr Gautam Hari Govind, | MSF medical activity manager: | “Picture a thousand-bed hospital. | There are 28 wards, as well as the | emergency, casualty and triage | areas. It’s a makeshift hospital in | a huge tent. Walking into it the | first time was a surreal experience; | I'd never seen anything like it. | It’s like walking into a huge ship. | ‘The ceiling is really high but the | ventilation is not great. During | the morning, Mumbai becomes | very humid. It’s excruciatingly hot. | And working in a protective suit | for six hours — it’s unimaginable. | The heat is almost too much | to take.” | Mabel Morales: “The team | is working six days a week in | eight-hour shifts. The situation | is terrible, but the commitment | and the dedication of the staff | is extraordinary. People are so | committed to helping the patients.” | Dr Gautam Hari Govind: | “Each member of our team is | really strong, both in their clinical | skills and their personality. This | is what I look for when I recruit | them. We're encouraging our | staff to start conversations early | on with their patients about the | possible eventualities of COVID, | to help both staff and patients | understand what might happen | — the possibility of their death. | We've had positive results from | this. It’s helped the patients, as | they're in some ways prepared. | And it’s helped our team accept | that not all patients will survive.” | Mabel Morales: “No one was | ready for the second wave. It | caught everybody by surprise. | In a very short time it turned into | a major crisis. It’s so much worse | this time and it was so sudden.” | Dr Gautam Hari Govind: | “COVID has changed me as a | person and as a doctor. People | are dying, but I’ve become used | to it. I used to be very patient- | centred, all about patient advocacy. | Now I'm scared of building | relationships with patients. At | first I did that, and then I would | come back for my next shift and | see their empty bed, and that | would break my heart. Even the | fact that I now refer to them as | ‘the patient’. I would have used | a name before. Or I would have | said, ‘my patient’. This is how it’s | changed me.” | Mabel Morales: “The needs | here are so great and what MSF | is doing is just a small part. But | it’s important for us to be here. | Staff from other hospitals are | coming to us to take part in the | trainings and we're able to support | them and share what we know. | I feel that we're in the right place | and we're all working together. | It’s hard work, but I’m glad to be | part of it.” | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE | PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE",Newspaper,NA,NA
291,"0 \ | | Ben | os OU moo | Lifeboats at Lifeboats | are on call right now around | 19,000 miles of coastline. | The RNLI is a charity | Independent from | government, so we | : rely on donations. | ® = lifeboat station | Donate today | The RNLI is the charity that saves lives at sea | The Royal National Lifeboat Institution, a charity registered in England and Wales (209603), Scotland (SC037736), | the Republic of Ireland (CHY 2678 and 20003326), the Bailiwick of Jersey (14), the Isle of Man (1308 and 006329F), | the Bailiwick of Guernsey and Alderney, of West Quay Road, Poole, Dorset, BH15 1HZ | ay = | ‘| don’t ask for payment. | | just ask for the kit and | training that keeps me | ready to save the | next life.’ | Dear friend, | There’s been so much uncertainty in the | last year or so, hasn’t there? It makes | me all the more grateful for the things | | can depend upon in uncertain times. | As a volunteer lifeboat crew member, | | don’t know when I'll be called to launch | — or what I'll face. | just know that I'll | be depending on protective gear, | well-maintained equipment and | up-to-date training. That’s what will get | me through. That's what helps us save lives, | and get home safe ourselves. And what | makes it all possible? Kindness. | That's why | need your help today. | ve been a volunteer here at Hayling Island | for 19 years, and something I’ve learned | from my time on the crew is that no two | rescues are the same. I’ve been called to | aid all sorts of people in all sorts of danger, | from swimmers, surfers and fishing crews | to beachgoers who might only visit the | coast once a year. | It’s the same for our crews all around and | across the UK, from Shetland to the Isles of | Scilly; from Loch Ness to the Thames: we’re | on call to save every one we can. Next time, | it could be someone you know and love. | But we can’t save lives without the kit and | training that keeps us ready. | Your £30 donation today will help | provide everything we need. | The need for our lifesaving service | is increasing — the weather's unpredictable, | there’s been a surge in staycations and | this year is expected to be our busiest ever. | That means we have to be better prepared | than ever. But kit gets worn out. Lifeboats | need to be maintained. And our world-class | training needs to be maintained with them. | Those are the things that make | the difference. Those are the things that | we depend on. And, because the RNLI is | a charity, that means that we depend | on generous people like you. | . = 2) | Lifeboat volunteers rel | ; to save lives an | Will you help give the | Y on kit and training | dcome home safely. | m what they need? | As you read this, we're on call, ready to | launch, day or night, rain or shine. And if | the pager sounds, we'll be depending on | our fellow lifesavers: the crew members | around us, and you. | We would be so grateful for any | support you can give to help us | in the face of uncertainty. | Your sincerely, | Mattox | Matt Farr, Volunteer Crew Member, | Hayling Island | How will your kind | gift | ian same) | could buy fuel for a lifeboat could go towards training a | and help our volunteers reach crew member so they have | people in danger at sea. the skills to keep others safe | — and themselves. | could buy a pair of sturdy, | protective, non-slip | seagoing boots, to keep a | Photos: Stephen Duncombe, Andy Lyons, RNLI/(Harrison Bates, Nigel Millard, Nathan Williams) | volunteer sure-footed on deck. | Will you send a gift that will help | to keep volunteers like Matt safe? | Three easy ways to donate: | ad de PLEASE CUT HERE | C4 Online: \. By phone: | VISIT THE WEBSITE CALL US ON | RNLI.ORG/HOMESAFE 0300 300 0109 | WE'RE OPEN WEEKDAYS, 8AM-6PM | In 2020, for every £1 spent on charitable activities, 56p was on lifeboats, property and | equipment; 27p on running the lifeboat service; 11p on lifeguard rescue; 4p on safety, | education and awareness; and 2p on international drowning prevention. | % By freepost: | COMPLETE AND RETURN | THE FORM OVERLEAF IN AN | ENVELOPE ADDRESSED TO | “FREEPOST RNLI’ | NO STAMP IS REQUIRED | PREP21016 | PLEASE COMPLETE AND RETURN THIS | FORM IN AN ENVELOPE ADDRESSED TO | TL RAL | ‘FREEPOST RNLI’. NO STAMP IS REQUIRED. Lifeboats | | WANT TO GIVE: [£10 []£30 [£55 | OR MY CHOICE OF: £ | | RR) | Thank you for your | kind donation today | YOUR DETAILS (PLEASE COMPLETE IN CAPITALS) | Title First Name (in full) Surname | Address | Postcode | Email Mobile phone number | GIFT AID | Gift Aid your donation and boost every £1 by 25p at no extra cost | In order to Gift Aid your donations, you must tick the box below confirming all the below applies: | [| YES | want to Gift Aid all donations | make in the future or have made in the past 4 years to the RNLI | until | notify you otherwise. Please enter today’s date | | am a UK taxpayer and understand that if | pay less Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax than the amount of | Gift Aid claimed on all my donations in that tax year it is my responsibility to pay any difference. | [| NO | am unable to Gift Aid this donation. | Please notify the charity if you: want to cancel this declaration; change your name or home address; no longer | pay sufficient tax on your income and/or capital gains. | Privacy Notice: We will always store your personal details securely, and they will only be used by the RNLI, RNLI Shop and RNLI College. | Your data may also be used for analysis purposes, to help us provide the best service possible. We will only allow information to be used by | suppliers working on our behalf and we'll only share it if required to do so by law. For full details see our Privacy Policy at RNLI.org/PrivacyPolicy | or contact our Supporter Experience Team on 0300 300 9918 (from the UK) or +44 1202 663234 (from outside the UK). | LET'S KEEP IN TOUCH! | Your support saves lives, and we look forward to keeping in touch with you by post and phone, sharing our | news, activities and appeals. Would you like to receive our emails and text messages too? | [] Yes, I'm happy to hear from you by email | [ | Yes, I’m happy to hear from you by text | Even if you have received our communications in the past, we'll make sure we honour the preferences you | express here. If you would rather not hear from us, or would like to change how we contact you, please | get in touch. Just visit RNLI.org/preferences or call 0300 300 9918. | PAYMENT METHOD | | enclose a cheque/postal order/charity voucher made payable to: ‘RNLI’, | Please debit the sum above from my debit/credit/CAF: | CARD NUMBER EXPIRY DATE | Registered with | THANK YOU! (FR) FUNDRAISING | REGULATOR | 33H LND ISV3I1d",Newspaper,NA,NA
292,"A broken smile brings a life | of heartbreak | Clefts are the world’s third most common birth defect. | In the UK most babies receive corrective surgery within the | first year but children in the poorest countries have | no chance of life-saving surgery. | Every 3 minutes, a child is born with a cleft lip or cleft palate. | Children that do survive are often malnourished, have | difficulties speaking and are bullied by other children resulting | in many missing school. | It can take as little as 45 minutes and | £150 to change a child’s life forever | Operation@)smile | Freepost RSYJ-HGEK-RGBX | Operation Smile | 126 Fairlie Road | Slough | Smile SL1 4PY | You are changing | a life today. | Thank you for | your gift | Charity Registration Number 1091316 | Please mend | my broken smile | Operation€)smile | “We travelled 24 hours to give my | daughter a chance in life.” | It is late afternoon. Operation Smile is in Danang, | Vietnam for two weeks. Hundreds of children and | their parents hover anxiously outside the hospital. | Way back in the crowd, hiding in her mother’s | arms, is two-year-old Ngan (pronounced nan). They | are hoping that life is about to change forever for | her. But are they too late? | Since she was born, Ngan had been living in shame | and isolation in a small village in southern Vietnam. | Her broken smile made her an outcast, an object of | revulsion. Her family thought she had been cursed. | They had never seen a baby with a cleft lip and a | hole in the palate before. | Ngan BEFORE | Every | three minutes | a baby is | born with | Donate at operationsmile.org.uk/brokensmile or call FREE 0800 408 1828 | operating | for free | @ During a typical project, our volunteer medical | teams will operate on 120 children. | @ Everyone on the team is a specialist in their field. | @ Everyone gives up their time to work long hours, | for two weeks, for free. | @ Their expertise, skills and dedication are why | our operations can cost as little as £150. | Ngan’s mother | Ngan’s name is called. Her mother sees many other | hopeful little faces turning to disappointment. There | is not enough time or resources to treat everyone. | A smile transformed, | life begins again | After her operation. Ngan looks in the mirror. Her | smile is mended. Everyone is beaming. Her mother | cries with happiness. A year later, on the Operation | Smile follow-up visit, Ngan is transformed. She is | accepted in her village. She is no longer cursed, no | longer an outcast. For the first time she has friends | to play with and is treated like a normal little girl. | It can take | just 45 minutes | and £150 to | change a life | \ forever | Medical Information | Scientific research | indicates that taking | multivitamins such as | vitamin B6, folic acid | and zinc one month | pre-conception and | during the early months | of pregnancy, as well as | not smoking or drinking | may reduce the | incidence of clefting | and other birth defects. | For more advice on | prenatal care,you should | consult your doctor or | healthcare expert. | Operation Smile provides free surgeries to repair cleft lip, cleft palate and other | facial conditions for children around the globe. | FOLD | DETACH HERE | ‘Tam happy to mend a broken smile and change a life. | Here is my gift of... | L] £150 helps provide cleft surgery for a child L] £15 pays for antibiotics for 10 children | L £50 helps pay for medical supplies for an LJ My gift of £ | operation | Mr/Mrs/Ms/Miss/Other First Name Surname | Address | Postcode | Home Telephone Mobile | Email | I authorise you to debit my card with the amount of £ | Amex L_] Mastercard |_| Visa L_] Delta _] tsestro |_| | cardno._L tT JLT TT ILELTICLOE] [ganna re | Start date LT WLI Expiry date LTV) Issue No | Signature | OR send a cheque: I enclose a cheque payable to Operation Smile UK for £ | ae a ar | LLL i & | SHADED BOXES FOR | MAESTRO ONLY | A Boe | | | OR Give on 0800 408 1828 or operationsmile.org.uk/brokensmile = | Seal y | Please don’t forget to tick here and make your gift worth even more | Make your donation worth 25% more at no extra cost to you. gi fea dit | Yes, | am a UK tax payer. | would like Operation Smile to treat all donations that | have made over the past | four years and all donations that | make now and in the future as Gift Aid donations unless | notify you otherwise. | | confirm that | have paid or will pay an amount of Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax for each tax year (6 April to 5 April) that is at least | equal to the amount of tax that all the charities and CASC that | donate to will reclaim on my gifts for that tax year. | understand that other | taxes such as VAT and Council Tax do not qualify. | understand the charity will reclaim 25p of tax on every £1 that | give. | j Your gift is changing children’s lives and we would like to show you the impact you are making. We will 7 | email info@operationsmile.org.uk. Operation Smile will not sell or swap your details with any other organisation. i | send you information about our work, campaigns and fundraising: | L} Don't contact me by Post [] YES, contact me byPhone (1 YES, contact me by email | L] YES, contact me bySMS — [_] Please send me information on leaving a legacy to Operation Smile in my will | You can change your personal details or request that we stop contacting you at any time. Call 0203 475 5126 or | Thank you from the children whose lives will change because of your gift | ° ° Registered with | FUNDRAISIN | Operation @)smile R) PORE RASING | Charity Registration Number 1091316 | MOISTEN HERE | FOLD",Newspaper,NA,NA
