306,"An open letter to | readers of The | | The people of Yemen need your help. For.more than six years, we’ve lived | under the shadow of war. In some areas of the country we are seeing near | famine conditions. Children, women and men could die from hunger. | Yet, the fighting goes on. The current offensive in Marib, less than 100 miles | from my home in Sana’a, has killed hundreds and forced tens of thousands to | flee. The war affects everything. Public sector workers, such as teachers and | police officers, haven’t been paid regularly since 2017. Electricity is 900% more | expensive than in 2015. People have sold all their assets, but many are barely | surviving. Tragically, some have taken their own lives. To me, they are just as | much casualties of this war as people who die when their homes are hit. | When the bombing starts, my wife and | take the children to the safe room. We | tell them the explosions are people playing with fireworks. We haven’t suffered a | direct hit yet, but many of my neighbours haven’t been so lucky. When | go for a | walk, | see displaced families living on the sidewalk and:under bridges, with only | cardboard and plastic sheeting to protect them. | And then we have COVID-19, which has-seen the international community cut aid | to Yemen. It feels as if, while our people suffer, the world is looking the other way. | UN Secretary-General Antdnio Guterres said recently: “Millions of Yemeni children, | women and men desperately need aid to live. Cutting aid is a death sentence.” | So yes, the situation is dire. But no, we will not give up. Famine is edging closer, | but it’s not too late to help people. UNHCR has identified the most at risk families, | and with your help today could provide them with an Emergency Payment of | £143. The payment of £143 supports a family of six and is based on the cost of | a ‘Survival Minimum Expenditure Basket’ of essentials such as food, water and | medicine. For'a family on the brink of famine, it could be the difference between | life and death. | Thank you. | Ebahion | Ebrahim Madaghesh, UNHCR Yemen | £143 could provide a family with an EMERGENCY | PAYMENT to buy food and other essentials | Give now at | unrefugees.org.uk/helpyemen | Call O800 029 3883, or fill in and return the form below | I would like to give: [|_| £72 |_| £143 [_] e286 [_| othere | L]1 enclose a cheque made payable to UK for UNHCR OR | L] 1 would like to make a donation by debit/credit card | ‘Card no. | Expiry date | FoR) tes | eS Oo ES Bd a a | Signature Date | CO Tick here, if you don’t want to receive an acknowledgement by post | Title First name | Address | Last name | Postcode | Email | Phone | Please tell us if you are happy to hear about UNHCR’s work: Esl By email O By phone | Please return this form along with your cheque to: | Freepost UK FOR UNHCR (you don’t need a stamp) | Respecting your personal data: We will use your details to process your donation and to keep | you up to date with our work, fundraising activities and other events. You.can read more about how | we use your data in our Privacy Policy unrefugees.org.uk/privacy-policy. You can opt out of any | communications at any time by contacting our Supporter Care Team using the details above. | oF 5 Scan this FUNDRAISING ¢ (f yy 3 ? | ak = QR code to x ( Y UNHCR | Sicses ts REGULATOR \ VEY) | ss == The UN Refugee Agency | United Kingdom for UNHCR | A minimum of 75% of your gift will directly help | displaced people in Yemen, funding Emergency | Payments and other humanitarian interventions. | Hn. United Kingdom for UNHCR is a registered charity in England and Wales (1183415). | P21YEIIB1",Newspaper,NA,NA
