328,"An open letter to | readers of | | The people of Yemen need your help. For more than six years, we’ve lived under | the shadow of war. In some areas of the country we are seeing near famine | conditions. Children, women and men could die from hunger. | Yet, the fighting goes on. The current offensive in Marib, less than 100 miles | from my home in Sana’a, has killed hundreds and forced tens of thousands to — | fiee. The war affects everything. Public sector workers, such as teachers and | police officers, haven’t been paid regularly since 2017. Electricity is 900% more | expensive than in 2015. People have sold all their assets, but many are barely | surviving. Tragically, some have taken their own lives. To me, they are just as | much casualties of this war as people who die when their homes are hit. | When the bombing starts, my wife and | take the children to the safe room. We | tell them the explosions are people playing with fireworks. We haven't suffered a | direct hit yet, but many of my neighbours haven’t been so lucky. When | go for a | walk, | see displaced families living on the sidewalk and under bridges, with only | cardboard and plastic sheeting to protect them. | And then we have COVID-19, which has seen the international community cut aid | to Yemen. It feels as if, while our people suffer, the world is looking the other way. | UN Secretary-General Antonio Guterres said recently: “Millions of Yemeni children, | women and men desperately need aid to live. Cutting aid is a death sentence.” | So yes, the situation is dire. But no, we will not give up. Famine is edging closer, | but it’s not too late to help people. UNHCR has identified the most at risk families, | and with your help today could provide them with an Emergency Payment of | £143. The payment of £143 Supports a family of six and is based on the cost of | a ‘Survival Minimum Expenditure Basket’ of essentials such as food, water and | medicine. For a family on the brink of famine, it could be the difference between | life and death. : | Thank you. | EZ bahwn | Ebrahim Madaghesh, UNHCR Yemen | £143 could provide a family with an EMERGENCY | PAYMENT to buy food and other essentials | Give now at | unrefugees.org.uk/helpyemen | Call O800 029 3883, or fill in and return the form below | I would like to give: |_| £72 |_| e143 |__| 2286 |_| Other £ | [J 1 enclose a cheque made payable to UK for UNHCR OR | (] 1 would like to make a donation by debit/credit card | Card no. ‘ Expiry date | | | peal fiom lice tal afro 4 | Signature Date ; P | C] Tick here, if you don’t want to receive an acknowledgement by post | Title First name Last name | Address | Postcode | Email | Phone | Please tell us if you are happy to hear about UNHCR’s work: = By email CO By ghane | Please return this form along with your cheque to: | Freepost UK FOR UNHCR (you don’t need a stamp) | Increase your donation by 25p for every £1 you donate | [Ji would like UK for UNHCR to claim Gift Aid on this donation (currently 25p for iP ttai a a | each £1 donated), as well as any donations ! make in the future and have made in | the past four years to UK for UNHCR, until ! notify you otherwise. | confirm | am a UK taxpayer and | | understand that if! pay less Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax than the amount of Gift Aid claimed | on all my donations in that tax year it is my responsibility to pay any difference. | understand that | council tax and VAT do not qualify. if your circumstances change or you have any questions, please | contact our Supporter Care Team on 0800 029 3883 or email supportercore@unrefugees.org.uk | Respecting your personal data: We will use your details to process your donation and to keep | you up to date with our work, fundraising activities and other events. You can read more about how | we use your data in our Privacy Policy unrefugees.org.uk/privacy-policy. You can opt out of any | communications at any time by contacting our Supporter Care Team using the details ab