ened to that woman if the pap tegrndady a Thank you. | Médecins Sans Frontiéres/Doctors Without Borders (MSF) is the world’s leading emergency medical humanitarian aid organisation. We help people affected by armed hospital wasn’t there. MSF | conflict, epidemics and natural or manmade disasters, without discrimination and irrespective of race, religion, creed, gender or political affiliation. We work in more | than 70 countries and go to places where others cannot or choose not to go. We can do this because we are independently funded, | with 99.8 per cent of our funding coming from individual or private donors, like you. | : PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT I | SUPPORTING MSF | We can’t do it without you | Your support makes our work | possible — THANK YOU! | “Without regular support from people like you, | MSF wouldn't exist.” | Javid Abdelmoneim, MSF doctor | Join the MSF team today and support our | lifesaving work. Here’s how: | BY) Call us free on: 0800 055 79 82 | Complete the Direct Debit form below, | place it in an envelope and post it to: | FREEPOST RUBA-GYZY-YXST | Médecins Sans Frontiéres (MSF), Bumpers Way, | Bumpers Farm, Chippenham, SN14 6NG | or visit | msf.org.uk/donate | Yes, | will support MSF’s volunteer medical teams Ze | | would like to make a monthly contribution of | £10 [| £20 [] or my own choice “TEETER | staringon 18{_j1sn{_jaon] o [TL] TT) | Alternatively MSF will claim on the next available date. | Please allow four weeks for the Direct Debit to be set up. | Please complete in BLOCK CAPITALS | (We will not pass your details to anyone else) | Tte[ si Forename [ | Surname | | Address [ | | 3 per month over a year | could provide a surgical kit | for an MSF hospital. | £1 a per month over a year | could send a doctor to the | field for three days. | £23 per month over a year | could pay for two folding | scoop stretchers to transport ~<— | injured patients. | Serdcotion tees 5)9[5|4]¢[2] Cezar | MSF UK, Chancery Exchange, 10 Furnival Street, London EC4A 1AB | Instruction to your Bank or Building Society to pay by Direct Debit | Name(s) of Account Holder(s) | 100623 | MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES | DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | | enk/Building Society Account number | TT TLL.) | Branch Sort Code [ I \/{ | | | | To: The Manager Name and address of Bank/Building Society | [ Postcode | [ | Telephone [ | Email [ | Hear from MSF by email | Sign up to our monthly email, Frontline, which provides first-hand accounts of | our work. You will receive Frontline, occasional emergency appeals, requests for | donations and event invitations. | C] Opt me in to email | Are you a UK tax payer? If so, you can make your gifts worth iftaid te | 25% more at no extra cost to you. Please tick the box below. 7 | [] | wish Médecins Sans Frontiéres (MSF) to treat all gifts in the last 4 years, | this gift and all future gifts as Gift Aid donations. | am a UK taxpayer and | understand that if | pay less Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax than the | amount of Gift Aid claimed on all my donations in that tax year it is my | responsibility to pay any difference. | roscoe | | Signature(s) | bate {| | WL Li {TJ | Banks and Building Societies may not accept Direct Debit Instructions for | some types of account. MSF’s Direct Debits will show as MSF UK on your | bank statements. | Alternatively phone 0800 055 79 82 | (open 8 am - 10 pm 7 days a week) | or make your donation online at | Chippenham, SN14 6NG msf.org.uk/donate | ote [1 VELL VLL1 I | | NB: Please let us know if your name, address or tax status changes, or if you | would like to cancel this declaration, so that we can update our records. msf.org.uk/donate | Respecting you and your data We won't allow other organisations to By supporting MSF, you will receive You can change how you hear from | Your support is vital to our work and have access to your personal data for our quarterly magazine Dispatches, MSF UK by emailing | we would like to keep you informed marketing purposes and we won't event invitations, and occasional uk.fundraising@london.msf.org | with first-hand accounts from our bombard you with appeals. emergency appeals with requests for or calling 020 7404 6600. | staff and patients about the lifesaving donations by post. Visit our privacy notice for more: | impact your support is having, from msf.org.uk/privacy i | combating epidemics to providing mrt | emergency surgery. | INDIA | ‘CGVID has | changed me as | a person and | asa doctor’ | The second wave of COVID-19 | in India has been devastating. | The country has now recorded | over 29 million cases and | over 377,000 deaths. At the | height of the crisis in May, | an MSF team reported from | a COVID-19 treatment centre | in Mumbai. | Mabel Morales, MSF medical | coordinator: | “The situation in India and in | Mumbai is very bad. It’s critical | across the country. We're providing | medical support to one of the | COVID treatment centres here | in Mumbai with 1,000 beds. | There are many, many cases and | the last week has seen a real | increase. The health workers are | overwhelmed and exhausted. | There is so much work to do. | The authorities here are well | organised and are adapting to | the situation as it evolves as best | they can. But as the cases increase, | it’s getting harder to find beds. | They're calling from hospital to | hospital trying to refer patients, | but they're struggling.” | Dr Gautam Hari Govind, | MSF medical activity manager: | “Picture a thousand-bed hospital. | There are 28 wards, as well as the | emergency, casualty and triage | areas. It’s a makeshift hospital in | a huge tent. Walking into it the | first time was a surreal experience; | I'd never seen anything like it. | It’s like walking into a huge ship. | The ceiling is really high but the | ventilation is not great. During | the morning, Mumbai becomes | very humid. It’s excruciatingly hot. | And working in a protective suit | for six hours — it’s unimaginable. | The heat is almost too much | to take.” | Mabel Morales: “The team | is working six days a week in | eight-hour shifts. The situation | is terrible, but the commitment | and the dedication of the staff | is extraordinary. People are so | committed to helping the patients.” : | Nangia/MSF | Dr Gautam Hari Govind: | “Each member of our team is | really strong, both in their clinical | skills and their personality. This | is what I look for when I recruit | them. We're encouraging our | staff to start conversations early | on with their patients about the | possible eventualities of COVID, | to help both staff and patients | understand what might happen | — the possibility of their death. | We've had positive results from | this. It’s helped the patients, as | they're in some ways prepared. | And it’s helped our team accept | that not all patients will survive.” | Mabel Morales: “No one was | ready for the second wave. It | caught everybody by surprise. | In a very short time it turned into | a major crisis. It’s so much worse | this time and it was so sudden.” | Dr Gautam Hari Govind: | “COVID has changed me as a | person and as a doctor. People | are dying, but I’ve become used | to it. I used to be very patient- | centred, all about patient advocacy. | Now I’m scared of building | relationships with patients. At | first I did that, and then I would | come back for my next shift and | see their empty bed, and that | would break my heart. Even the | fact that I now refer to them as | ‘the patient’. I would have used | a name before. Or I would have | said, ‘my patient’. This is how it’s | changed me.” | Mabel Morales: “The needs | here are so great and what MSF | is doing is just a small part. But | it’s important for us to be here. | Staff from other hospitals are | coming to us to take part in the | trainings and we're able to support | them and share what we know. | I feel that we're in the right place | and we're all working together. | It’s hard work, but I’m glad to be | part of it.” | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE | PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT IMSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | SOUTH SUDAN | Runaway | backpack | People living in conflict-riven areas | of South Sudan are often forced to | flee new outbreaks of fighting at a | moment's notice. To ensure that | people on the run have access to | a minimum of medical care, MSF | developed the ‘runaway backpack” | to provide on-the-go medical | treatment for common ailments | and minor injuries. | Containing a range of supplies, from malaria | test kits through to antibiotics and water | purification tablets, the backpacks are designed | so that community health workers with | minimal medical training can use them | even when they are miles from the nearest | medical facility. | “The concept may seem simple,” says Amor | Chandoul of MSF’s Nairobi Displacement | Unit, which was tasked with redesigning the | backpack. “But crafting a mobile form of | healthcare that could move at the same pace | as those fleeing the conflict’s frontlines and | that was more than just a first-aid kit was a | process of trial and error.” | “At first, we thought each backpack should also | have space for telecommunications equipment,” | says MSF medical coordinator Dr Aziz | Harouna. “However, we soon realised that a | Each backpack | weighs 8 kg, hasspace | for the wearer's | personalitems andis | designed to provide | basic medical care for | upto20 people. | Therunaway | backpack has now | been adopted by MSF | teams in Democratic | Republic of Congo | and other countries | where MSF works. | ut ital i TI (Nesey arenas | ‘ al ee SEH le a | ) | | 8 & ) | | oy | Portable malaria | test kit | Antimalarial | drugs | Solar charger | Sterile water | Wound dressing | materials and | pack with high-tech kit like a satellite phone | might put the wearer at risk of being targeted | by an armed group, so we dropped it.” | While most items of clothing worn by MSF | staff are white, to make them stand out, these | backpacks are plain black with a small MSF | logo. Close up, they are identifiable as MSF, | but without singling out the wearer as a target. | The decision to carry a backpack is totally | voluntary. Volunteers are trained in how to use | all the medicines and supplies. The backpacks | are kept fully stocked, with all the drugs in | date, so they are always ready to grab and go. | The new backpacks were first used successfully | in April 2017 when an outbreak of fighting in | Upper Nile state caused 25,000 people to flee. | Among them were 29 health workers, each | carrying a runaway backpack, who were able to | provide people with emergency medical care. | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE | PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | Paracetamol | ., %e (for pain, fever, | =F Me and inflammation) | * th Oral rehydration | % *.. solution | = - “*, . | Antiseptic wipes | Small MSF reference Water purification | guide on proper use tablets | and dosage of drugs, | including a register Antibiotics | t rd what hi | teens states £ | YOUR SUPPORT | £9.10 per month over | a year could pay fora | runaway backpack. | Iilustration: R. Palmer | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | AROUND THE WORLD | Around the | world with MSF | WE’RE PROUD OF HOW WE SPEND | YOUR CONTRIBUTIONS | 86% 12% | ETHIOPIA | MSF translator Tedros gives | ON OUR HUMANITARIAN WORK ONFUNDRAISING(WERAISE ON OFFICE MANAGEMENT instructions to women ————_— | £8.11 FOR EVERY $1 WE SPEND) waiting with their children AFGHANISTAN | for a medical consultation at Ahmadullah visits his nephew x | a mobile clinic in the village Samiullah, 12, who suffered a m Mi | of Adiftaw, in the northern gunshot wound to the head in early | Ethiopian region of Tigray. : May. Samiullah and his mother had | Photograph © Igor Barbero/MSF : to travel for two and a half hours and | CENTRAL AFRICAN : ae cross a river to avoid fighting before | REPUBLIC : reaching the hospital supported by | , “ MSF in Boost, Helmand province. | France Beldo is brought to the | 2 recovery room after a surgical i Photograph © Tom Casey/MSF | vas procedure to get her CT i eer | stitched after being wounded | by a stray bullet. | Photograph © Adrienne | Surprenant/MSF | DEMOCRATIC REPUBLIC | OF CONGO | MSF midwife Laure checks a | newborn baby at the health | centre in Ndu. | Photograph © Alexis Huguet | CAMEROON | An MSF nurse plays : | with children at Maroua i | hospital where MSF M ADAGASCAR | provides nutritional care, | emergency surgery and An MSF staff member | mental healthcare. measures oon s : | : arm circumference to | ial eras check for malnutrition in | southeast Madagascar | 4 where people are facing | RAG a food crisis. | BRAZIL Photograph © iAko M. | An MSF nurse treats a COVID-19 Randrianarivelo | patient in an emergency care | unit in Porto Velho, Rondénia. | } Photograph © Diego Baravelli | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE | PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE | YOUR SUPPORT MAKES OUR WORK POSSIBLE | PLEASE HELP US SAVE LIVES AND DONATE AT MSF.ORG.UK/DONATE",Newspaper,NA,NA
