01,"N\A March OND 1 | LE MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES | DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | msf.org.uk | Delivered by | ROYAL MAIL | Royal Mail POSTAGE PAID GB | — MELKSHAM10 | Lele | eeses 869% 68. COE Se | a t——<__ co  - | MSF UK, Lower Ground Floor, Chancery Exchange, 10 Fumival Street, London, EC4A |AB | uk.fundraising@london.msf.org.uk | Registered Charity No. 1026588 | Crrecycle | This communication is printed on sustainable paper and is recyclable | They tell me that she has five children. If she doesn't survive, what will their future be? Rapidly I tell the | family we need blood donors and, out of the many volunteers, we find three with matching blood types. | Throughout the night I stay with Nyame. Her family camps in the same room. Nobody sleeps. At the same | time, a team works to find a way to get her the surgery she desperately needs, But the runway is | consumed by mud and, with planes unable to land, options are limited. | As dawn breaks, we still don’t have an answer. | check the clock and realise that I've been in the hospital | for 26 hours and with Nyame for 18 hours. | hand over to another MSF doctor and take some rest. | I wake to learn that Nyame's haemoglobin levels are falling. There’s no visible bleeding, so we do an | ultrasound. Nyame is haemorrhaging into her uterus. We are now close to 24 hours post-delivery. | | update the family and explain that there's a high chance that Nyame will not survive, but that we will | not stop treating her. | As the sun starts to set, we hear the most amazing news. Another humanitarian aid organisation will | provide a helicopter to transport Nyame to Juba. In Juba, MSF will arrange and fund the surgical care. | | cannot even begin to express my gratitude. There aren't exactly spare helicopters in South Sudan. The | earliest the helicopter can arrive is midday tomorrow. We have no more blood for Nyame, but we have | hope on our side. | The next day, the helicopter is waiting. | rig up a makeshift pole so we can continue to provide her with | intravenous fluid and make final adjustments to the infusions. Around 30 people are gathered outside | the maternity ward, everyone wanting to offer a hand. We all watch as the aircraft rises and flies | towards the horizon. | Over the coming days, we frequently refresh our emails, eagerly awaiting an update. Eventually we learn | that Nyame is recovering well. She is expected to return to Old Fangak in the coming week. | Nyame is alive and her children still have their mother because of the commitment, passion and | generosity of many. Thank you for supporting MSF and showing this same commitment, pela and | generosity, which will help us save even more lives. | Kind regards | —TheMt | Dr Tom Niccol | MSF doctor | Thank you for donating under the Gift Aid scheme. As a_ This declaration was made by you at the time of | UK taxpayer you have agreed Médecins Sans Frontiéres donation and notified to you on 04/03/2021. You are | may reclaim the tax on donations you have made in the — entitled to cancel your Gift Aid declaration at any time | last four years, this gift and all future donations. You within 30 days of this letter. If in the future you are no | must pay the amount of Income Tax and / or Capital longer paying enough tax, equal to what MSF is | Gains Tax for each tax year, that is at least equal to the reclaiming, or you wish to cancel your declaration, | amount of Gift Aid claimed on all your donations in that _ please notify us in writing. Please also tell us if your | tax year; and if you have paid less it is your name or address details | responsibility to pay the difference. change so we can . «ats | update our records. giftaid uw | By supporting MSF you will receive our quarterly magazine, event invites and occasional emergency fundraising appeals. | You can change how you hear from MSF by contacting us using the details overleaf. Visit msf.org.uk/privacy for more. | 04 March 2021 | Dear | Thank you so much for setting up a regular gift of £3.00 per month | to Médecins Sans Frontieres (MSF); your support means we can | provide medical aid where it is needed most. | “Emergency in maternity!"" | The call pierces an otherwise silent period on the MSF radio. 1 head | towards the ward, uncertain what I'll find. Anything is possible in | Old Fangak... | Old Fangak is 500 km north of Juba, the capital of South Sudan. It's a | swamp region with a population of around 50,000. There aren't any | roads to Old Fangak. During the rainy season, when the runway | turns to mud, three helicopter flights and a boat trip are required to | travel here from the capital. 1 work at the small MSF hospital here, | and today it is raining. | Arriving at the maternity ward, | see the midwife has blood on her | shoes. This isn’t wholly unusual, but 1 can read from her facial | expression that the situation is serious. The patient has given birth | to a healthy baby girl. However, there have been complications and | she is now haemorrhaging. There's a river of blood flowing over the | front of the bed, only partially captured by a nearly full bucket | below. Within a moment of my arrival, the patient loses | consciousness. | Crammed into the tiny delivery room, where the temperature must | be approaching 40°C, sweat is pouring from all the staff, their | safety glasses fogging up. We squeeze fluid into drips in each of the | patient's arms and! start her on a potent medication that raises | blood pressure. Within 10 minutes, the patient starts to groan. She is | regaining consciousness. | call her Nyame. (Nyame is not her real | name, but a polite form of address in the local language, Nuer.) | Nyame’s condition requires surgical support. We don't have those | facilities in Old Fangak, so we immediately begin the process to | transfer her to another hospital. In the meantime, Nyame | desperately needs blood. I gather all of her family members together | and explain that Nyame is in a critical condition. | sm""5%. Medical aid where it is needed most. Independent. Neutral. Impartial. | MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES (UK) | English Charity Reg No. 1026588 | English Registration No. 2853011 | Acompany limited by guarantee | Registered office as above | <SESIRS | 15 / W0439507 / 7001 / COLDDDGA / WEL2DD | -sé SF | MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES | DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | Lower Ground Floor | Chancery Exchange | 10 Furnival Street | London EC4A 1AB | United Kingdom | +44 (0) 20 7404 6600 | uk.fundraising@london.msf.org | msf.org.uk | A four-month-old girl, Nyapa, has her pulse checked by an | MSF doctor at Old Fangak hospital, South Sudan. | Photograph © Frederic Noy/MSF | ""They tell me that she | has five children. If she | doesn't survive, what | will their future be?"" | VY @msf_uk | Ei msf.english | FIND OUT MORE | You can find out more and | subscribe to our email updates | at msf.org.uk or find us on: | ¥ facebook.com/msf.english | Fa twitter.com/msf_uk | Front cover: A child | receives an oral | vaccination against | poliomyelitis in Central | African Republic, as | part of MSF’s mass | vaccination campaign | targeting children | under five. | Photograph © Pierre-Yves | Bernard/MSF | Above: Medical supplies | for MSF's hospital in | Agok, South Sudan, | are unloaded from a | plane. During the rainy | season, when roads are | often impassable, the | plane is essential for | bringing in supplies and | transporting staff to and | from the capital, Juba. | Photograph © Valérie | Batselaere/MSF | msf.org.uk | Médecins Sans Frontiéres | Lower Ground Floor | Chancery Exchange | 10 Furnival Street | London EC4A 1AB | Call 020 74046600 | Email uk.fundraising@london.msf.org | Registered Charity No, 1026588 | MEDECINS SANS FRONTIERES | DOCTORS WITHOUT BORDERS | WELCOME | IEDECINS SANS FRG | DOCTORS WITHOUT | SS ae | Thank you | “When you look around | the globe, it’s easy to feel | overwhelmed at the many | situations where people | have urgent medical needs. | ‘The coronavirus crisis has rapidly | evolved, threatening the lives of | people around the world and | presenting even greater risks in | countries with weak or fragile | health systems; the war in Syria | continues to create devastation; | the conflict in Yemen persists, | “Without the generosity of | individual supporters like | you, we wouldn't exist.” | with the Saudi-led coalition | bombing civilian | targets, including hospitals; | while in northeast Nigeria, a | decade of conflict has left | 7.1 million people in need of | humanitarian assistance. | That's without mentioning all | the chronic crises and neglected | diseases that exist, as well as all the | people who are unable to get the | medical care they need. MSF’s | mission is to address all of these. | This is where you come in. | You've decided to help redress | those inequalities and respond | to those needs by supporting MSF | with a regular gift. Without the | generosity of individual supporters | like you, we wouldn’t exist. 100% | independent funding means that | there are no political or other | agendas at work. | Your donations will ensure that | MSF can deliver vaccinations, | outpatient consultations, surgical | operations, counselling sessions, | safe births and much more. Each | of these is an interaction between | an MSF staff member and a | patient; each involves an act of | caring between one person and | another; each brings healthcare to | people in need and helps save lives. | Thank you for deciding to support | MSF. By doing so, you are joining | our team. Welcome.” | Dr Javid Abdelmoneim | Chair of MSF UK | CONTENTS | WAR AND CONFLICT | A Providing medical care to people | wounded or forced to flee their | homes because of war | NATURAL DISASTERS | Acting fast after an earthquake, | flood or hurricane to start saving | lives as soon as possible | DISEASES AND EPIDEMICS | Using our expertise to treat people | infected by deadly diseases and | stop epidemics in their tracks | ACCESS TO HEALTHCARE | Providing medical care to people who | are unable to see a doctor because | of who they are or where they live | LOGISTICS | Making it all happen, so that our | medical teams have the transport, | equipment and medicines they need | MSF IN NUMBERS | Where our funding comes from | and how we spend it | SUPPORTER PROMISE | Your support means we can operate | independently, without having | to wait for media interest or | government funding | A newborn baby is cradled by an MSF midwife in Gogrial, South | Sudan. Antenatal care and safe births are a key feature of many MSF | projects in South Sudan, where numbers of deaths during pregnancy | and childbirth are among the highest in the world. | Photograph © Isabel Corthier | We pride ourselves on being detonating themselves in the | neutral and impartial - middle of large gatherings of | principles which really show people. On both occasions, 50 | their value in the middle of a to 60 people died at the scene. | warzone, where they help Most of the time, you don’t know | protect our teams and the how many wounded are going to | patients under ourcare.MSF’s —_ come through the door or what | neutrality keepsourstafffrom _ state they'll be in. You just know | | 4. : C4 ON SI becoming targets, allowing that there are going to be alot of | Lee. * them to cross checkpoints them. As they arrive, we triage the | y <2 & - and provide medical care to patients, deciding whether they | f ™,"" . people on both sides of need to come to theatre | Ns . : beutliviie. straightaway, or whether they | hs els | ¢ : need to be stabilised first with | , ; y ie Our teams save lives in some blood and fluids in the | of the world’s most dangerous emergency room. | places. Yemen is in the midst of | a civil war. Since March 2015, a | i ws 4 yet Saudi and Emirati-led coalition | ‘ae has been fighting Ansar Allah | forces, resulting in bombing, | gun battles and widespread | destruction. Ordinary people | are bearing the brunt of a brutal | conflict. Many clinics and | hospitals have been destroyed, | an ) : ) Rte : : ou work as a team and you save | Se Me ae Cf while those that are still y : y | a /, : i : as many lives as you can. | “A ~ r@ functioning are in urgent need | ‘ \ of medical supplies. It’s afterwards that it hits you. | Then you realise what you've | a , ELIMA WONG IS AN just witnessed: a terrifying scene | ; “ , ANAESTHETIST WHO HAS of so many people injured, and | . DONE FOUR ASSIGNMENTS the knowledge that, somewhere | IN YEMEN quite close to you in the city, | Mass casualty incidents are awful, | and they’re not helped by the | chaos. The injured are arriving | and you don’t know when they’ ll | stop. Relatives and family | members are all in the mix. You | create space in corridors, you grab | mattresses where you can. You | keep your head, you stay focused, | From visti’ to Bens from SduthSedanto ; ee ee 50 or 60 people just lost their lives | Syria, MSF has@worked in most of the world's 7H ape ""eg ~—s in anumber of mass casualty for nothing, | warzones over the past 49 years, Our aim is, \ = ( § incidents, which is when 10 or | to provide lifesaving medical care to people \’ == 1S injrond patienitcarayeauthe | hospital in Mocha, Yemen, after surgery to | caught a violence, eee ctive of who f \ . times were in our emergency remove his right leg below the knee. Ali was | they are, what they Believe or which side trauma hospital in Aden — the injured when a landmine exploded as he | crossed a field on his way to meet friends. | of a conflict they're on. ——_ see result of suicide bombers Photograph © Agnes Varraine-Leca/MSF | , Eighteen-year-old Ali (centre) receives | | hospital all at once. The first two physiotherapy from Farouk (left) at MSF’s | THE CHRISTMAS BABY | Having worked in Yemen so many | times treating emergency trauma | patients, it was nice to branch out | into maternity care on my most | recent assignment. I was based in | Mocha, an area which has seen a lot | of fighting. We set up a tent hospital | to treat trauma injuries from | landmines and gunshots. But | almost immediately, a lot of | pregnant women with complications | during childbirth began to arrive, as | we were the only healthcare facility | within a six-hour drive. | We couldn’t send them away, so | we had to adapt. We converted | some critical care beds to a neonatal | unit and began to provide maternity | services, performing caesarean | sections and caring for premature | babies. It made me feel very proud | about the way MSF works: | SAUDI ARABIA | SUDAN | YEMEN | @ Mocha | @ Aden | ETHIOPIA | we respond to people’s real needs, | adapt and do whatever we can. | I remember on Christmas Day, | at 10 minutes past midnight, we | delivered a baby for a mother who | had come in bleeding. Her placenta | had come away from her womb | and she was in a life-threatening | condition, so we had to do an | emergency caesarean. We wrapped | the baby in one of our survival foils | and managed his feeding. It was | incredibly challenging, but we | made it work and he did well. | OMAN | Anaesthetist Elma | Wong uses ultrasound | to locate a bullet | lodged in the hip of | 30-year-old shepherd | Abdulwali in | MSF’s hospital in | Mocha, Yemen. | Photograph © Agnes | Varraine-Leca/MSF | Even our tough, old-school trauma | surgeons soon got to love having | the babies around. When you're | dealing with a lot of trauma | injuries, it’s a real boost to be | involved with the successful | delivery of a newborn. | WE COULDN'T DO IT | WITHOUT YOU | I feel compelled to keep going | back to Yemen. It’s my way of | dealing with the frustration of | seeing the headlines, of knowing | that, every day, people in Yemen | are living in a persistent warzone. | Each time I go back, the situation | is worse. The trauma injuries are | relentless, whether from gunfire | or landmines or airstrikes. | Meanwhile the health system | has completely broken down. | The surgical team | operate on a patient | at MSF’s hospital in | Mocha, Yemen, the | only health facility | to provide emergency | surgery for the | local population. | Photograph © Agnes | Varraine-Leca/MSF | That’s why MSF’s work in | Yemen is so important. We're | there helping to fill the gap, | providing trauma surgery, | running nutritional programmes | for malnourished children, | treating infectious diseases. | The sad and devastating truth | is that if we weren’t there, many | Yemenis would have virtually | no healthcare. | We couldn’t work where we're | working and do the things we're | doing without supporter donations. | Our ability to react to situations | and set up tents and hospitals in no | time whatsoever is purely because | we have the funds to do so. | So this is a huge thank you to you, | for enabling us to do this work. | It really does save lives and, if we | weren't there, I can’t imagine what | would happen to these people.” | When an earthquake strikes or a | hurricane hits, we are often the | first aid organisation on the scene. | Our skilled doctors and nurses are | trained to work in the high pressure | environment of emergencies, while our | sophisticated logistics network ensures | that our teams hit the ground with the | specialised equipment they need to | immediately start saving lives. | In the aftermath of natural | disasters, we run hospitals and | clinics providing lifesaving medical | care. We perform surgery, prevent | diseases from breaking out, provide | survivors with clean drinking water, | essential relief items and shelter | materials, and offer psychological | care to the traumatised. | On 14 March 2019, Tropical | Cyclone Idai made landfall in | southern Africa, causing | widespread devastation in Malawi, | Mozambique and Zimbabwe. | MSF emergency teams rapidly | deployed to assist teams already | on the ground. | THE FIRST SIX DAYS | Dr Marthe Frieden is the medical | team leader for MSF’s emergency | response in Zimbabwe. She was | working on an MSF pilot project | for managing diabetes and | hypertension when Cyclone Idai | hit Zimbabwe’s mountainous | Manicaland province on the | night of 15 March, causing | flooding and deadly landslides. | Nyamavhuvhw’ — the month of | wind. That’s what August, the | windiest month before the first | rains, is called in Shona, one of the | local languages in Zimbabwe. | ANGOLA ZAMBIA | MOZAMBIQUE | BOTSWANA | Yet this year, winds of more than | 200 km/hour swept down from | the mountains on the night of | 15 March, heralding the arrival | of an immense cyclone. | It knocked down trees and | turned placid streams into wild | torrents. Bridges and essential | roads crumbled before it, as did | houses and animal shelters. | Landslides raged down the | mountains, lifting up rocks and | rushing them downhill — ‘like | roaring trucks with no brakes’, | in the words of one resident. | Entire families and homes | disappeared under the seething | mud and rock, and when the earth | stopped moving what was left was | a mass grave. | Day1 | Saturday 16 March | I attend an urgent meeting at | which a sense of desperation and | helplessness grows as the extent of | the damage becomes clear. We are | in emergency mode now. We swap | our casual football shirts for | official MSF T-shirts. | The Cocoricoé female | football team help | build an MSF cholera | treatment centre in | their community, | Chingussura, in Beira, | Mozambique. Cyclone | Idai severely damaged | Beira’s health system, | damaging or destroying | all of the coastal city’s | health centres. | Photograph © Pablo | Garrigos/MSF | Day 2 | Sunday 17 March | The MSF team leaves Mutare with | the aim of delivering medical | supplies to Mutambara hospital. | After a day of muddling through | a labyrinth of collapsed bridges | and roads blocked by land and | rockslides, we realise that neither | the two main roads nor the | secondary dirt roads into the | district are accessible. The district | is completely cut off. | Day 3 | Monday 18 March | Contact is established with the | Zimbabwean army and permission | is given to erect three tents as part | ofa stabilisation centre for survivors | at a strategic point overlooking the | affected area in Chimanimani, | now known as ‘Skyline’. | A request is made to the army to | help deliver much-needed supplies | to cut-off hospitals. Members of | the local community gather in | groups to discuss strategies to | save lives of friends and family | members trapped in the | Chimanimani valley. Meanwhile, | the rains continue to pour down | and mist turns into fog, keeping | the helicopters grounded. | Dozens of deaths have already | been reported and missing person | reports are flooding in. The clock | is ticking. | Day 4 | Tuesday 19 March | The MSF emergency coordinator | arrives from Harare. A team | of young, highly specialised | Zimbabwean volunteer doctors | have seemingly popped out of | nowhere and taken possession | of our tents. | They are from an energetic | network of churches, hospitals | and the University of Zimbabwe. | Some have already been flown into | the disaster zone by helicopter. | “Welcome!” we cry. | Nurse Cecilia talks | to a patient at an | MSF mobile clinic | in Inhamizua, a | neighbourhood in | Beira, Mozambique. | Photograph © Giuseppe | La Rosa/MSF | The MSF team transfer | essential relief items, | such as soap and | blankets, to their | boat. These will be | distributed to | communities in areas of | Mozambique that were | cut off by Cyclone Idai. | Photograph © Giuseppe | La Rosa/MSF | The first patients arrive. Lying | on plastic sheeting on the floor, | patients are examined and | stabilised by doctors and nurses. | As the sky clears up, more patients | with broken bones and deep | lacerations are evacuated by | helicopter to Skyline. Private | ambulances take patients to | hospital, while MSF transports | people with less severe injuries | to Chipinge district hospital, | 50 km away. | Day5 | Wednesday 20 March | A well-known soft drinks | company gains my respect by | dragging hundreds of bottles | of drinking water to where clean | water is so desperately needed. | A fuel company comes in with | heavy equipment to reopen roads, | while mobile toilets are starting | to appear and a national life | insurance company has provided | many beds. The process of | rescuing the injured, stabilising | them and transferring them to | hospital continues, amid | deep concern for those not | being reached. | Walking into the valley isn’t an | option. The army and private | companies on site are trying to | open a direct road. Can our team | get into the district tomorrow? | Day6 | Thursday 21 March | Finally, a water tank arrives at | Skyline. The number of patients | arriving with trauma injuries | decreases rapidly. | Residents of the mountainous | area surrounding Skyline turn | up on foot in search of medication | for HIV, diabetes, hypertension | and asthma. | Some have lost their medication | to the floods, and many can no | longer access their health facilities. | We have to bridge the gap, and so | the stabilisation centre becomes a | primary healthcare unit. | A road is opened up, and our | mobile teams are now able to | access the affected areas by vehicle. | With the emergency response | ongoing, the dead being buried, | the survivors being supported | to rebuild their lives, and | infrastructure being | reconstructed, it becomes clear | to me that a bridge must be built | between the emergency response | and chronic care. | Trauma victims with fractures | or those who have sustained severe | injuries will need follow-up | care in the medium or long term, | especially those with | spinal injuries. | Our MSF teams will remain, | helping where we can.” m | After I’ve sent my teams on their | way, I start my tour with Al Noman | — my right-hand man. We walk | from one team to another. Uphill | and downhill, in a heat you never | get used to. The only thing that | helps is to drink, drink and drink | more water... We must keep going! | We support our teams whenever | they need us. We make hundreds | of phonecalls each day to stay in | touch with all the team members. | We bring them supplies if they are | getting low on vaccines. We keep | in contact with the local staff of | the World Health Organization | (WHO). We solve every problem | that crops up throughout the day. | There’s no time for lunch, so we | nibble on some nuts and | cucumbers that we buy at one of | the many market stalls. | At the end of the afternoon we | have a final meeting to evaluate | the day, before planning the | next one. | When I get home I’m tired, but I | feel lucky. Lucky, because all my | colleagues are great at what they | do and it’s a lot of fun working | together as a team. But most of all | because vaccination campaigns are | one of the few activities where you | know the impact at the end of a | day: we reached everyone we | could, and every person vaccinated | is one fewer who will get sick. | I feel very proud of what we | achieved in just seven days — | and you should feel proud too. | Your support means we have | protected thousands of people | from cholera. Thank you for | supporting MSF — without your | generosity, this amazing work | would not be possible.” | A Rohingya woman fills | containers with clean | water provided by | MSF in Jamtoli | refugee camp, Cox’s | Bazar, Bangladesh. | Photograph © Anthony | Kwan/MSF | Abdul (left) and Noor | walk along the dusty | paths of Kutupalong | refugee camp. Many | Rohingya children | lost family members | to the violence in | Myanmar and now | have to take on adult | responsibilities | and care for | younger siblings. | Photograph © Sara | Creta/MSF | Sometimes MSF staff have to go the extra //A | mile to reach patients, even if it’s through a | vast swamp. British nurse Melissa Buxton | Shares her story... | ‘T've just spent eight months | working as an outreach nurse | in South Sudan, flying | between eight small clinics. | Most are in areas soremote | that people have no access | to even basic medical care, | The remotest clinic is situated in | the southern islands — a vast | swampland with small areas of | raised ground. The islands were | previously used by fishermen but, | due to insecurity, many families | have fled there seeking refuge | from the violence. | As a result of security concerns | and annual flooding, an MSF | team had not been able to visit the | clinic for nearly a year. | There was a desperate need for us | to resupply the clinic and to treat | people. So early one morning, a | small team — two logisticians, a | midwife, a health promoter and | me — set off into the unknown. | THE JOURNEY BEGINS | Our journey began in a light | aircraft. After landing in a field | beside a small village, we faced a | three-hour trek across grasslands | and swamps to the first island. | Two MSF logisticians | make their way back | to base in Thonyor, | South Sudan, after | distributing medical | supplies and food to | more than 2,000 people | on Kok island hiding | from violence in the | Surrounding area. | Photograph © | Dominic Nahr | We had to transport camping | equipment and food for the | duration of our trip, as well as | medicine for the clinics. As there | were no roads, and the amount of | equipment and supplies was too | much for us to carry, we hired | some local women as porters to | assist us. | We set off in 45-degree heat | shortly after midday. The first | hour or so, walking over decent | terrain, was reasonably | comfortable, but with a fierce | African sun beating down and the | weight of heavy backpacks, it soon | began to get uncomfortable. Little | did we know that things were | about to get a lot worse. | ENTERING THE SWAMP | We arrived in the swamplands to | find that there were no canoes or | boats to help us across to the first | island. The swamp itself was a | murky chocolate brown colour, | teeming with wildlife. | We waded in and the water was | soon above my knees. I wondered | what lurked beneath the surface. | I couldn’t see anything. We knew | there were fish, but what else? | The porters made walking through | the swamp look easy. Most of | them were very tall and would | not have looked out of place on | a fashion catwalk. | They were greatly amused by our | less-than-refined efforts, as we | tripped and stumbled through | the reeds. | At times, the water was chest-deep | and we had to carry our backpacks | above our heads. We trekked on. | It was beginning to get dark and | we were hungry and tired, having | eaten nothing since our departure. | EXHAUSTION | I was exhausted and fell under | the water several times. One of | the local team forged ahead and | found a termite hill on a small | island. We stopped for a few | moments to catch our breath but | were soon on the move again. | We were suffering from cramps | and blisters; our morale was | low and it was getting late. | And to make matters worse, the | mosquitoes were starting to bite. | ARRIVING AFTER DARK | As we continued onwards, | I spotted a small green snake | wrapped around a reed stem. | I wondered what other creatures | might be lurking in the depths. | Finally, we arrived at our | destination in complete darkness. | We were greeted with rapturous | applause, singing and dancing, | with small excited children all | wanting to shake our hands. | It was an amazing feeling. | At the clinic, we busied ourselves | erecting our tents under moonlight | before cooking some noodles. We | couldn’t get into bed fast enough. | We were totally exhausted and | knew that the next day in the | clinic would be busy. | MSF team members | (from left to right) | Nyatini, Philippa and | James treat a sick | woman at a mobile | Clinic in Thaker, in South | Sudan’s Leer county. | Photograph © | Siegfried Modola | - are , | ot. te quence, & Ai, | In the distance, we heard the | sound of gunfire. Later we learned | that it was local people shooting | hippos. These animals, although | vegetarian, can be extremely | dangerous, and are responsible for | more human casualties than lions | and crocodiles put together. In an | area where many people die of | malnutrition, a hippo can feed | a large number of people. | RESUPPLIED AND | REASSURED | The next morning the clinic was | buzzing with women and small | children. The most common | health problems we saw were | diarrhoeal diseases, respiratory | infections and malaria. | ‘There were moments on the | journey when I questioned why | I was putting myself through all | this. But to see the appreciation | of the people who were relying | on us for medical care made me | understand that it was all so | worthwhile. | We had a long trek back to the | plane, but we were happy. The | clinic had been resupplied. The | local people who relied on it had | been reassured that their health | needs would still be met. And I | had been reminded why I had | chosen humanitarian aid and | medical care as my life’s work.” = | A doctor or nurse isn't much use in the middle | of the bush if they don't have their medicines | delivered at the right time, to the right place and at | the right temperature, Without the support of our | logistics teams, we wouldn't be able to work with | the speed and efficiency that we do. | Without supplies, | communications, water, | transport or a safe place | to sleep, our medical staff | couldn't do their jobs. | That's why one in three | of the international staff | working in our projects is | alogistician. | BIKERS WITHOUT BORDERS | In the remote east of Democratic | Republic of Congo, skilled MSF | motorcyclists face mudslides, | armed militias and vertical drops | in their efforts to reach people cut | off from medical care. | The road from Minova to Numbi is | two hours of slippery slopes, mud | and obstacles which require skill, | steady hands and steely nerves. | An MSF boat carries | essential relief items | for distribution in | Wechkoari, Ulang, | South Sudan, to help | communities affected | by the severe flooding. | Photograph © Nicola | Flamigni/MSF | MSF team members make | their way though the bush | to reach a health centre | in Lungonzo, Democratic | Republic of Congo, as | part of a mass measles | vaccination campaign. | Photograph © Pablo | Garrigos/MSF | “Logisticians are the nuts and bolts of MSF. | We stay very much behind the scenes, | facilitating almost all of the work that | MSF does. Although we aren't doctors or | nurses, I like to think we are putting the | ‘sans frontiéres' in MSF."" | Robert Malies, MSF logistician | Every day, teams of MSF bikers “There’s really no typical day here, | follow this route and ones circumstances vary enormously. | like it to transport illand pregnant Last September, a grenade blast | patients from their remote homes _ injured a dozen people in a village, | to hospitals and health centres and we had to find a way of getting | many miles away. MSF biker them all to hospital. Despite some | Akonkwa Kacihambra describes _ serious injuries, we still managed | the challenges he faces: to get everyone to safety. Later we | found out that everyone had got | better. Hearing that gave usa lot | of satisfaction. | Recently we were driving a | pregnant woman to the hospital, | but she started to have the baby. | Luckily, the guy on the support | bike had some medical experience | and we were able to help the | woman give birth. Everything | went well and I was very relieved | and proud to be able to transport | both mum and newborn safely | to hospital. | We cover a very big area, and the | terrain is harsh. Sometimes we | have to climb quite high into the | mountains, up to 2,200 metres in | altitude, to pick people up, and it | gets extremely cold. | Every situation is different and | needs a different approach. | People’s reactions vary when they | see us arrive on our bikes. Some | are very ill and, when you ask | them to get on the bike, they don’t | want to. Eventually we convince | them it’s for their own good. And | sometimes people just come out | to thank us, as they understand | that we are trying to help them | in difficult conditions. It’s not | even a choice most of the time. | It’s about saving their lives. | What motivates me in my | job is observing how | humanitarians work. | “Once we were driving through | the province when militiamen | stopped us at aroadblock. | Things got ugly and we had | to flee as they shot into the air.” | Pascal, MSF biker | I used to admire the way they a eee | worked selflessly for other people, _ a village at the centre | so, when I was given the nite ac | in Bas-Uélé province, | opportunity to sign a contract Democratic Republic | of Congo, to vaccinate | with MSF, I was very happy children against | because this gave me the chance Photograph © Diana | to do the same. I like it that we Zeyneb Alhindawi | have to put ourselves to one side | and think about how we are going | to save other people in extremely | challenging situations. Some | situations are really critical and | I know we've already helped to | transport hundreds of people | in the past six months, and | that’s fantastic.” m | MSF UK IN | NUMBERS | The one thing we can't do without is your support. Not only | does it allow us to provide medical care to people in need all over | the world, it means we can continue to operate independently, | without having to wait for government funding or answer to | any political or religious interests. | OTHER | DONATIONS | REGULAR | ol) ere | TRUSTS on cccsessssssseetsnee | WHERE | OUR MONEY | COMES FROM | APPEALS | .. GIFTS IN WILLS | CORPORATE | GIFTS | HUMANITARIAN | MIG wicca sicaiawteaciar | FUNDRAISING | For every £1 we spent | on fundraising in 2018, | we raised £7.57. | WHERE | YOUR MONEY | GOES | OFFICE | MANAGEMENT | A young patient | with a foot injury | exchanges glances | with Dr Paul van der | Laan in Bossangoa | hospital, Central | African Republic. | Photograph © | Tom Koene | We respect and value all our | supporters and will not share | your data for marketing | purposes, nor will we bombard | you with constant appeals, | We do know that words can make | a real difference for our patients | around the world, so we would | like to share stories about the | reality of our work with you. | If you would like to stop receiving | communications from us please | contact our Supporter Care team | on 020 7404 6600 or by emailing | uk.fundraising@london.msf.org | and we'll be happy to help. | Thank you for all your support. =",Mailout,NA,NA
02,"CONCERN | worldwide | Supporter Number: | 04 Marcl | A healthy baby needs a healthy mum — thi | children like Yusuf through to 2 | ENDING | EXTREME POVERTY | WHATEVER | IT TAKES | Return Address: | Concern Worldwide Delivered by BVAL MAALL | 47 Frederick Street @ | Belfast nis Royal Mail POSTAGE PAID GB | BT1 2LW b/s /e. | Saline holding | _ hernewborn | daughter Cynthia | Thank you for building | a better future for | babies like Cynthia | Return address: | Registered with ¢ | Concern Worldwide | REGULATOR 47 Frederick Street | Belfast, Bll 2LW | Registered charity numbers 1092236 (England and Wales) and SC038107 (Scotland). | Photos: Jennifer Nolan / Concern Worldwide / Kenya | » ReOY CY a thia | INSIDE: Your first building block sticker | Find out how you're giving children a healthy start in life. | Start filling your photo frame as you transform children’s lives. | concern.org.uk #throughto2 | Take a look at the family picture here. You can just see the happiness | shining out. Fatuma and her husband Abdulahi couldn’t look more | delighted with new baby Yusuf — and big brother Mohamed is bursting | with excitement! | But without Concern, Fatuma’s story might have ended in tragedy. | In this part of Kenya, drought is common, poverty is widespread and | staying healthy is a constant struggle. | Women and children are particularly vulnerable to malnutrition. And, | if a pregnant woman doesn’t get enough of the right nutrients, both | she and baby are less likely to survive birth. If the little one does live, | the chances are the child will be smaller, weaker and slower to learn. | But because supporters like you were there for us, we were there for Fatuma from the start — giving her | the advice and support she needed to bring a healthy baby into the world, as she explained to me: | “| went to the clinic and Concern visited every two weeks. The community health volunteer and the nurses | encouraged me to give birth in the health facility. We were prepared, and when my labour started | went straight | there. | was in labour for a long time, but there were no complications. The baby was healthy and was immunised | shortly afterwards.” | So, thanks to people like you, more and more families here and around the world are welcoming healthy | babies into their lives - and more mums are surviving childbirth. | And that’s just the first step of the journey. Over the next 24 months, you’ll hear about the other ways you're | helping babies to grow into healthy two-year olds with a bright future ahead of them. You'll also find out how | more parents are better able to care of their families and themselves. To mark the progress we’re making | together, we'll send stickers for you to collect and display in the special photo frame I’ve enclosed. Your first | building block sticker overleaf is a lovely picture of baby Yusuf! | | know you'll be happy to hear that little Yusuf is flourishing: “I’m feeding him and he’s putting on weight”, Fatuma | told me proudly. | hope that you too will be very proud of what you’re achieving by helping babies like him to get | off to a healthy start. | With heartfelt thanks, eS. Borause Conetr supporks Lorie Ae like Se | one hefe WW N\ouple Ww 24 of Ae usolds poorest | c= vourkties, guts lke yours halp daldron autos Ae | leloe, “T hank you for Gr“ng Som ao dnorve ond | % up | fe orn heatdoreale. | Immaculate Murgi Nthiga "" | Officer for Health and Nutrition SONG, SO Many) fon ane | Moyale, Kenya | Concern Worldwide is grateful for your support, and we would like to keep you informed about our work tackling | extreme poverty. If you prefer not to receive future communications or wish to change the way we communicate | with you, please email enquiries.info@concern.net, call 0800 032 4001 or write to us at 47 Frederick Street, | Belfast BT1 2LW | A copy of our Privacy Statement is available at: concern.org.uk/privacy Registered with | Concern Worldwide (UK) registered charity number 1092236 (England and Wales) and SC038107 (Scotland). A charitable company, FUN DRAISING | limited by guarantee, registered in England and Wales under company number 4323646. Registered address: Units 13-14, Calico T R | House, Clove Hitch Quay, London SW11 3TN. All Photography: Peter Caton/Kenya/2017 REGULA (@) | cod h | Con CERN | worldwide | Tel: 0800 032 4001 | Email: enquiries.info@concern.net | Supporter Number: www.concern.org.uk | Join the conversation at | #throughto2 | 04 March 2021 | A healthy baby needs a healthy mum - thank you for helping | children like Yusuf through to 2 | Dear Girling, | Thank you so much for helping us build a better future for children living in | extreme poverty. Your regular gift to Concern Worldwide means we can be | there for little ones on their journey to that milestone second birthday — giving | them the very best chance for a happy, healthy life. | As Concern’s Officer for Health and Nutrition, here in Moyale, Kenya, | Fatuma with her healthy | see the impact of kindness like yours every day. | see it in the smiles of New baby, Yusuf. | young mothers tenderly cradling their new-borns. | see it in the eyes of | proud fathers. And | see it in the delight of brothers and sisters too. | Every year, more families are sharing those precious moments. And more parents are watching their | children grow and thrive — because they get the care they need. That care begins before birth by making | sure women are looked after and properly nourished throughout their pregnancy. | Thanks to kind supporters like you, Concern is currently helping 13,650 mums and mums-to-be in Moyale. | We support 31 health clinics and work with 600 health volunteers to reach every home with a mother and | child. | Working in partnership with local nurses and health staff, we aim to provide every expectant mother with | regular health checks, support parents to make a birth plan, and prevent and treat diseases like anaemia. If | a mum to-be is under-nourished, we make sure she gets a nutritious porridge with all the ingredients she | needs for a healthy pregnancy. | Take a look at the family picture overleaf. You can just see the happiness shining out. Fatuma and her | husband Abdulahi couldn’t look more delighted with new baby Yusuf — and big brother Mohamed is | bursting with excitement! | Please turn over... | Your latest building block | << | | Thank you for providing crucial antenatal care and nutrition | for a pregnant mum like Fatuma, helping to keep her and her Ki | baby healthy. | Antenatal cate, | and Nufrition | Place this building block sticker in your frame to keep track of the ry le | difference you're making to children around the world. ee peak | We hope you enjoy hearing | about how you can give children | the building blocks of a healthy | life. If you have any questions, | please email: | enquiries.info@concern.net | call 0800 032 4001 | or write to us at | Donor Care, 47 Frederick Street, | Belfast, BT1 2LW | Concern Worldwide (UK) registered charity | number 1092236 (England and Wales) and | SC038107 (Scotland). | Photo of Abudho: Photo: Jennifer Nolan/ | Concern Worldwide/Kenya | www.concern.org.uk | ConceERN | worldwide | Thank you for providing the building Concern | blocks to get children through to 2 worldwide | Thank you for | helping children | through to 2 | Join the conversation | at #throughto2 | Scholastica Mbmya with | her 17-month-oeld daughtey | Francisca. Sgholastica | received suppenirom | a Concern-supponeg | Community Health | Volunteer before, UGS | and after her pregnancy | Seven-month-old | Jal at a Concern | supported health | centre in South | Sudan. | You’re giving babies | the building blocks | of a healthy life | you for your commitment to help babies | Thank you so much for | helping children through to 2 | Fatuma with her | healthy new baby, | Yusuf. | You and Concern ~-— | With the support of kind people like you, | Concern has been working with the world’s | poorest and most vulnerable people for 83 3” | almost 50 years, Working together with - | communities we combine local knowledge | and our expertise to provide practical, | intelligent solutions that save children's | lives and give them and their parents a | brighter and healthier future. | Together, we can help children in four | different ways: responding to emergencies How donations are spent* | like droughts and other natural disasters, 83.3” Overseas programmes | improving children's health by providing 12.8” Fundraising | food and vital health services, giving 3.5% Advocacy and | children the chance to have an education, CEN | and enabling parents to build livelihoods so Ore cere mnance | they can feed their children in the long term. | *Concern (UK) expenditure in 2018 | Tel: 0800 032 4001 ConceRN | 1@) Email: enquiries.info@concern.net worldwide | w www.concern.org.uk | All photography by Concern Worldwide. | Registered charity numbers 1092236 (England and Wales) and SC038107 (Scotland). | concern.org.uk | #throughto2 | 24 months of milestones — how you are helping children t | Before birth 4 months 8 months 1 year 16 months | You'll help pregnant Your support will help You'll help ensure babies You'll help ensure In addition to helping You'll help provide | mums to keep their mums throughout receive the proper babies continue to children get the care clean water to children | babies healthy. Your the birthing process, vitamins and nutrients receive the care they they need, you'll also at a time when they are | support helps provide | antenatal care, nutrition | and support for mums | to ensure that babies | get the nutrients they | need in the womb — | and give them the best | start in life. | ensuring they have the | shortly after birth. That's | why your support is so | vital to keep newborns | alive and healthy. | The first two years of a child’s | life are vital to their physical | and mental development. | Concern works in | 29 of the poorest | countries across | the world | ® Haiti | they need during the | needs — how much to | feed them, how often, | and how to get the right | combination of nutrients. | breastfeeding exclusively | for the first six months | and how and when to | introduce new foods. | HOINAD GE | You'll be there for children where help is needed most | so she can grow | need as they grow older. | help provide emergency | help save children’s lives | and prevent the long- | term consequences | of malnutrition. | especially susceptible to | appropriate medical critical first few months That includes helping food when it’s needed infection — as they begin | care. Without proper of their life. This includes parents provide the to children who are to walk confidently they | care, many babies die teaching mums about food their growing baby malnourished. Youcould come into contact with | more bugs and germs. | Your support could give | them the fresh, clean | water they need. | a _ e Turkey | e Syria | Lebanon e ye e Afg | Kali receives | f therapeutic food e Pa | up healthy. | e Niger | eChad e Republic of Sudan | One-year-old Hailmo | sss peg Sd Sierra Leone @ Central African, S°uthSudan  , Ethiopia | Khayro, being F . @ - © e | treated for severe Liberia Republic Kenva P | acute malnutrition in age ® Somalia | a Concern supported | health centre in Filtu, e Rwanda | Ethiopia. Democratic Republic of Congo e ais | urundi | Malawi | e Mozambique | hrough to 2 | 20 months | As children approach | the crucial two-year | milestone, your support | can help families build | a sustainable income. | You may help provide | agricultural training or | support families to earn | another livelihood so they | can feed their children | for years to come. | You'll help children get through to 2. Thanks to your | support, they'll have the chance of the best possible | start in life — allowing their brains and bodies to | develop so they have the all-important chance of | fulfilling their potential. Thank you for bringing | children through the most crucial time in their | young lives. | Democratic People’s Republic of Korea e | hanistan | ikistan | © Bangladesh | Your support | will take children | through to 2",Mailout,NA,NA
03,"We campaign for a fairer world, | where people with disabilities | have their voices heard. | Disability discrimination affects | millions of people around the | world. But together, we can change | this unacceptable situation. | See how you can help at | Sightsavers/Ambrose Watanda | The future | is in our hands | Every day, thousands of vulnerable people | lose their sight because, unlike those whose | stories you’ve read here, they haven’t been | able to access the medication to prevent or | cure devastating conditions. But it doesn’t | have to be this way. | With your help, we can go on working to | eliminate avoidable blindness. We're determined | to save sight where we can. And we'll also ensure | that people with irreversible sight loss and other | disabilities get the support they need to live their | lives with dignity and independence. | Thank you for being a sight saver. | can protect 50 people against | excruciating, blinding trachoma | could pay for a sight-saving | cataract operation | could pay for a short course | in inclusive education for | two teachers | www.sightsavers.org | Support | You help us provide essential Seyna bou’s story | equipment, support, and promote | equal opportunities for people with | Seynabou is an intelligent girl, but she was | falling behind at school because of her sight | permanent visual impairments and loss. One day, she heard about another, more | other disabilities so they can live inclusive, school during a talk given by its | ith i igni i , Khady. | with independence and dignity headmistress, Khady | their own communities Despite her shyness, Seynabou approached | Khady to ask if she could come to her | Sightsavers-supported school. She enrolled later | that year, and with extra support, she was soon Every penny cou nts | studying alongside children without disabilities. and for every pound we receive* | What your gifts mean... | Khady’s school is the first of its kind in Kaolack, q ° | e glasses and braille kits | Senegal. She says, ‘Sightsavers have helped us a 91% goes on vital charity work | ¢ inclusion training for teachers lot, with training, special tablets and punches for | fe) . | braille typing, and the children’s medical care.’ ? % eo | and our funding | e ensuring disability rights | www.sightsavers.org/disability “including donated supplies | Both images © Sightsavers/Javier Acebal . | www.sightsavers.org 9 | Treatment | You're helping fund the treatment | of painful eye conditions and | life-changing cataract operations. | Your gifts help provide... | e vital surgical equipment | e transport to hospital | | ¢ training for ophthalmic surgeons and | eye-care workers | Both images © Sightsavers/Tommy Trenchard | Suhura and Gordane’s story | Suhura and Gordane live in Nampala, | Mozambique. They are very much in love, | but since cataracts robbed them both of | their sight, they haven’t been able to see | each other’s faces. | At 70, Gordane was still very talented with his | hands, braiding beautiful mats, but those skills | faded with the sight loss. And sociable Suhura | would often hear parties in the distance that | she couldn't get to on her own. | Three long years after losing their sight, the | couple were both diagnosed with cataracts | during one of our home screening visits and | referred for sight-saving operations. After | surgery, they broke into song and danced with | each other in the middle of the hospital as | health workers clapped along. | With his sight restored, Gordane says he feels | “reborn” having rediscovered his crafts, and | Suhura is enjoying socialising with her friends | and family again whenever she wants. | Read Gordane and Suhura’s heartwarming | story at www.sightsavers.org/suhura | www.sightsavers.org 7 | Prevention | Through eye examinations, | preventative medication, screening | in communities and hygiene | education in schools, your support | is helping prevent painful diseases | that cause sight loss, such as | trachoma and river blindness. | Your gifts help provide... | e distribution of medication | e training of community volunteers | e education programmes to support | eye care and better hygiene | Both Images © Sightsavers/Jason J Mulikita - | Left image, Philly with her grandmother Fatuma | Philly’s story | Philly is a little girl with a big smile. But at | just five-years-old, she was already suffering | with trachoma - a horrific infection that | causes excruciating pain and sight loss. | Philly's trachoma was diagnosed by a Sightsavers- | trained doctor who was visiting her community | in Tanzania. Unable to afford her treatment | themselves, Philly’s parents feared she would lose | her eyesight completely, and with it, her future. | Thanks to help from kind supporters like you, | Philly received the treatment she needed | through Sightsavers. Her eyes are now perfectly | healthy. Philly’s grandmother Fatuma said, | “We feel so blessed to have this opportunity. | We are very lucky that you helped us.” | © Our aim | is to eliminate the suffering and sight loss | caused by trachoma in countries where we | work. And this goal is now closer than ever - | Ghana recently became the first country in | sub-Saharan Africa to eliminate the disease! | Read a first-hand account of the work you’re | supporting at www.sightsavers.org/trachoma | www-.sightsavers.org | of blindness and visual | impairment can be | cured or prevented | %. Fatuma is now free of painful, blinding | “trichiasis, following an operation | provided'by a Sightsavers team in | Ruangwa, Tanzania. | Read more about Fatuma’s story at | www.sightsavers.org/fatuma | Source: http://atlas.iapb.org/global-burden-vision-impairment | Left and cover image © Sightsavers/Jason J Mulikita | Together we can restore more lives | Thank you for being a sight saver and supporting our | life-changing work. We're so grateful to have you with us. | We've been working with local communities and our amazing | partners in hospitals, clinics and outreach projects for more | than 70 years, to restore sight and support families living with | sight loss and disability. And our work is possible thanks to the | generosity of people like you. | We've achieved so much - but there’s still more to do. In fact, | 75% of blindness or visual impairment can be prevented or cured | when people have access to the treatment they so desperately | need. Read on to find out how vital your support is, and how | you’re helping to transform lives. | (ade tlespU/ | Dr Caroline Harper, Chief Executive | million | eye surgeons, | eye-care workers, | community volunteers | and specialist | teachers trained | million | people treated with | trachoma antibiotics | www.sightsavers.org | Feeling inspired? | Keep up to date with all the amazing | work you’re supporting by signing up | to our online newsletter at: | www.sightsavers.org/news | £ SightsaversUK | @Sightsavers | ) SightsaversTV | Our promise to you | Your support is crucial for people around the | world with avoidable blindness, which is why | we take our relationship with you very seriously. | © Sightsavers/Ruth McDowell - Children from | Beposo, Ghana where Sightsavers is educating the | Read our supporter promise: www.sightsavers.org/promise community on Neglected Tropical Diseases (NTDs | Se eHEATE Sightsavers d , | Sightsavers id | All statistics correct as of May 2020. Registered charity numbers 207544 and $C038110. | WPL2020 | Bumpers Way isi | ‘S Sig htsavers Bumpers Farm | Chippenham SN14 6NG | +44 (0)1444 446 600 | info@sightsavers.org | www.sightsavers.org | Dea 04/03/2021 | Thanks for being a sight saver! | You've done something really amazing. Today, because of you, someone in the world can | see once more. They could be a child, teenager, a parent or grandparent. Your kindness | and generosity in supporting Sightsavers touches many people’s lives, young and old, | giving them reassurance against disease, relief from pain, and the joy of regaining their | sight. | We're really grateful that you decided to support us with a regular gift, and you can | rest assured that your monthly donations will be put to good use. Please see the | reverse of this letter for further details about your gift. | Your ongoing support makes a huge difference to what we can achieve. You enable us to | reach some of the world’s poorest people, and give them urgent protection and treatment. | For those whose sight can’t be restored you offer a lifeline, by ensuring they receive the | help they need to live with dignity and independence. | Thank you so much for helping us work towards a world where no one is blind from | avoidable causes. | Yours sincerely, | : % et hiJupp | Becki Jupp | Sightsavers | If you would like to change how you hear from us in the future, please get in touch on 01444 446 600 | Registered charity numbers 207544 and $C038110 Ej SightsaversUK W @Sightsavers (9 SightsaversTV | Confirmation of your Direct Debit instruction: COREE! | Debit | Thank you so much for setting up your regular donation by Direct Debit. You'll find all | the information below. | Account name | Account number Sort code | Collection day First collection | Debit amount | For your information, the collections will be made using the following reference: | Service User Number Reference number | If any of the above details are incorrect please call us as soon as possible on 01444 | 446 600 or email us at info@sightsavers.org. | However, if your details are correct you need do nothing and your Direct Debit will be | processed as normal. You have the right to cancel your Direct Debit at any time. A | copy of the Direct Debit Guarantee is below. | DIRECT | The Direct Debit Guarantee Debit | e This guarantee is offered by all Banks and Building Societies that accept instructions | to pay Direct Debits. | e If there are any changes to the amount, date or frequency of your Direct Debit, | Sightsavers will notify you 10 working days in advance of your account being debited | or as otherwise agreed. If you request Sightsavers to collect a payment, confirmation | of the amount and date will be given to you at the time of the request. | e If an error is made in the payment of your Direct Debit by Sightsavers or your Bank or | Building Society, you are entitled to a full and immediate refund of the amount paid | from your Bank or Building Society. If you receive a refund you are not entitled to, you | must pay it back when Sightsavers asks you to. | e You can cancel a Direct Debit at any time by simply contacting your Bank or Building | Society. Written confirmation may be required. Please also notify us. | Return Address | Sightsavers | Bumpers Way | Bumpers Farm | Chippenham | SN14 6NG | Registered Charity Numbers 207544 and SC038110 | WwP0201 | Account Postage GB’ D Delivered by | ‘S Sightsavers Aree 2 | N/3/2) | SEPP Pee eee eee eee eee eee eee eee eee eee ee ee eee eee eee eee eee ee eee | BERL eee eee eee ee eee ee eee eee eee ee eee eee eee eee eee eee ee es! | See eee eee eee eee eee eee eee ee eee eee eee eee eee eee eee!",Mailout,NA,NA
04,"v4 Save the Children | Your Supporter Number is | March 2021 | Dear | If | close my eyes and clear my mind | can almost hear them. Listen! Those are the giggles of gleeful little children giving the | whole world a great thumbs up. And that picture on the front of the envelope — that’s what hope looks like. By supporting | Save the Children with a regular gift, that’s what you are giving — hope, and a childhood, to the millions of children around | the world who don’t have one. | I’m delighted that you were inspired to join us. Your monthly gift of £3 will save children’s lives. | know you have used your | voice to support our campaigns in the past, so I’m writing to say thank you so much for supporting us in this new way, you | really are amazing. | Right now, as you read my words, a mother enjoys her first unforgettable cuddle with her tiny new born baby. Because of | your support, we can ensure that her baby’s first day is the first of many. Somewhere else in the world, perhaps in a Save the | Children mobile health clinic, a health worker brings a severely malnourished child back from the brink of death. Thanks to | your generosity, we can prevent that child from dying. And in some of the toughest places on earth, your compassion means | we can deliver life-sustaining supplies to frightened children and their families. | Quite simply, without you and others like you, we would not be able to reach the children who so desperately need us. So | | want to share with you some of the most moving stories I’ve heard, directly from children whose lives have been protected | and transformed thanks to the support of people like you. | hope the stories of baby Shwe Khin Phyo, Zakaria, Fatima, Sharaf, | Jeny and Jenisha in the leaflet enclosed will remind you of the difference you are now making. | In the meantime, my team and | love hearing from our supporters — so if there is anything at all you'd like to ask us about, | then please do give us a call on 020 7012 6400 or email us at: supporter.care@savethechildren.org.uk. | David, you are the beating heart of Save the Children. Your regular gifts will power our life-saving programmes across the | world. Your commitment will protect children from danger. Your trust in Save the Children will be matched by our own | unceasing efforts to ensure that all children — no matter where they are born — have the chance to grow up and fulfil their | potential. | With my deepest thanks, | (Nn. | Katherine Ayres, Supporter Care | P.S. We can’t wait to share our success stories with you, because after all they do not belong to us — they are yours. | As a reminder I’ve enclosed a lovely mural painted by a little girl in Cox’s Bazar. Pin it on your fridge at home, or your | noticeboard at work, or even keep it in your wallet. Carry it with you and in your heart — because this is what saving a | child’s life feels like. | Save the Children UK Telephone +44 (0)20 7012 6400 Save the Children Fund is a registered company limited by guarantee (Company No. 178159). | ’ Registered Charity No. 213890 (England & Wales) and Scotland (SC039570) | 1 St. John’s Lane Fax +44 (0)20 7012 6963 Registered office: 1 St. John’s Lane, London EC1M 4AR. | London EC1M 4AR savethechildren.org.uk Patron: Her Royal Highness The Princess Royal; Chief Executive: Kevin Watkins | B00000861.16 | S1p|IYD ay2 BADS/o.107 DIsny -ydDubo | 7. = ' i | It's fantastic that you’ve agreed that your gifts can be Maced as ~ £ | Gift Aid donations. You.can.find an explanation of how the ~ ges | scheme works below. _—, wy | Thank you for agreeing to a Gift Aid Declaration on 03/03/2021. This is to confirm that you want Save the Children | to treat all donations you have made for the last four years and all donations you make in future as Gift Aid | donations, until you notify us otherwise. | Please note, for each tax year covered by this declaration, if you pay less Income Tax and/or Capital Gains | Tax in the current tax year than the amount of Gift Aid claimed on all your donations, it is your responsibility | to pay any difference. | Save the Children will reclaim 25p of tax on every £1 you have given. | You are entitled to cancel your Gift Aid Declaration at any time up to 30 days from receiving this letter | without any tax being claimed by us during this period. | Your additional support will help to fund Save the Children’s vital work across the world. | Want to change how you hear from us? | If you'd like to talk to us about our work reaching every child, or simply need to start, stop or change | how you hear from us, including by post, please call our Supporter Care team on 0207 012 6400 or email | supportercare@savethechildren.org.uk. | We're committed to protecting your personal information. For more about how we collect, use and look after your | details, pelase read our full privacy policy at www.savethechildren.org.uk/privacy | Registered with | FUNDRAISING | @ REGULATOR | 25616 LT1 | ew gt Ft, or if you'd like | OUR PROMISE TO YOU. | Your support helps save and transform the lives of children in crisis all over the world. Without you, we simply | wouldn't be able to reach every child in urgent need. That's why, we promise to be open and transparent about | the way we will reach you and look after your details: | * your kind donations will always help children in the most effective way possible. | * you'll receive some of the amazing stories of how your support helps transform children’s lives. | * your privacy will always be respected, and we will make it clear and easy for you to choose how you hear from us. | * your personal details will never be sold. | For more information about our promise to you, please visit www.savethechildren.org.uk/supporterpromise | Want to change how you hear from us? | If you'd like to talk to us about our work reaching every child, or simply need to start, stop or change how | you hear from us, including by post, please call our Supporter Care team on 0207 012 6400 or email | supportercare@savethechildren.org.uk. | We're committed to protecting your personal information. For more about how we collect, use and look | after your details, please read our full privacy policy at www.savethechildren.org.uk/privacy | If you have a guest ion | about any aspect of your | to find out more about | Save the Children, don’t | hesitate to get in touch! | Katherine Ayres | Supporter Care | Save the Children | Tel: +44 (0)20 7012 6400 | Email: supporter.care@savethechildren.org.uk | www.savethechildren.org.uk | | £| facebook.com/savethechildren Ww twitter.com/savechildrenuk | Registered with | FUNDRAISING | @ REGULATOR | 4 Save the Children | Save the Children does whatever it takes — | every day and in times of crisis — transforming | children’s lives and the future we share. | The Save the Children Fund is a charity registered in England and Wales (213890) ond Scotland (SC039570) | Thank you! | 654322. A | Khin Phyo is all smiles | Baby Shwe. | Dear David, | Thank you so much for supportingjus. Your gift of £3 will help save children’s lives. | I've put together some of my favourite stories and photos so you can see for yourself | the difference your generosity helps to make! | Let me start with little Shwe Khin Phyo, in Myanmar. Her mother, Khin May Thwe, | received help from Save the Children during the highly important first two years of her | child’s life. She received about £8 a month to support her food costs, learned about the | importance of breast feeding and was encouraged to attend antenatal classes, sessions | about healthy eating and to vaccinate Shwe Khin Phyo. | Sadly, in Myanmar a third of families face poverty. But as you can see from Shwe Khin | Phyo’s brilliant smile, your support can make all the difference! Thank you again. | Katherine | The Save the Children Team | 0000086 | 4 CAN MAKE! ES | | A Tiny Alix was treated for | severe malnutrition in a | Save the Children supported | clinic in drought-ridden | Somaliland. His mother, | JUST LOOK AT THE Z | DIFFERENCE |/ile | Rahma, said she was ‘very | happy because he is | receiving treatment and his | health is getting better’. f . | “BOTH HE AND HIS MOTHER ARE DOING WELL | NOW. THE DOCTORS REALLY DID GREAT WORK | IN SAVING BOTH OF THEM.” | Zakaria’s father, Farah | After a difficult and painful labour, Zakaria was | safely delivered by trained midwives at a Save | the Children supported hospital in Somalia. | When he was born, Zakaria wasn’t breathing, | but thanks to the swift action of the medical | staff and the resuscitation equipment they had | to hand, Zakaria pulled through, much to the | delight of his proud father Farah. | Zakaria, hewbhorn | UaspIIND 2p 2ADS/UPIOH GY 220d | Fatima* attends a Save the Children Child | Friendly Space set up for families fleeing Syria’s | brutal conflict. Child Friendly Spaces are set up | immediately after an emergency to provide a | safe and nurturing place for children to learn, | play and receive counselling. The Child Friendly | Spaces help children like Fatima to come to | terms with what they have seen or experienced. | Child Friendly Spaces let children be children | again. | | UNOD :or044 | 47 <n 7 | Photo; Colin Crowley/Save thai@iidren \ | Sharaf’s education was cut short when his village | in South Sudan. came under fierce attack. He and | his mother fled to Doro refugee camp where | Sharaf now attends the camp school run by Save | the Children. Sharaf is getting on really well and | is learning to read and write. He has also started | VO-PHYD ayy aAns/fispmous | to study English. | PUYD oy Ans/fiaymosy URED sonoyy | Jeny and Jenisha, 5, with | their mother Bishnu | UaUpIIYD aUp BADG/SWOF}Y UDINDUOT :0204g | Sharaf. 12 | Mother, Bishnu | Five-year-old twins Jeny and Jenisha managed to | escape their home as it was collapsing when a massive | earthquake hit Nepal. The family managed to find | shelter but had nothing other than the clothes they | were in. Thankfully, Save the Children had teams | nearby. Jeny and Jenisha were given fresh clothes, | blankets and hygiene necessities, like toothbrushes. | We also set up latrines, health centres, and safe | spaces for children to learn and play. | *Names have been changed to protect identities. | ~ eye | This beautiful mural was painted by | Anwara* who takes port in an art | programme facilitated by Save the | Children in a refugee Came in Cov's | Barar, Bangladesh. in one of the | most deprived places on Earth, a child | Can Shil imagine, and drow beauty . | Please Reep this as @ reminder of how | much Your Support and kindness | matters 4p childcen aArevrd the world. | v4 Save the Children | Registered with | The Save the Children Fund is a charity registered in England Photo: Kristiana Marton / Save the Children FUNDRAISING | and Wales (213890) and Scotland (SC039570) *Name has been changed to protect identity REGULATOR | 654322_F | \-32905_0E1 | ¥ If undelivered please return to: Save The Children UK, 1 St John’s Lane, London, EC1M 4AR | Registered charity England and Wales (213890) Scotland (SCO39570) | REGULATOR | Registered with | Crrecycle (FR) FUNDRAISING | “ROYAL MAIL | Royal Mail POSTAGE PAID GB | HQ1984 | ad 3 | sesesesesesese’ | : Ue o% Children | - .— e. Wher $3 £3 38 | memmmmeghes/Save the Children % 4 , .",Mailout,NA,NA
05,"CARE International UK | 89 Albert Embankment | London, SE1 7TP | tel +44 (0) 20 7091 6000 | fax +44 (0) 20 7582 0728 | - email info@careinternational.org | Ca re www.careinternational.org.uk | Defending dignity. | Fighting poverty. | Reference: | 10 March 2021 | Dear © | Your support means the start of something wonderful and a future to look forward to for some of the | world’s poorest people. Thank you so much for choosing to give to CARE International. On behalf of | the families you will help, we extend a very warm welcome and are delighted to have you with us. | When you agreed to start a regular gift of £3.00 monthly, | thought how motivating and inspiring it is to | our teams working in the field that someone in the UK cares enough to help. | In emergency situations we are often the first to arrive providing the basics people need - food, water | and a safe place to sleep. And once the crisis is over, we stay to rebuild people’s lives for the longer | term, like in Nepal where we are helping families whose lives were ripped apart by the earthquake in | 2015 to build safer homes and restore their livelihoods. | If you have agreed to hear from us, you will soon begin to receive updates including stories of the | successes made possible through the support of people just like you. We are a learning organisation | and whilst we have over 70 years of experience behind us, the world is ever changing and we are | constantly looking for ways in which to adapt, so we’ll make sure to be honest with you about how we | are progressing. And from time to time we'll be in touch about other ways in which you could help to | reach even more people. | We know that poverty can be beaten and lives can change for the better, we see it every day. But we | also know that it can only happen with the support of people like you. Please don’t hesitate to contact | us with any questions or suggestions; we love hearing from our supporters. Thank you once again. | With best wishes, | (en Sline ~ 1eten | Caroline Tatum | Supporter Care Team | Tel: 020 7091 6100 Email: supportercare@careinternational.org | PS. Please turn over for important information on the set-up of your Direct Debit. | Registered Charity No. 292506 * Private Company Limited by Guarantee No, 1911651 | Registered in England and Wales | IMPORTANT: | Confirmation of the set up of your Direct Debit Instruction, including future donation details. | Thank you very much for deciding to support CARE International UK with a regular Direct Debit. This | letter is confirmation of your Direct Debit details, and is for your information. Payments will be debited | on or around the «DD_Day_Nth» of the month. Our Originator Identification Number i: | Please check that the information below is correct, including your donation details. | Account name: Account number: | Bank sort code: Amount to be debited: . | Frequency: monthly Date of first donation: | If any of the above details are incorrect, please call us as soon as possible and preferably within 14 | days on 020 7091 6100. However, if your details are correct you need do nothing and your | Direct Debit will be processed as normal. | You can make changes to your Direct Debit at any time by contacting us on 020 7091 6100. We will | process any changes you wish to make immediately, and send you confirmation. The changes will | take effect the next time a donation is due. You are able to cancel your Direct Debit at any time. A | copy of the direct debit guarantee is shown below. | Data Protection | Information provided by you will be used to process your donations and to send you information on | how you can further support CARE International’s projects and activities. If you have any questions | about how CARE works, your gifts or the information you receive about our work then please do call | us on 020 7091 6100 during office hours or email supportercare@careinternational.org. | This guarantee should be retained by the Payer | THE DIRECT DEBIT GUARANTEE (please retain for your records) Debit | e This Guarantee is offered by all Banks and Building Societies that take part in the Direct Debit Scheme. The | efficiency and security of the scheme is monitored and protected by your own Bank or Building Society. | e lf the amounts to be paid or the payment dates change CARE International UK will notify you 10 working days | in advance of your account being debited or as otherwise agreed. | e If an error is made by CARE International UK or your Bank or Building Society, you are guaranteed a full and | immediate refund from your branch of the amount paid. | e You can cancel a Direct Debit at any time by writing to your Bank or Building Society. Please also send a copy | of your letter to us. | CARE International UK | . 9th Floor | 89 Albert Embankment | a London, SE1 7TP | Defending Dignity. tel 020 7091 6100 | satiate init email supportercare@careinternational.org | www.careinternational.org.uk | Reference | 10 March 2021 | Thank you for sending real help, right now. | Dear Supporter, | Because of you, we’re creating a world where people can live in dignity and safety, no matter where they | are or what they've been through. Thank you. | By the time you finish reading this letter, nearly 20 people will have been forced from their homes because | of conflict or persecution. They will have run for their lives, leaving behind everything they've ever known | - many will only have the clothes on their backs. | Working at CARE International, I’ve seen for myself the devastating impact that conflict can have. How | women and men are forced to make decisions that no one should have to make. And how people like me | and you can become refugees overnight. | But I have also seen the a | difference that CARE’s work can ie aad | make to people’s lives. I know in Jordan with | that - thanks to your support - CARE package. | we're providing the help people | need, when they need it the most. | In the booklet I’ve enclosed, you | can meet Mubila, Precious and | Dana, whose stories show a snap | shot of CARE International's work. PO SWEE “hy | These three amazing people have Ce | all been through so much and at | times their stories can be hard to SWEETRBNTER WTTH 2 | read. But I wanted to share their | experiences with you because | they are the people behind the | @CARE/Marcus Rhinelander | Please continue... | statistics. And they are proof that you're improving lives by sending real help, right now. | Thanks to your generosity, we’re responding to humanitarian disasters like the refugee crisis in Bangladesh | and the conflict in Syria. We’re protecting and caring for vulnerable women and children. We’re providing | emergency shelter, water, food and medical care to people with nothing. And when the immediate danger | passes, we'll stay there to help communities recover from the trauma they've faced. | With your gift, you’re also helping us to end poverty. Through our projects in Africa, Latin America, the | Middle East and Asia, we’re empowering women and girls to take control over their lives. We're giving | them education, skills and opportunities they need to earn an income and lift themselves out of poverty. | When one woman is helped, her family and community are helped too. Healthier women have healthier | children, and educated women are more likely to be able to send their children to school and be financially | independent. | And to make sure we create lasting change, we campaign to raise awareness of the plight of refugees and | people living in poverty. Thanks to you, we'll continue to fight injustice and speak out for those in need. | Our work is made possible by supporters like you, and we would love to know your reasons for choosing to | give to CARE. Please let us know by using the enclosed reply slip. Alternatively, you can call or email me | directly. My contact details are below. I would love to hear from you. | On behalf of everyone at CARE, thank you for supporting our work. | My very best wishes | ( ae line | Caroline Tatum | Senior Supporter Care Officer | CARE International UK | Direct line: 020 7091 6100 | Email: supportercare@careinternational.org | Thank you from the children of Sakonzeka Village, | Malawi. CARE worked in the village to improve farming | productivity so now people aren’t going hungry. | Dana's story | Before the conflict in Syria, Dana | and her husband were happy. They | had their own flat, good jobs and | their children went to school. | But all that changed when the | fighting reached their town. There | were daily bombing raids. Dana was | forced to quit her job as a teacher. | Her eight-year old daughter, Basma, | was banned from going to school. | Then one day, Dana’s husband was | kidnapped. | ip rural Zimbabwe, the burden of | collecting water falls to women and | girls. Children like 9-year-old Precious | in Chivi District who used to face long, | dangerous journeys just to collect | enough water for her and her family. | Twice a day, every single day, she | walked one hour down an isolated track | to the river and carried it back up a hill | to her home. | Every step of the way, Precious felt | scared. There are crocodiles in the | river and snakes on the hill. And | without access to clean water, families | had no choice but to rely on rivers | that often carry deadly disease. | Precious often got sick from drinking | the water meaning she would miss | days at school. | But thanks to supporters like you, | we've been able to provide more | than 40,000 people in the Chivi | District of Zimbabwe with secure, | clean water. And Precious’s village is | one of those that has benefited from | a borehole being built. Now Precious | only needs to collect water once a | day, from a borehole much closer | to home and the water is clean and | safe to drink. | “I am so happy to have | seen our water coming out. | I feel happy. There is now | water in our village.” | 1945 | CARE is founded in the USA so that | individuals can send packages of | food and supplies - known as ‘CARE | packages’ - to families in Europe | recovering from World War 2. | 1950-1970s | CARE expands into Asia and | Africa, responding to emergencies | in Biafra, Vietnam, Cambodia and | Bangladesh, and staying on to | help rebuild affected communities. | 1980s | CARE International is founded by | CARE USA, Canada and Germany. The | UK joins in 1985. Major famine relief | work is launched across Ethiopia, | Somalia and Sudan. | A brief | history | of CARE | 2000s | Committed to | fighting poverty and | responding quickly | to emergencies, | CARE becomes one of | the world’s leading | humanitarian charities. | In the Hoar region of Bangladesh, many | women make money from raising poultry | but sadly chickens often drown in the | increasingly frequent, severe flooding. | CARE began helping women switch from | rearing chickens to ducks. Ducks can | Survive the wet seasons and flooding, | provide fat and protein-rich eggs and | meat, and are also a good income | - source. Sometimes, the simplest ideas | High in the Ecuadorian | mountains, people are often | surrounded by fog and mist | — but have no water for | drinking and washing. | CARE found an ingenious | solution for harvesting water | from the mist. The system | can collect up to 200 litres | of water, which is then | CARE saves millions | of children’s lives by | contributing to a decade of | unprecedented immunisation | programmes. We also set up | the first Village Savings and | Loan Associations. | ) are the best. filtered for use in the home. | Thanks to A hee like you, | CARE now works in almost 80 poor | and developing countries, saving | lives and delivering lasting change. | We focus on empowering women | and girls to lift their families and | communities out of poverty. | Our vision for the future | By 2020 CARE and our partners will support | 150 million people from the most vulnerable and | excluded communities to overcome poverty and | social injustice. | careinternational.org.uk | We will always respect your privacy; W We will share stories of how your | For every raised we spend at least | if you prefer not to be contacted by support is helping to change lives | on fighting poverty and just certain methods please let us know for the better | on fundraising | Y We will always protect your data; we We will be honest with you; if we | and su ort will not sell or share your personal face challenges or make a mistake | pp details with any other organisation. we will let you know, we are always | Y We have a Supporter Care team who learning | are dedicated to you, we always Y Weare accountable to the people | love to hear from you we reach through your support | ©CARE/Peter Caton .. | Mubila’s story | CS her divorce in 2011, | and with very little education | behind her, Mubila from Zambia | struggled to support her three | children on her own. | Mubila got her first loan from | Lendwithcare in 2015. Through t | initiative from CARE International, | people in the UK can make a loan | from just £15 to an entrepreneur | in one of the world’s poorest | communities - enabling them to | grow and develop their own | business, and work their own | way out of poverty. | Mubila used the loans to expand her | small business selling fruit and now | sells vegetables, small dry fish and | other food. She is making a good | profit and can support her family. | “I use my profits to feed | my children and pay for their | school fees. I’m grateful to | Lendwithcare for the loan. | It feels good to run my own | business.” | Visit Lendwithcare.org to | find out more. | Because of your support, | we are providing | emergency help to families | inside Syria, as well as | those who have fled to | countries such as Jordan, | Turkey and Serbia. | To find out more about the | difference you're making, visit | You're sending real help, right now. | Our teams are on the ground today, responding to the devastation | caused by conflict, natural disasters and the global refugee crisis. We are | delivering safe water, emergency food, shelter and vital medical care to | people whose lives have been turned upside down. | With your support, we will continue to work in these countries long | after the media spotlight has turned away. And through our long-term | development projects we will work with communities, helping them to | rebuild their lives and livelihoods. | To cut poverty off at its root, our work focuses on women and girls. | We give them the skills and opportunities they need to provide for their | families and transform the future for their children and their community. | Thank you so much for your generosity. | These children'in Zimbabwesare eating | better thanks to the farming skills thei | grandmother has gained from CARE. | OF, “sal | "" 4 2 * | oe "" a | , oi | You are sending real help now. | Thank you David | OCARE/AlalfajHolmbe | ‘a Js ad | On the cover: CARE Bangladesh Country Director Zia Choudhury with a little girl in one of the | makeshift camps in Cox’s Bazar for people fleeing horrific violence in Myanmar. | I support CARE International because... | We would love to know your reasons for choosing to support CARE International, | so please complete and return this slip to us today. | Thank you — | for alee, ‘ | ars. * | i : | | Two friends at the Chamishku camp for displaced | | people in Northern Iraq. CARE is working with | local Iraqi organisations in the camp to provide | clean ‘drinking water to people who live there. | If undelivered, please return to: ~ , | CARE International UK, 9th Floor, 89 Albert Embankment, London, SE1 7TP | CARE International UK is a registered charity in England and Wales (292506) | Delivered by | whist! | Royal Mail | C9 10002 | pA Ueda cacdgfytongg ago thafy ATP aafANfLafoaf dpe | Hs HeslocesfucalleuadefbecsaslHiT HHfhs Halli acd ilstech | FAR E|PERUREELEpAE,Aapp AEE aig PALMAR i | : : ts: : stered with 2: 2! ; | A DG Ei | All the items in this pack have been printed on paper from sustainably managed forests. Please recycle thi path after use. T | avid - right now, lives are being improved because of you. 1 | hank you so much for your support.",Mailout,NA,NA
07,"cord 4 x | “ie (2) ,. Concern | - worldwide | Tel: 0800 032 4001 | Email: enquiries.info@concern.net | www.concern.org.uk | Join the conversation at | Supporter Number: #throughto2 | 06 May 2021 | Dear | APIMPHOM UWe2U0D JO} | SO1N}DIg SOUR / 49107 BION :0}04g | Thank you for helping to bring healthy, happy babies into the world. | I want to thank you from the bottom of my heart. You’re helping mums living | in extreme poverty to give birth safely and making sure that their newborn | babies get the care they need. | Diamond with her baby Patience | As you know, your kind regular gifts to Concern Worldwide play a part in | providing children with building blocks for a healthy future. The first two years of life are so special. By | ensuring little ones get the nutritious food and healthcare they need during this crucial time, you’re saving | and transforming lives. | You may remember a recent letter from my colleague in Kenya, Immaculate Murgi Nthiga, where she explained | how you’re helping pregnant women eat healthily so that their unborn babies develop properly. But that’s | just the beginning. In my role as a nurse training mums and their communities to bring up healthy children in | Liberia, I see first-hand how your support is helping to shape the next step of a child’s precious journey. | Babies are at their most vulnerable at birth and in the first seven days of life. That’s why your continued | support is so crucial. It means that during labour, birth and that all-important first week of life, both mums | and newborns get the healthcare and support they need. | Sadly, in the world’s poorest countries, many women miss out, because they simply can’t get to a health | centre. Instead, they are forced to give birth without medical care and advice from trained professionals, or the | sanitary conditions needed to prevent infection. This puts their lives and the lives of their babies in jeopardy. | Heartbreakingly, more than two thir f newborn babies who die would have survived had they received | te healthcare at this critical time. What’s more, th r bie tin those first few days influences | their chances of growing up strong and healthy. | Please turn over... | Your next building block | Thank you for providing vital healthcare to babies during their first | week of life — the time when they are most vulnerable. | Place this building block sticker in your frame to keep track of the difference | you’re making to children around the world. | Peel here | Thanks to your commitment to help babies through to 2, we can make sure mums and newborn babies have | quality healthcare, when they need it most. | In some of world’s poorest places Concern helps expectant mums access health centres to give birth safely. | We also help ensure that clinics are clean, and have enough equipment, beds and, of course, trained staff. That | includes local midwives, nurses like me and community health workers who know how to care for mums and | babies and how to recognise complications and emergencies, so they can get them to the nearest hospital quickly. | In ways like these, your special regular support means more mums and babies around the world are thriving. | Here in Liberia, for example — where there’s a chronic lack of good healthcare and nutrition — Concern is setting | up mothers’ groups to advise new and expectant mums on good nutrition practices to help keep themselves and | their babies healthy. | “I didn’t face any complications during birth. I went to the nearby health facility for the delivery. That was the | message we received from the health workers in the village. Afterwards, there were no problems with the child or me.” | “Patience is doing well. I’ve been back to the clinic for a check-up and I don’t have any challenges with feeding | her. My mum taught me to give my child breast milk for the first six months, to wash my hands before feeding and | for me to drink sufficient water and eat extra food every day.” | It’s heartwarming to see how happy and healthy baby Patience and her mum are, thanks to the generosity | of people like you. | Please find your next building block sticker — for healthy births — attached to this letter. On it, you can see a | picture of Diamond’s beautiful baby, Patience. I hope you will place it on your photo frame and that it will remind | you that, thanks to your kind gifts, precious babies like her have been given a real chance of growing up strong, | well and happy. | Iam so grateful for your generous through to 2 support. You’re making a wonderful difference to children | and families. | Warmest wishes, | Please keep an eve out For Your neact building block | update on howl You are giving childven the best . | possible start in life. You'll hear how Youy ae | Korpo Jallah is helping babies get the nuctvition they need duving | Concern Field Assistant, Liberia dye first few months of theiv lives. Thank You. | Concern Worldwide is grateful for your support, and we hope you enjoy hearing how you can help change the lives | of people around the world. If at any time you prefer not to receive future mailing communications, please email: | enquiries.info@concern.net, call 0800 032 4001 or write to us at 47 Frederick Street, Belfast, BT1 2LW. Registered with | Concern Worldwide (UK) registered charity number 1092236 (England and Wales) and SC038107 (Scotland). A charitable company, FUNDRAISI NG | limited by guarantee, registered in England and Wales under company number 4323646. Registered address: Units 13-14, Calico REGULATOR | House, Clove Hitch Quay, London SW11 3TN. | Con ~NCERN | worldwide | Safia, holding her one-month-old son Abdifataye, being shown a healthcare | pamphlet by her Community Health Volunteer, Alinur. Concern-trained volunteers | like Alinur support mums living in Kenya to create their own birth plans. | Abdifataye was the only one of Safia’s children to be delivered at a health clinic, NAS | the rest were all born at home. Safia told us: “The difference is that it is better to <9 | give birth in hospital. I received the help I needed because a nurse was present.” ~O | Photo: Peter Caton/Concern Worldwide —- | FUNDRAISI NG Acharitable company, limited by guarantee, registered in England and Wales under company number 4323646. | Regiseredt Wits Concern Worldwide (UK) registered charity number 1092236 (England and Wales) and SC038107 (Scotland). | REGULATOR Registered address: Units 13-14, Calico House, Clove Hitch Quay, London SW11 3TN. | A healthy birth HOW YOU | Babies are at their most vulnerable at birth and in the first | week of their life. You’re helping their mums to give them | FE L Pl N G | the best possible start in life. | Your gifts are keeping newborn | babies warm | It’s essential that mums know how to keep | their newborn babies warm. We show them | the importance of keeping a baby’s head | warm, and the necessity of skin to skin | contact. These measures may sound very | straightforward but they can be critical to a | baby’s survival during this time. | BAUS /APIMP|LOM UJ82U0D/UO}ED Ja}eg :O10Ud | Seven-week-old baby Rahma is | wearing a hat to keep her warm. | You can help make sure babies are | born in hygienic conditions | A lack of cleanliness can have devastating | consequences for both mothers and babies due | to their susceptibility to infection. You’re helping | to ensure that women are able to give birth in | clean health centres with clean towels, sanitised | equipment and skilled birth attendants — who are | SCORERS TEER. FR a fora ES trained to look out for danger signs in mums. | under a tree at a Mobile Health Clinic in | a remote rural area of South Sudan. | Photo: Abbie Taylor-Smith/Panos Pictures for Concern Worldwide | Kindness like yours helps ensure i . ; aS] | that mums are well enough to care , SS | for their babies | A newborn baby has a much greater chance | of being healthy if their mum is well enough to | look after them. Because of Concern, women | are able to access much-needed postnatal | care. This includes assessing and treating | PeYD/epiMpyOM UWs9DU0D/WeYxo}g A2N7 :o}OUg | et : ‘ 40-day-old Bahar’s mum Ache told us: “Through | complications, supporting breastfeeding and Concern’s Community Health Volunteers, I learnt that | promoting good nutrition. when you have a newborn baby you cannot give it any | water, but only the breast exclusively for six months.” | THK N/EIY oU | Return address: Supporter Care, 47 Frederick Street, Belfast, BT1 2LW | Registered charity numbers 1092236 (England and Wales) and SC038107 (Scotland). | Building Block 2. Photos: Nora Lorek/Concern Worldwide/Liberia. | Crrecycle | Baby p {tien : | SVEN CO Aa Dai : | Appily Sleeping | INSIDE: Your next building block to show your support | Plus your exclusive update on how you're helping women | give birth safely to healthy, happy babies. | concern.org.uk #throughto2 | (SIs. | CONCERN | worldwide | Supporter Number: | ENDING | EXTREME POVERTY | WHATEVER | IT TAKES | cord 4 | 06 May 2021 | Return Address: | Concern Worldwide Delivered by | 47 Frederick Street a | Belfast POSTALSORT [ereIEy | BT1 2LW = | Thank you for | helping mums bring | their babies safely | into the world",Mailout,NA,NA
08,"Rose’s community liveat | the mercy of the weather. _ | You can change that. phi | % \ t j. | May 2021 | The extreme weather has left Rose struggling to survive. | But today you can help | her take a stand. | Dear’ | When Rose was a young girl, she remembers her village being green and full of life. There was always plenty to | eat. But these days, the landscape of Kitui, Eastern Kenya, looks very different. The ground is cracked and dry. | Months pass by without any rain. When the rain finally comes, it pours, creating flash floods. This climate chaos | is disastrous for Rose and her neighbours who rely on farming to survive. With no reliable source of water, crops | die, animals die, and families go without food. | Climate chaos has left communities like Rose’s in grave danger. Will you give £5 today, and help | them withstand this crisis? | We can’t make it rain. But we can help protect every last drop of the rainwater that does fall. By | sending a gift today, you could help us work with our local partners in Kenya to build an earth dam. re) | This is a basin dug into the ground which collects and stores rainwater effi ciently, so that it can be 8 6) Yo | piped into taps for the community. It’s a simple, but life-changing innovation. | of people in | It couldn't come sooner for Rose, who has spent years of her life walking in the baking heat to collect INE A@laulan s@clehier | water for her six grandchildren. Feeling pangs of hunger as she walks, 68-year-old Rose sets out rely on | on the six-hour round trip, which takes its toll on her in every way. Rose explains: ‘I become tired | and | have pain in my legs because of walking. When | get back from fetching water | just rest in the | evening. | have no energy to do anything else.’ | rainfall to | grow food | Rose is spending her later years in pain. Your kindness could help build a reliable water | system, setting her free from this constant strain, and giving her a safer future. | We know that dams have the power to change everything. We have already seen how transformative they | have been in nearby villages - you can read some of these joyful stories in the leafl et I've enclosed for you. | Dams allow land to be farmed, crops to grow, and families like Rose’s to eat nutritious meals filled with fresh | fruit and vegetables. | And, of course, there’s another huge reason that a stead source of water is vital right now. As the pandemic | continues, Rose and her neighbours urgently need to have enough water for handwashing to curb the spread of | coronavirus. At this crucial time, your generosity could be lifesaving. | Continued overleaf... | CHR38_1_L732546_120521\057259\114518\20f2\ | The community works together to clear | away weeds from the scorched earth. | Please send a gift today and stand with Rose, against the elements. | Rose has shown incredible resilience. She’s dedicated all of her time and | energy to caring for her grandchildren, doing all she can to give them the | best start in life. But she can only stay strong for so long. As the weather | becomes increasingly extreme and unpredictable, she feels she is fighting a | losing battle, and the fear can be overwhelming: ‘Because of climate change | and drought | worry a lot about food.’ | Rose carries the weight of the world on her shoulders. Together, we | can help her stand strong. | I'm sure you share my outrage that some of the world’s poorest people have | to face the very worst of our climate crisis. Can you imagine living with such | a scarce supply of water, and then also having to withstand the coronavirus | pandemic? No one should have to choose between vital sanitation and | feeding their family. | It's an especially worrying time. But I've seen what we can achieve when we | stand together. This Christian Aid Week, our community can rally and give | someone like Rose a water source they can depend on. We can give families | the chance to build a better life, where food grows plentifully, and no one | has to live at the mercy of the weather. | Climate chaos has left communities like Rose’s in grave danger. Will With rain so scarce, people of all | you give £5 today, and help them withstand this crisis? ages must fetch water every day. | Thank you, | Sophie Brightwell | Christian Aid Week Manager | This Christian Aid Week, you can | ane halps m2 Persouere 7 Will Wan show God's help someone like Rose stand | PS Rose says 1 beliae God gives me | love and stand we, Reso in the steuga le strong against the elements. | apyinst Climate chogs, by making a itt today? | If you would like to stop receiving information from Christian Aid by post, or you would like to change the way in which you hear | from us, please call 0333 555 1060. | Registered with | PO Box 100, London SE1 7RT ® Telephone: 020 7620 4444 seein’ Tagezrscke | Email: info@christian-aid.org © caid.org.uk actalliance (FR REGULATOR | Eng and Wales charity no. 1105851 Scot charity no. SC039150 Company no. 5171525 Christian Aid Ireland: NI charity no, NIC101631 Company no. NI059154 and ROI charity no. 20014162 | Company no. 426928. The Christian Aid name and logo are trademarks of Christian Aid. Printed exclusively on material sourced from responsibly managed forests © Christian Aid May 2021 | Photos: Christian Aid, Christian Aid/Tom Pilston J210213 | But we’re | not all in the | same boat. | As the coronavirus pandemic | continues around the world, | sanitation has never been | more important. | But for the thousands of people | still living without a reliable water | source in Kenya, handwashing | and hygiene guidance is much | harder to follow. | No one should have to choose | between watering their crops | and washing their hands. | hristian | This Christian Aid Week, \you | could give someone water and | hope of a brighter future. | to stand strong | against the elements. | Florence Muthiani, 58, now has a | farming livelihood she can depend on. | GUA Aus | va LS | Piusy AL. | 2 we | ~ma) | Lamu | a is | Lo eA te | a \ a | Eases | yer 4 | Your kindness | could help | someone like | Rose stand | strong and build | a safer future. | could help buy taps | for a dam's | waterpoint, helping | someone like Rose | access water more | easily. | could help buy | cement, building a | strong and durable | dam a community | can depend on. | Please transform | lives using this form, | £15 or donate online at: | could help teach | farmers how to plant | drought-tolerant | crops that can | survive the drought. | caid.org.uk/rain | Training | Yes, I'll stand with | someone like Rose | against the elements. | D Girling | 13 Hilly Plantation | NORWICH | NR7 OJL | C-5036474 CAM-001455 | I'll send a gift today. | | Here is my gift of This Christian Aid Week, | you can also donate online | Here’s my giftof [ |£5 [ ]e10 [_]e15  [_]othere Fis caid.org.uk/rain | or call 0333 555 1060 | We will use your donations for projects such as those featured and where we consider | the need is greatest. | My payment details | Please make your |_| cheque | postal order [_|CAF voucher* | payable to: Christian Aid. (Please do not send cash by post) *delete as appropriate. | OR debit my | |Mastercard [_|Visa [_|Maestro [_]CAF Charity Card | Card no. (Please copy the long number along the middle of your card) Start date Expiry date Issue no. | | / / | (Maestro only) (Maestro only) | : Today’s date | Signature y | | / / | 3 | Making my gift go further giftaid uw | . Our records show you have agreed to Gift Aid your donations | As a result of your action, every gift you make to Christian Aid is worth 25 per cent more, | which means your gift goes further towards helping the world’s poorest communities. | Please note that if you pay less Income Tax and/or Capital Gains Tax than the amount of Gift Aid claimed on all your | donations in that tax year it is your responsibility to pay any difference. Thank you. | Please notify Christian Aid if you: | * want to cancel this declaration | * change your name o