estro OD MasterCard 0 CAF CharityCard | Please copy the long number from the middle of your card and add the issue number if using Maestro. | er ee ee eee ee | Valid from : DT] Expiry date ‘ LT 1] Issue no. | Signature sia "" : q zi | . ""| : | f | | Detach and enclose in the FREEPOST envelope provided or post to: Freepost RUCR-XLYC-RCLY, | CAM-002256 Charity Gifts, Christian Aid, York House, Wetherby Road, York, YO26 7NH | You can also order by calling 0333 555 2025 | or by visiting charity-gifts.christianaid.org.uk | te Please quote CAM-002256 | Firstname | Surname | CJ | want to Gift Aid my donation | and any donations | make in the | future or have made in the past four | years to Christian Aid. | lam a UK taxpayer and understand | that if | pay less Income Tax and/ | or Capital Gains Tax than the | amount of Gift Aid claimed on all my | donations in that tax year it is my | responsibility to pay any difference. | Please notify Christian Aid if you: | * want to cancel this declaration | * change your home address | * no longer pay sufficient tax on your | income and/or capital gains | Today's date | DI] D |/| M MII Y Y | | Registered with | REGULATOR | | We hope you enjoy reading about how your | support is transforming the lives of people | overseas. If you would like to stop receiving | information by post, or you would like to | change the way you hear from us, please call | our Supporter Care team on 020 7523 2493 | or email info@christian-aid.org including your | full name and address. We promise never to | share your details with any other organisation | for marketing purposes. For more information, | please visit caid.org.uk/privacy | Christian Aid | magazine | for supporters Autumn/Winter 2021 | am WW ebde Y Wes WY 9424 24V7Q &* | These challenging times have reminded us how important it is to love our | neighbours as ourselves. Making a Will is itself an act of love, a way of | taking care of your family and friends. And by including a gift to Christian Aid | in your Will, you can extend that love to your global family too. | Your gift could support future generations of women like Ida Lazalo in | Malawi. By joining forces, Ida and her sisters are already starting new | sustainable businesses that could one day offer a way out of poverty | and protection from the climate crisis. | Write your Will with Will Aid | This November you can write or update your Will with Will Aid. Rather than | charge their fee, participating solicitors will write your Will for a suggested | charitable donation, shared by nine charities, including Christian Aid. | Visit willaid.org.uk or call 0300 0309 558. | Ida showing off the bottles | of baobab juice that she and | her fellow Makande Women’s | Group members have made. | The vaccine rollout in Britain has been a blessing. | I'm sure, like me, that you're grateful to be able to | do more of the things you enjoy and see the people | you love. | But I'm acutely aware that not all share this | privilege. It is deeply unjust that so many people | across the world cannot access vaccines. And for | some communities, the spread of coronavirus is | not the only crisis that they face. The climate crisis | continues to devastate lives and livelihoods. | As we approach the last few months of the year, | we have a unique opportunity to raise the voices of | our global neighbours and call for climate justice. | In November, the UK will host the United Nations | climate talks, COP26. | If we truly wish to end poverty, we must seize | every opportunity before us to call for justice - | Christian Aid Magazine | aie | Issue 17: Autumn/Winter 2021 .»~Qiited | Editor: Laura Oakley | Sub-editor: Natasha Fiala | Art editor: Rachel Irwin, Rebecca Gray | Design: Rachel Irwin, Rebecca Gray | Coordination: John Lamb, | Lorna Amahson | Published by Christian Aid | 35 Lower Marsh, London SE1 7RL | 020 7620 4444 | info@christian-aid.org | caid.org.uk | 4-51 | £.2D 4. be | 6-8 Rise to th | Cover story | Janet Ben | proudly shows vacrina | the beans she vaccine | now grows and | sells ata market | 10-11 Song | Photos; Cover Christian Aid/Malumbo | Simwaka; page 2 Christian Aid/Malumbo | Simwaka; page 3 Christian Aid, Christian | Aid/Latitude Space; pages 4-5 Christian | Aid/Amaru; pages 6-8 Wirestock, Christian | Aid, 1D Campbell; page 9 Christian Aid/ | Latitude Space; pages 10-11 Christian Aid/ | Alex Baker; pages 12-13 Christian Aid/Tom | Pilston, Christian Aid pages 14-15 Christian | Aid/Amaru; pages 16-17 Christian Aid/Haya, | Christian Aid/Enddy Ziyera; pages 18-19 | Christian Aid/Shuvo Roy, Christian Aid; pages | 20-21 Christian Aid/Silvano Yokwe; page 22 | Christian Aid, Christian Aid/Elaine Duigenan; | page 23 Christian Aid. | Ld a | Harvest | 14-15 | 14-13 | Time for climate justice | Explore why climate justice is so | central to the work we do. | e Momer: | Join us as we journey to Cop26 and | call for climate justice. | 2 thanks for your | See how your gifts of thanksgiving | are helping our global neighbours. | of the prophets | Discover Song of the Prophets: a | Requiem for the Climate. | 12-13 Christian Aid Week | Find out how people celebrated this | year's Christian Aid Week. | A y PI n 9eal | Read about the fis green fruit | changing lives in Malawi. | ey | | stana | 16-17 From where | | Get a glimpse into the lives of the | starting with COP26, but | beyond that too. Creating | real and lasting change is a | marathon, not a sprint. But | together our actions have | impact, and together we can sustain one | another for the journey ahead. | In this edition of Christian Aid Magazine, | you can learn more about the climate crisis and | the important role you can play in seeking justice. | Many | Pete Moorey | Head of Campaigns and UK Advocacy | 1 8-19 Love | builds hope | Remind yourself about the change | and hope you bring to the world. | 20-21 Christmas Ap} | Stand with mums in South Sudan | I who are facing impossible choices. | 22 Remembering loved ones | Read about the life of supporter John | Eckersley, and our remembrance | service for all loved ones. | Word | Meet Rachel Mander from Young | Christian Climate Network (YCCN). | 23 Last \ | ey actalliance | people we work with and for. | Eng and Wales charity no. 1105851 Scot charity no. SCO39150 Company no. 5171525. The Christian Aid name and logo are trademarks | of Christian Aid, Printed exclusively on material sourced from responsibly managed forests © Christian Aid September 2021 J229969, | The acceptance of external advertising does not indicate endorsement. | Editorial | Janet Ben gestures at a pile of muddy bricks. It’s all that’s left of | her old house in Malawi, destroyed by Cyclone Idai. Together we | must press on for climate justice. | anet had reached rock bottom. Her | family lost everything they owned on | that calamitous day over two years ago, | including their livestock and only source of | income. Cyclone Idai spared their lives, but | only just. | Homeless and only able to eat oncea | day, Janet was in a desperate fight for | survival - for herself, her husband, and | her precious children. | Cyclone Idai was one of the worst tropical | cyclones to hit the southern hemisphere. | Experts believe the destructive power of | such storms is only going to get worse | because of the climate crisis. | Our hearts break when we hear stories | like Janet’s. We are stunned by the raw | poverty that she faces. We are outraged | by the flagrant injustice of her situation, | as she confronts the worst impacts of this | climate crisis. | It’s because we know, deep in our core, | that this shouldn't be happening. Not | anywhere, not to anyone. | Like you, we believe everyone is equal in | the sight of God. That truth has driven us for | over 75 years to stand in solidarity with our | global neighbours, of all faiths and none. | So, we will keep fighting for climate justice | alongside our neighbours like Janet. | Janet Ben’s house was destroyed | by Cyclone Idai in 2019. This rubble | is all that remains. | We'll keep supporting her in her new business, | creating and selling baobab juice with women | in her community, so she can feed and clothe | her loved ones. | We'll take our fight for justice to the UN | climate talks in Glasgow this November, | pushing our political leaders to tackle the | climate crisis without leaving our global | neighbours behind. | And we'll respond quickly as layers of | crises - conflict, Covid-19 and climate - aS aie | pre tanis | increase global hunger, so that women, Danes | RPT Os | men and children will not lose their lives. | Climate justice has never been more | important than it is today, and we are in | this battle for the long haul. The truth is | that we couldn't achieve a fraction of this | without you, our supporters. So, thank you | for standing together with us. | Janet's radiant smile says it all. Her small ~ | business has helped her find away outof | poverty: ‘My two older kids now go to school | on a full stomach. The vision | have for my | family is that we should no longer be poor. | | want us to be a family that will inspire others.’ | Together we can help more families press | on towards climate justice. Thank you Janet, | for your inspiration. | Turn to page 14 to read more about Janet’s |: | remarkable story. | he climate crisis is a storm we all face, but | we're not all in the same boat. Every day, | people living in poverty battle the worst ofa | crisis they did not create. This is deeply unjust. | But a better way is possible. Together we can | tackle the inequalities in wealth and power that | have led to this climate crisis - starting now. | In November, the UK Government will host | the United Nations key climate talks, COP26. This | is a decade-defining opportunity to impact and | influence the decisions made. We must ensure | COP26 is the 26th meeting of | world leaders at the UN climate | change talks. The conference | has been described as the most | important gathering on climate | change since the Paris Agreement | was signed in 2015. | After being postponed in 2020 | due to the coronavirus pandemic, | COP26 is due to take place in the | first two weeks of November 2021. | For many, this conference remains | the best, and only, global platform | to build consensus on climate | justice and action. | All being well, COP26 will be held | in Glasgow. This is a fitting city for | such an event. Fondly known as | the Dear Green Place, it was once | a centre of industrialisation. Now, | the very site where the conference | will be held is at risk of being | reclaimed by the rising River Clyde. | For two centuries, Glasgow | has been the birthplace of | social movements sparked by | injustice. Here’s hoping the warm | Glaswegian hospitality and long- | held passion for justice will set in | motion climate justice for all. | One of the key characteristics | of the COP conferences is that | people from all over the world | are represented, both in official | delegations and from civil society. | A crucial part we play is ensuring | that the voices of our partners and | the communities we work with are | heard during the conference. | We can't achieve climate justice | without involving the people | our global neighbours are heard. ey | That's why Christian Aid is joining topether, | with Young Christian Climate Network (een) | for Rise to the Moment - a journey of prayer * | and action in the run-up to COP26. Together». | we can amplify our prophetic voices and | create one unified movement for change. | From now on, every moment matters a t | every action counts in the fight for climati | Will you rise to the moment? | most affected by the crisis in the | discussions and decisions. | At COP26, we will be looking | for progress on the key issue of | climate finance - making sure | that those most affected by the | crisis are provided with money to | help cope with it. | In 2009, the world’s richest | countries, including the UK, | agreed to deliver $100bn a year | in climate finance to the world’s | poorest and most climate- | vulnerable countries by 2020. This | promise was not fulfilled. We will | be pressing this issue alongside | faith communities in the UK, | partner organisations around | the world, and YCCN. | In the run-up to COP26, we're | supporting an exciting relay with | YCCN to push for ambitious action | to tackle the climate crisis. | YCCN is an action-focused | community of people aged 18-30, | following Jesus in the pursuit | of climate justice. They share | Christian Aid’s determination to | bring about a fairer world. | The relay kicked off in Cornwall | after the G7 Summit in June | and will make its way across the | UK until it reaches Glasgow in | November. | There are lots of things you | can do as an individual, church or | community to support the relay | and help press for climate justice - | and it all starts with a paper boat. | Taking inspiration from the | model boat YCCN have built to | journey with them on the relay | - which represents hope - we're | asking you to create an origami | prayer boat. | This prayer boat is the start | of several ways you can rise to | the moment and call for climate | justice ahead of COP26: from | creating a display of boats in your | church, to adding it to our online | fleet, to using it to catch your | MP's attention. | You can even send your boat | to join an installation at COP26, | helping us ensure decision makers | cannot ignore our calls for action. | Download the prayer boat | activity pack from | The pack contains all the | information you need to know. | Wi RED | Vail | | YCCN's model boat | represents our hope | that we will rise to the | + moment and use this | year to set sail towards | a more just future. | Write a prayer on your paper | There are prayer points and an | example prayer in the pack. | Fold your piece of paper | into a boat | Follow the step-by-step | instructions in the pack. | Set sail for climate justice | Look at the options in the pack and | choose which voyage your boat | will take! | You can find out more about | YCCN and the relay in the Last | Word (page 23) where we | speak to relay co-lead, Rachel | Mander. | Are you a teacher or a youth leader? As children and young people | have been at the forefront of calls for climate action, we know you | We've worked together with The Climate Coalition on the | Together for our Planet COP26 pack for schools which can be | , downloaded on Twinkl's website at | ~ twinkl.co.uk/resources/twinkl-partnerships/cop26 | There's also our Letters for Creation project which encourages | a creative and spiritual response to climate justice. Through | community art making and exhibitions, children and young people | can exercise their voices in the run-up to COP26 and beyond. To find | out more go to caid.org.uk/lettersforcreation | For those of you based in Scotland, you can get involved in Get your | skates on. Inspired by the iconic painting of The Skating Minister by | Henry Raeburn, and the race against time to stop this climate crisis, | Glasgow-based artist ID Campbell has recreated this masterpiece | and invites others to do the same. | Get your skates on captures all the activities happening locally | in Scotland in the run-up to COP26. Find out more at | caid.org.uk/get-your-skates-on | We know some of you avid campaigners are used to attending | COP. But with coronavirus still a part of our everyday lives, | nothing is certain. | We do hope there will be opportunities to attend events in | Glasgow and other satellite cities across the UK to make your voice | heard. Plans are still to be confirmed but the main day of action | will be 6 November - so save the date and sign up to our campaign | emails to ensure you're the first to know more. Sign up at | caid.org.uk/campaignwithus Artwork by ID Campbell | ‘In our 75-year | history, we've | never taken on Be teeta eee cgacarers the | easy challenges. ; aeuine foncianee beyond | The climate crisis <23 |. | may be our biggest ee individua | challenge yet’ | Pete Moorey | Head of Campaigns and | Advocacy at Christian Aid | ‘Receiving the coronavirus | vaccination is like receiving’ | a blessing. 1am so glad to | be able to share it’ | Christian Aid supporter | hen the vaccine rollout against coronavirus | We underway in Britain, many of us felt | a sense of relief and joy. Our loved ones | would finally have some protection against the virus. | But the process also made us starkly aware of the | inequality that still exists in our world, especially when | it comes to health. | One of you in the Diocese of Gloucester felt this | so strongly that you got in touch asking for a way to | give in gratitude for your vaccine. Seeing you were | not alone, our Give thanks for your vaccine appeal | was launched. | The appeal allowed people like you to act on that | sense of thanksgiving and pass the blessing on, | helping others in crisis who have little protection | from the pandemic. | We have been truly humbled by the response to | this appeal. From Wilton Parish Church to the Church | of Scotland, you've taken part as individuals, churches, | denominations, church networks and Christian Aid | groups, raising over £300,000. | Standout contributors include the 474 members | of the United Reformed Church who have helped | raise over £30,000. Reading the messages left on our | online Wall of Thanksgiving has been both moving | and encouraging. | All your gifts of thanksgiving are helping our global | neighbours stay safe while the vaccine remains out of | Dookenger happily | holds a hygiene kit | that will help her | family in Kenya stay | safe from coronavirus. | reach. What's more, your support has enabled | Christian Aid to expand its work in India where the | coronavirus situation has been particularly | devastating. | Christian Aid has been working with local partners | in India since the start of the pandemic, providing | 130,000 vulnerable people with support, from | life-saving information on the virus, to food parcels. | But your solidarity helped us do more, providing | PPE to frontline health workers and oxygen | concentrators to health centres. We and our partners | are grateful to have been able to do more for our | sisters and brothers facing such uncertainty. | Many communities around the world still face an | uncertain wait for a vaccine as questions remain | around the cost, timing and extent of a global | vaccination rollout. | Going forward, Christian Aid will support | vaccination programmes in countries where it is | operating. This might mean providing data on | hard-to-reach populations to local health services, | or challenging misleading information about the | virus and vaccination. | As a member of the People’s Vaccine Alliance, we | will also continue to call for a ‘people's vaccine’ so that | everyone, everywhere is able to get a vaccine. We | believe this is a matter of justice as well as fairness. | Songs of the Prophets | Song a | the Prophets | In June, Chineke! Orchestra performed the world premiere | of Song of the Prophets: a Requiem for the Climate in St Paul's | Cathedral, London. | The piece was commissioned by Christian Aid to help raise | awareness about the devastating impacts of the climate crisis. | Watch the performance online and be inspired as we journey | to COP26 at caid.org.uk/song | We've been amazed by your creativity | and determination to celebrate this year’s | Christian Aid Week. We know it wasn't | an easy one to plan for with coronavirus | restrictions, but you rose to the occasion | beautifully. | From the 300,000 steps in May challenge | to posting envelopes to bake sales, you've | done so much to show your solidarity with | our global neighbours facing the climate | crisis - in Kenya and beyond. | Thank you all for your continued support. | We hope you enjoy reading about some | of the events that happened across many | the country. , | When we first met Rose in Kenya, | extreme weather robbed her of a | reliable source of water. Without | water, every day was a struggle. | giving pages set u | North Yorkshire: A team of walkers | (and Keeva the dog) from churches | across Northallerton took on the | 300,000 steps challenge this year. | Michael Webster, chair of the local | Christian Aid group, said: ‘In total, at  [~>~~. | least four million steps were walked | or run, despite challenging weather on | some days! We're approaching a total | of £3,400.’ | West Yorkshire: Creativity is a | strong force in Haworth , where ‘ | villagers ran their first ever Scarecrow | Festival. It featured 54 scarecrows and | raised over £1,700. | ="" = | Moray: Minister | Andrew Kimmitt did | a sponsored tour of | the River Spey in one | day. He ran 15km | from Loch Spey to | Garva Bridge, cycled | 135km to Fochabers, | then kayaked the last | stretch to Spey Bay. Phew! | Anglesey: Nan Powell-Davies | and Anna Jane Evans completed | the 300,000 steps challenge - in | just one week! Together they | have raised nearly £6,000. 1 | Without water, Rose was thirsty | and hungry. | Your support has given people | like Rose the hope that they can - . | stand strong against the elements. INCAIrLY | steps taken | and action | ank you for your incredible gifts, | this | - re | Lsewiilh LULAGs | envelopes ordered | 300,000 steps challenge | re ae Lp et ‘ | 1-40! | prayers | Christian Aid Week | Kinross: A water bucket | challenge organised by the | Kinross Christian Aid group | helped engage young people } | in this year’s appeal stories | and raise over £5,000. | 7 Glasgow: The Boys’ Brigade | / organised a sponsored carwash, | , raising almost £700. | i si Northumberland: Inspired by Rose's | ‘ Ps story, John McArdle and Tony Brookes | at from Hexham walked five miles from | their home to fetch water from the | riot rs River Tyne before returning home with | , it. Their Christian Aid group raised | / around £2,000. »). | Lincolnshire: St Thomas | C of E Primary School | in Boston took on the | 300,000 steps challenge | this May. Each class | walked three laps around | their football pitch, raising | £192. A fantastic result! | Neath: Father Rhun ap Robert | from the Church in Wales cycled | around his local Afon Nedd Ministry | Area (Neath) to raise funds. | Cardiff: Llandaff City | primary school in Cardiff | raised over £2,000 during | Christian Aid Week. | Suffolk: Supporter Christine | Stainer sold afternoon tea boxes in | her village, raising £200. Each box | contained sandwiches, scones, tea | bags, and lots of scrummy cakes. | Harvest Appeal | Sisters | stronger together | Janet Ben stands in front of a massive baobab | tree in Malawi, holding fuzzy green fruit picked | from its branches. It’s this fruit that’s made all the | difference to the lives of Janet and her friends. | n Malawi, women like Janet | are struggling in the face of | the climate crisis. Janet and | her family lost everything when | Cyclone Idai hit in March 2019. | The cyclone destroyed her | home and swept her family’s | livestock away. Homeless and | only able to eat once a day, | Janet was in a desperate fight | for survival. | Hope came from an unusual | fruit - and an amazing group | of women. | Janet knew of the Makande | Women's Group, a collective of | local women standing together | to change their own lives. | ‘Lused to admire the women | who were already members of | the group,’ Janet said. | ‘They were able to support | their families.’ | So Janet decided to join | too. Through a project run by | Christian Aid and our local | partner, Eagles Relief and | Development, the women’s | group learnt about making | and selling baobab juice and | received a low-interest loan to | get started. Soon the women | were making juice from the | fruit of the large baobab trees | near their homes. | ‘Once we've made the juice, | we take it to the market to | sell. After selling, we bring | the money together,’ explains | Janet. | The women make up to | 6,000 bottles of juice a month, | working in shifts. Their average | monthly income has increased | tenfold since the beginning | of the project. And as more | women join the group, they | have learnt valuable skills | from each other. They have | plans to branch out and supply | dried fruit and vegetables | across Malawi. | Joining the group has | changed everything for Janet | and her family. With a radiant | smile, she shows us her new | home and explains how proud | she is that her children are at | school. Janet now has a reliable | source of income from the | juice and has also opened her | own stall at the local market | selling beans. | In Malawi and beyond, more | and more people like Janet are | facing the fallout from extreme | weather. But together we can | stop this climate crisis. | SER ru IN ae | Jane yc the baobab | uit that ee to | ipyide: ak etter life for | ant uot) | tok ea fami | inspire others” | We are stronger | together. It's not too | late to join us this | face of crisis. Donate | at caid.org.uk/Harvest | - orscan this QR code using | 4 your smartphone camera. | Here are two from Haya and Enddy. | Overcoming trauma in Syria | Haya took part in a photo-journalism project helping | young people in north-west Syria deal with trauma | and express their hopes for the future. | It's part of Christian Aid’s Creating Alternative | Futures programme, funded by the European Union. | This programme helps young people affected by the | Syrian conflict catch up on their education, learn new | skills and access psychosocial support. | Many of these young people have lived through | conflict for almost half their lives. Photography | allows them to catalogue what they see every day | and express themselves. | Haya’s story | My name is Haya. | am 20 years old from north-east | Syria. After | finished high school, my dream was | to go to university, but the circumstances of war | and displacement prevented me from achieving | this dream. | At that time, | felt darkness everywhere around | me, as if life had ended - the case for many of my | Christian Aid is learning more and more about | the communities we work with thanks to new ae | projects that help people share their own stories. » _. | Haya taking photos of her surroundings. | country’s people. But | finally found light through | my camera which expresses my pain and hope at | the same time. | | love photography because every picture | has a beautiful story, different memories, and | unforgettable moments. | In the past, | took many photos in different | places, but when | joined the photography training, | | benefited a lot from this experience to improve | my photos. | ‘T finally found light | through my camera | which expresses | my pain and hope’ | We photograph to understand the meaning of our | life. lf you cannot feel what you are photographing, | you will not be able to make others feel anything | when they look at your pictures. | How many people entered this place for quarantine | after being infected with coronavirus? Some of them | died after they struggled with the disease, and others | survived to tell us the story of their suffering and how | important it is to adhere to preventive measures. | When I see | the bees | absorb the | nectar from | this white and | pink flowering | tree, it gives | me hope, and | | feel reassured | and secure. | This road tells us hundreds of stories full of tears | and laughter of the people who crossed it - during | the journey of displacement and asylum, or in | ambulances on their way to the hospital after being | infected with coronavirus, or even hikers enjoying | the beauty of nature. | See more photography from Haya and others at caid.org.uk/mysyria | Sharing community concerns | Our True Voice is a network of community | reporters from Nigeria and Zimbabwe sharing | first-hand accounts of the challenges and solutions | of social exclusion. | It's part of Christian Aid’s project, Evidence and | Collaboration for Inclusive Development (ECID), | funded by UK Aid, and is helping to focus support | where it is needed most. | The reporters are individuals from the marginalised | communities the project aims to support: women, | people with disabilities and young people. | One of them is Enddy Ziyera from Zimbabwe. | He has covered stories across a range of topics | including health and gender-based violence. | These reports are valuable evidence in | advocating for improved services and they make | sure that people's voices are heard. | Below is one of Enddy’s latest reports. | Enddy Ziyera | June 2021, Zimbabwe | Access to clean water | remains a challenge for | residents of Gimboki | Access to clean water remains a pipe dream for | many residents of Dangamvura especially those | residing in Gimboki. | Women and children spend hours in long | queues at the two communal water points, to fill | up a few containers. Altercations, shouting, and | cursing are a daily ritual as people jostle for a chance | to fill up their containers. At these water points, social | distancing, wearing of face masks and other Covid-19 | safety protocols are not observed. | Many women do their laundry by the nearby rocks | to take advantage of the water point. This increases | the chance for the virus to spread. Some water | vendors charge people as high as US$3 to fill up | a 200-litre container. But this is very expensive | compared to the less than Z$90 (US$0.25) for 1,000 | litres of water charged by the local authority. | This has increased the levels of poverty and | inequalities among residents, especially women whose | social status continues to dwindle. It is time that local | authorities and developers found a lasting solution to | the water problem. | Head to evidenceforinclusion.org/our-true-voice | yw #OurTrueVoice on Twitter t | In May, violence spiralled out of control in Politicians failed to deliver the commitments the world | Israel and the occupied Palestinian territory. needs on vaccines, debt relief and the climate crisis at this | We asked you to write to the British Prime year's G7 Summit. But you showed up. Ahead of the event, | Minister and demand he do all he could to you helped amplify the voices of our global neighbours: | support the ceasefire and support peace with from the postcards you sent to the Prime Minister, to the | justice. Your solidarity was overwhelming, Wave of Hope hands you displayed in your window. Let's | The events of the last few years have been tough. But th rough | making this one of the most acted-upon take this momentum to COP26 in November. | it all, people like you have continued to think of others. It is this | requests we have ever asked of you. | William Bell, Head of Middle East Region said: | ‘Thank you very much. | know that all of | our partners, Israeli and Palestinian, felt | that solidarity.’ | love you share, even for strangers, that keeps hope alive. | You inspire us. And as we face big challenges ahead on our | journey towards climate justice, we wanted to remind you of the | change and hope you bring. | Thanks to your support of a joint project | with the World Food Programme, Christian | Aid launched new projects for Rohingya | refugees. People like Shomsu Alam have | been able to earn an income through a | cash for work scheme. Now he can buy | books for his children, grow a small garden | and keep chickens - all of which will help | his children as they grow. | STOP FUELLING | THE FIRE © | Last year, 25 young Christians from our Prophetic Earlier this year, we asked some of you to share | Activist scheme launched Stop Fuelling the Fire - a what gives you hope. Hundreds of you returned | ee : a Ie campaign to stop the UK Government financing the messages to us and we read every single one. | After iii dian 2 a of YOUR CaInpalsranig tS make aia extraction and burning of fossil fuels overseas. The Here are two that really resonated. We hope they | big companies pay slg fair share of se finance Alaabui cl UK finally announced they would stop this stream of give you encouragement too. | from the G7 sca bi came to a historic deal. Multinational funding, and the campaign won the ‘David and Goliath’ | companies will now have to pay at least a 15% tax rate on award from the Shella McKechnie Foundation. | their profits in the countries where they operate. While there | is more to do, this is still a monumental step forward. | 18 19 | or a long time, Adut Mariu had no | choice but to give her children dirty | water. She knew it wasn't safe, but | she had no choice. | ‘We were in desperate need,’ Adut | said. ‘To look after my children, they | had to drink the dirty river water.’ | The River Makadh runs through Adut’s | village of Biet in north-west South Sudan | and it was the community's only source | of water. It is dirty and unreliable. | The river dries up during long periods | of drought and it floods during heavy | rainfall, polluting fragile water sources, | killing crops and destroying homes. | Combined with years of conflict | and the Covid-19 pandemic, climate | chaos forces mums like Adut to make | impossible choices for their children. | A morsel of food for each child, or a | bigger portion for one? School fees, or | materials to rebuild the family home? | Dirty water, or none at all? | Not anymore. Adut now has access to | a borehole near her home. That means | clean water for her children to drink, | whatever the weather. | ‘The water from the hand pump | is very good. It’s disease free. We | drink it with peace of mind,’ said Adut. | It means no more upset tummies | from drinking dirty water. It means | healthier bodies and minds. And it | means more family time together, | sharing stories and laughter. | But while the borehole is | life-changing, there are still many | challenges ahead. The borehole is | being used by around three times more | people than it should be. Clean water is | in desperate need across the region. | Mums on the frontline of the climate | crisis face impossible choices every day. | But our decision to stand with them is an | easy one. | Your gifts this Christmas could help | build more boreholes, provide seeds and | farming tools, and give mums the chance | to set up small businesses to take care of | their families. | With clean water, nutritious food and | ways to earn money, mums like Adut in | South Sudan and beyond won't have to | make such impossible choices. | Life for Adut has been tough, but so | is she. She's lived through displacement, | conflict, loss and now the climate | crisis. But she is still full of hope for her | children’s futures. | We all do what we can for the children | in our lives, especially at Christmas. Stand | with mums this Christmas and help the | next generation thrive.",Mailout,NA,NA
16,"“Its really nice to be given the creative | freedom to do the visual merchandising, | you wouldn’t really be able to get that | anywhere else. It’s quite fun really.” | Jess, Window Dresser Extraordinaire | “| like volunteering at Oxfam. | My favourite thing is helping other C5 V a A | people, it’s like a family.” | Wagayu, Book Guru L | L E | N ow lockdown has eased, many of us | are looking to reconnect, make new | friends, and gain new skills. If you have | some time to spare — especially as the | Christmas season approaches - our | shops have a range of roles and hours | to suit everyone, and we can even help | you develop new skills. | You might see yourself igniting that | creative spark by window dressing or | brushing up on your digital skills by | managing a busy social media account. | Whatever your skill set, we have an | opportunity for you. | You can even volunteer with a friend! | “| had a lot of free time and thought | volunteering was a way to be a part of | something meaningful, and creative for | myself, something to make a difference.” | Phillip, Stylist and Hat Connoisseur | If you don’t have anyone to bring with you, | our friendly shop teams mean you're bound | to find someone like-minded and make | new friends in-store. When you apply, your | local shop manager will ask you to come in | for an initial chat, just a chance to get an | idea of what you'd like to do in the shop. | From there you can meet the rest of | the team and get involved in the area of | the shop that takes your interest. | DID YOU KNOW | Our amazing network of shop volunteers | are the heartbeat of Oxfam. Without | them our lifesaving work just wouldn't | be possible. On average a team of 30-35 | volunteers helps run each shop. Which | means we have thousands of volunteers, | donating around 3.5 million hours to raise | a whopping £30 million for Oxfam. @ | >BECOME A SHOP VOLUNTEER | call 0300 200 1333 or go to | oxfam.org.uk/volunteer | Photos: Radka Dolinska/Oxfam | “| THINK | ITS THE | LET’S FINISH mol ido ume | THE WORK | eh co mei | WE STARTED a : FROM THIS SO | MANY OTHER | INJUSTICES FLOW. | Throughout her life, Linda has worked to help | others. Now she’s left a gift to Oxfam in her Will | LINDA, OXFAM SUPPORTER | to help people for generations to come. | Mf \ \ive in the East Midlands. I’m retired now, | live on | my own and country living suits me well. I’m lucky I’ve | got a garden... | enjoy gardening - that’s something that’s | developed over the years. | | was a social worker. | suppose | had a glimpse of | inequalities and lack of opportunities in this country. But | the minute you start to look beyond your own situation | it seems to me that there are just so many instances of | people suffering and people in need of help. | just have | that feeling that we are here to do more than just look | after ourselves. | | don't have children or dependants so my estate | will go to a variety of charities of which Oxfam is a major | beneficiary. | see Oxfam as a major player in the fight | against poverty and injustice. | | think it’s the inequalities in the world that really get Lyi st a4 Picts 3 | Pi $ | to me - from this so many other injustices flow — lack Sf ae ee, ; ° a ee P | Ng Se) | f 9 eee | sag Ee} | ‘oo a | ged teavakiees iit""! | at aun qnvaane(aecenesaungeneeseven Oueseee | suteenssanere 4 | of education, impact of climate change, work, housing CT | ae Pe ae + F HELP CREATE A FAIRER WORLD | and health insecurities. ; So, * me ee ey Be | | love that Oxfam doesn't just do crisis work. That 7 | aspect which is about putting in the water supply so people | if. If you've been inspired by Linda to leave a gift | ming in your Will to Oxfam, we are here to help. We | have a free gifts in Wills guide and are pleased | don’t get sick, installing toilets so women don’t have 8 H ; e to offer all our supporters a free Will writing service. | to go out into the fields putting themselves at risk from | animal and human attacks. All of the things that will | hopefully help a community both healthwise and otherwise | in the long term — and | think that is probably the best thing | about Oxfam, the long-term improvements for people. 44 | Photo: Abbie Trayler-Smith/Oxfam | To find out more please call our Gifts in Wills advisors | on 0300 200 1331, email giftsinwills@oxfam.org.uk | or visit our website at oxfam.org.uk/willgift | Thoughtful gifting is easy this Christmas | with our beautiful range of ethical and | sustainable products. | . Butterfly and Bee Friendly seeds | Rewild Your Life book | Grow Green book | . Recycled paper birdhouse ball | Recycled sari birdhouse ball | . Tony‘s Chocolonely chocolate bar | Rainbow design wool throw | . ji | (AB me PLS ee | Ukuva sauces | Ukuva spice grinders | . Recycled sari cushions | Recycled sari kantha stitch throw | Rainbow design wool throw | . Post-able bunting | Ss = | = 5 = y | 4. Zaytoun Olive Oil Handmade felt Christmas pud decoration | . Recycled iron Scotty dog book ends | Recycled sari laundry basket | Printed cotton rug | . Kashmiri design tea tray | . Recycled handmade paper star garland | . Spice Kitchen World spice gift set | WeJXQ/SOIPNYS WW ‘WejXO/AeW a},;02Y9 :S0}0Ud | “ONE OF THE | BEST THINGS | YOU CAN DO | IS USE YOUR | VOICE TO CALL | FOR CHANGE.” | GRACE DE BLACA | OXFAM CAMPAIGNER | FIGHTING | FOR CLIMATE | JUSTICE | We've been at the forefront of campaigning for climate | justice for over 30 years, because we know that as | temperatures rise and extreme weather becomes more | frequent, it is the poorest people in the world who will be | affected the most. With your support, we’ve successfully | challenged companies and governments to take action. | Take a look at some key wins we’ve achieved - together. | Photos: Ainhoa Goma/Oxfam, Ng Swan Ti/Oxfam, Pablo Tosco/Oxfam, www.shutterstock.com, Velislav Nikolov | 1986 2007 2009 2015 | AN EARLY GLOBAL CLIMATE eyitaee FOR | SIGNAL HEARINGS | ON CLIMATE In the run up to COP15 ADAPTATION | CHANGE in Copenhagen, Oxfam Oxfam plays a key role | in securing a commit- | ment for 50% of the | Green Climate Fund | to be allocated to | much-needed climate | convenes ‘climate | hearings’ where people | from around the world | come together to | testify to the impact | Oxfam publishes | Weather Alert! - a | report establishing a | connection between | unusual weather | SUPPORTING | CLIMATE | MARCHES | Jessy and Isaac - | Malawian school | students and climate | activists - travel to | London with the | support of Oxfam and | CLIMATE | FINANCE SHADOW | REPORT 2020 | our partner ‘Send my HOLDING | Friend to School’. COUNTRIES | They share personal | experience of the TO ACCOUNT | climate crisis and | address crowds | gathered in London | to protest against | climate change. | Oxfam publishes its | biannual Shadow | Report on Climate | Finance which | assesses the progress | rich countries are | making on providing | promised climate | finance. This year’s | report exposes the | overwhelming | provision of climate | finance as loans | rather than grants. | The UK will be hosting the 26th UN Climate | Change Conference (COP26), attended | by world leaders from 200 governments, | this November. The more people we have | campaigning with us and sharing our | patterns and the | negative impacts | of a hidden climate | crisis on the world’s | poorest people. | PEOPLE AND | POLAR BEARS | Oxfam campaigners | from around the world | come together at the | United Nations Climate | Change Conference | in Bali. Dressed as | polar bears and | carrying ‘Save the | humans’ placards, | they highlight the | impact of | climate change | of climate change on | their lives. Thousands | of people participate | globally, sending | a unified, urgent | message on climate | change to the | world. | CHALLENGING | FOOD COMPANIES | Together with farmers, | consumers and investors | Oxfam launches the | Behind the Brands | campaign calling on | the world’s biggest | food producers to cut | emissions. 238,000 | people support the | campaign and Kelloggs | and General Mills commit | to significant reductions. | ¢——4 | . gAVE | rs 3S pMANS Other food production | natural ~~ ad GF companies follow. | (Ae eh | | world. » | adaptation. The UK is | the first country in the | world to commit 50% | of its climate finance | to adaptation. | CALLING TIME | ON COAL | Oxfam campaigning | helps secure the UK’s | commitment to phase | out coal by 2025 and | the historic decision | by the European | Investment Bank to | end all fossil fuel | lending. | message, the more we can achieve. | SIGN THE PETITION | Tell Prime Minister Boris Johnson to take | a global lead on climate finance and | reducing carbon emissions as the UK | prepares to host COP26. | FUTURE | CHANGE IN MOTION | Take a look at some of the incredible things our supporters are doing | to help create lasting change for communities around the world. | Northampton Shop Manager, Vimal, discovered | that Anita Neil, Britain’s first black British female | Olympian, lived just down the road, he felt compelled to | find out more about her achievements. But he noticed | that despite winning medals at two Olympic Games, | Anita has been sadly ignored by history. Together with | his community he set about changing that. “People in | my town only just learned of her during the | last lockdown. We made some noise and | as aresult she is getting more coverage | and recognition. I’m hoping she gets a | statue in the town. She paved the way!” | ‘ OMMUNITY BACKS AN OLYMPIAN When our | Photo: Express via Getty Images | Push Krishnamurthy has taken on three | mammoth walks worldwide to raise funds to | fight climate change. That’s 31 million steps! And | he’s about to take on his fourth. He’s walking from | London to Glasgow for COP26 - encouraging others | to walk with him and visiting Oxfam shops en route. | lf Push’s walk has inspired you, you can take part | in the remote World Climate March. More info at | worldclimatemarch.org | P USH KEEPS ON PUSHING Oxfam supporter | ONNIE DONS HIS WALKING BOOTS 8-year- | old Oxfam supporter Donnie has raised an amazing | £2,062, so far, for Oxfam walking 20,000 steps every | day during his Easter school break. Donnie’s mum said | he was inspired to take on the challenge when he learned | about children around the world who have to walk miles | each day just to fetch water. The money Donnie has | raised will go a long way towards helping provide water | to communities who need it most. | >IF YOU HAVE A STORY TO SHARE please email heretohelp@oxfam.org.uk | VACCINES | FOR ALL | WHAT YOU CAN DO | since we received Join millions of people who are | the message to stay campaigning for a People’s Vaccine | at home. And arguably = and urge the UK Prime Minister, | life has only been able to get Boris Johnson, to: | back to some form of normality @ Push , | ‘ : pharmaceutical companies to | i me eisai tothetapld reduce the cost of vaccines by setting | vaca nate programme tat the price nations pay as close to the | rolled out eater In tne year true cost of manufacturing as possible | - offering protection to anyone | who wanted it, for free. @ Back the proposal to suspend | But for billions of people intellectual property rules for all | living in the world’s poorest Covid-19 vaccines, tests and treatments | until we reach global herd immunity | countries, the wait for a Covid-19 | vaccine may be many years — @ Make sure pharmaceutical companies | 57 according to the WHO. share the vaccine science, technology | and know-how so more manufacturers | Right now, the whole world | is dependent on a handful of can help scale up production. | pharmaceutical corporations | who simply can’t make enough “A COVI D = ] 9 | Ms for everyone. We need VACCINE MUST | em to share the science freely | — So more manufacturers BE SEEN AS | around the world can produce A GLO BAL | the vaccines at the scale | we urgently need. And PUBLI C GOOD, | at a cost which makes A PEOPLE'S | it affordable for nations "" | to provide vaccines VACCIN = | free of charge to UN SECRETARY-GENERAL | their citizens. ANTONIO GUTERRES | t’s over 18 months | >SIGN THE VACCINES FOR ALL PETITION oxfam.org.uk/peoplesvaccine | KEEPING PEOPLE SAFE | At Oxfam, it is our duty to protect | our staff and the people we work | with. Our Safeguarding team explain | how Oxfam is working hard to | keep people safe. | CLIFFORD ISABELLE | DIRECTOR OF GLOBAL | SAFEGUARDING | 44 \n recent years, we've taken significant | steps to address our past failings, including | safer recruitment; increased resources and | supervision for the Safeguarding team; | removal of barriers for people reporting | sexual exploitation or abuse and increased | support for survivors of sexual exploitation | or abuse. | But we know there is still much more to | do. Working in partnership with communities | is crucial - both in preventing abuse and | ensuring that survivors have the confidence | and safe spaces to report misconduct - and | we are committed to doing that. 44 | XARA CHURCH | SAFEGUARDING MANAGER | OXFAM GB | 44 \ have been with Oxfam GB since January | 2019 and, during my time, we have seen | many changes to safeguarding. We now | have a global case management system that | allows us to not only be more accountable | but helps to demonstrate decision-making | and lessons learnt. All our investigations | are survivor-centred and we are acutely | aware of the risks that survivors, reporters | and witnesses face and this governs all | our decisions. 44 | NAZAN NAWAZ | SAFEGUARDING ADVISOR | OXFAM GB | 44 Norking in the Oxfam GB Safeguarding | team, one of the most important parts for me | is to work together with the ‘Safeguarding | Focal Points’ - these are the designated | people in a region or country that hold the | safeguarding role. Every month, | get to | meet the Focal Points from the Asia region | where we explore and share best practices | among Asian countries. 44 | MANDY JONES | HEAD OF SAFEGUARDING | OXFAM GB | 44 \ have been with Oxfam GB since March | 2021. We have a duty of care to protect | anyone who comes into contact with Oxfam | and our work, particularly the people we | support, from sexual exploitation and other | abuses. My work is very much to do with | working collaboratively with others to | promote a safe environment in which we | are all responsible for challenging abuse. 44 | ALINA ROSENFELD | SAFEGUARDING ADVISOR | OXFAM GB | 44 \ have worked in the Safeguarding team | for two years. | have been impressed by the | progress in survivor support at Oxfam. We | put survivors at the heart of everything | we do and work with them to ensure they | receive the support they need. 44 | >READ MORE ON SAFEGUARDING AT OXFAM oxfam.org/safeguarding | lavel, who is 62, has gone hungry too | Venezuela with her 11-year-old grandson. | Her husband had to emigrate to find work — | leaving Clavel alone. There have been many | times she has had to stop eating in order | to feed-her grandson. | Political instability, made worse | by the pandemic, means inflation | has caused food prices to rise | dramatically for families like Clavel’s. | Climate change has contributed to | heavy rains causing rivers to overflow and | harvests to be destroyed. Food is scarce. | Since the pandemic began, Clavel has been | able to buy less and less. Her pension | -is only enough to buy a bag of flour or one | kilogram of rice per month. | “Before, we ate what we sowed here | on the little piece of land that we have but | the river took everything.” | Donations to Oxfam have helped set up | community kitchens in rural Venezuela to | make sure families don't go hungry. Every: | day, Clavel and her grandson enjoy one | hot meal at their local kitchen, nicknamed | the dining room. 4,000 people have been | + reached so far. | “| have received good food from the | dining room, very good food. Apart from | food, we get fruits. Also, they have given | many times. She lives in a border town in ° | The devastating combination of climate change, conflict | : and Covid-19 has caused a global hunger crisis. For people | like Clavel, sacrifices have to be:made. | us kits, with alcohol, soap, and things | to disinfect. Everything has helped us.” | The triple threat of Covid-19, conflict | and climate change has deepened existing | inequalities and is fuelling a devastating | hunger crisis for millions of people around | “YOU DO NOT KNOW THE NEED WE ARE | EXPERIENCING IN THIS PLACE... 1 CAN | ENDURE HUNGER, BUT THE CHILD CANNOT.” | the world. It is predicted that by the end | of the year, 11 people could die of hunger | every minute.* We urgently need to help | more families put food back on the table, | today and tomorrow. | HOW YOU CAN HELP | Any donation from you could make sure | a family like Clavel’s does not go hungry. | £25 could help local volunteers distribute | emergency food to families, including items | like cooking oil, rice, tomatoes and pasta. | £40 could provide a family like Clavel’s | with training on how to farm with drought- | resistant seeds. | £100 could help six women adapt their | livelihoods to be able to continue working | ina changing climate. | Please help with a donation today | 35 EASY | WAYS TO | DONATE | ONLINE | oxfam.org.uk/hunger | POST FORM | in envelope enclosed | La CALL 0300 200 1300 | Lines open 9 - 5, Monday-Friday | Figures sourced from Oxfam Media Briefing ‘The Hunger Virus Multiplies: Deadly Recipe of Conflict, Covid-19 and Climate | Accelerates World Hunger (published July 2021) Photo: Rolando Ouarte/Oxfam | Thanks to the generosity of supporters like | you, we raised an incredible £1.4 million for | our India Covid-19 Appeal. This helped Oxfam India set up Mission Sanjeevani | to help healthcare providers across the country join forces to protect some | of the most marginalised and vulnerable communities from the virus. | have been set up - | one in the southern state of | Karnataka, and the other in | the northern state of Bihar. | Seven more are scheduled for | set up in the coming months. | .E for one month | including some of the most | marginalised and vulnerable | people across 16 states. | have been distributed. I to treat people | with Covid- 19 - including | oxygen cylinders, hospital | beds and oxygen-monitoring | units — have been sent to | hospitals in urgent need. | enabling them | to buy the essentials they | need to survive. This was | a lifeline for many migrant | workers who were forced | to stop working during | lockdown. | have been distributed to | protect medical staff and | healthcare providers. | Figures correct as of September 2021 but increase daily as we support more people. | HOW YOUR SUPPORT | HAS SAVED LIVES | Back in May, Palestinians | in Gaza came under attack, | following weeks of increasing tensions and violence in | Jerusalem, the West Bank and Gaza. | Bombing from the Israeli military destroyed lives and over | 1,000 homes, and devastated public services. At least 253 | Palestinians were killed and around 100,000 were displaced | from their homes. People already living in poverty were put | at increased risk from Covid-19 as hundreds of thousands | of people had no access to basic hygiene. | We launched an emergency appeal and, thanks to you, | we were able to work with our local partners to provide: | TO 1,760 PEOPLE affected by the crisis | - KITS TO 5,501 ee nln | SHELTER AND PSYCH l for those | traumatised by the bombing | [AT | | facilities restored and used to | anbeetic over 20, 000 people. | Photos: Vaibhav Raghunandan/Oxfam, Hosam Salem/Oxfam | S are spreading | awareness Of life-saving | hygiene practices. Vans | like these are one of the | most effective ways to | communicate with as | many people as possible. | given to | families to protect them from | Covid-19. These contain | essentials such as sanitiser, | soap, washing powder and | buckets. | TS given out 1 COVID-1 LL CENTRE | to help keep medical staff and has been set up to help | healthcare providers safe. medical staff effectively | distribute life-saving | equipment to healthcare | providers. The Call Centre, | called Sanjeevani, responds | to all queries in English, | Hindi, Bengali, Odiya, | Marathi and Kannada. | (female | community health workers) | have received training and | safety kits so they are | protected when reaching out | to the most marginalised | communities. | | f e the sewage. and | water lines for a residential community in | Beit Lahia town in the northern Gaza Strip. | Tools to tackle climate | change (cover) | Muhammad Khan is a farmer | in Baden, Southern Pakistan, | where we have been working | with rural communities to | prepare, adapt and respond | to climate change. This has | included training on more | efficient farming methods and | supporting the installation of | new solar-powered irrigation | systems. Structural inequalities | within the community have | meant that women and | marginalised groups have not | had their voices heard. As part | of this project, Oxfam has | successfully campaigned for | women and other excluded | groups to be included in the | water management | decision-making process. | Allinformation was accurate at time of press. | Opinions included here are not necessarily | those of Oxfam. Oxfam is a registered charity | in England and Wales (no 202918) and Scotland | (SC039042). Photos: (cover) Khaula Jamil/ | Oxfam, Ng Swan Ti/Oxfam, MM Studios/Oxfam, | Rolando Ouarte/Oxfam, (this page] Samuel | Turpin/Oxfam, Cynthia Matonhodze/ Oxfam, | Nana Kofi Acquah, Andy Aitchison/Oxfam, | Emma Ward/Oxfam | Marketing code: 21SCIODMO4 | Inhouse: 9568 | = 2 os : | Growing collectively | | In Burkina Faso, recurrent droughts are a constant | challenge for the communities who live there - most | of whom depend on farming for their livelihoods. | Conflict in the north of the country is forcing people to | flee their villages. As a result, 2 million Burkinabé are | facing a hunger crisis. Working with local partners, | Oxfam was able to establish a farming collective | where vulnerable women could work together | to learn new climate-adapted farming methods, | so they can continue to provide for their families. | Keeping you posted on Donate by Post | Great news on our new Donate by Post | scheme — a way for you to _ | donate your pre-loved clothes | and accessories without | having to go into an Oxfam | shop. Thousands of you have | ordered a donation bag to fill | and we've loved receiving | incredible clothes and getting calomel | them ready for new wardrobes. | If you haven't ordered yours | yet, you can get one at | oxfam.org.uk/donatebypost or by calling 0300 200 1300. | Winning against poverty | SPEOPLE’sm | Since 2014, players of People’s | POSTCODE | Postcode Lottery have raised | BLOTTERYS | over £11 million for Oxfam. | During the pandemic this has | been a lifeline for local communities to keep safe, | enabling the distribution of over 200,000 hygiene | kits containing essentials such as laundry | detergent, soap and menstrual products, plus | buckets for handwashing where no taps are | available. Thank you so much! | Alerting farmers by text | A weather station which sends advice via text | message to farmers, like Sfiso, has been installed | in Zimbabwe. Sfiso lives in an area where climate | change is creating unpredictable weather and | making it increasingly difficult to grow crops. “We | get messages on our phone with weather alerts, for | example: ‘Take note of rains to come. Watch children | don’t roam too far. Move to higher ground’. It greatly | assists us - we can react immediately.” This initiative | is part of a consortium between Oxfam, Save the | Children and International Rescue Committee to | help communities adapt to the changing climate. | [rity Defending people from the sea | A Government of Ghana initiative, funded by the | European Union, has recently undertaken a life- | changing project for people living in Axim — an ancient | costal town in Ghana. Rising sea levels have been | causing erosion to the town, putting the community | and their fishing livelihoods at risk. Through advocacy | and influence, Oxfam has helped raise the concerns | of the people of Axim to government level and, in | response, a rock wall has been built which protects | the town from the ravaging sea. It stretches | 5.5 km from Brawire to Nkekem and means people | can continue to fish and live safely. | Message in a bottle | In June, when the G7 Summit | was held in Cornwall, our climate | campaigners sent them a clear | message that rich countries must | do more to help the most vulnerable | countries respond to the impact of | climate change. The group posed | as world leaders, too busy relaxing | on the beach to notice a stark | message in a bottle from countries | devastated by climate change. As | Glasgow gears up for world leaders | arriving for COP26 — the 2021 United | Nations Climate Change Conference | - we'll be keeping the heat on. | OXFAM | > Bw | Second Hand September | Lots of you took part in Second | Hand September —choosing | to shop only second hand for | 30 days. It’s a stand against | fast fashion and the damage | it does to our planet - the | textile industry accounts for up | to 10% of global greenhouse | gas emissions. The wonderful | Sienna Miller was the face of this | year’s campaign and we loved | seeing all your amazing second | hand outfits on Instagram. Get | some inspiration by searching | #SecondHandSeptember. | GIVE GIRLS A | BRIGHTER FUTURE | WITH MOOMIN | =. 2017, Oxfam has felt We're launching a brand new | a strong connection to range that celebrates the | those that reside in Moomin’s love of reading | Moominvalley, sharing and writing because we | their values of know how important | equality, kindness literacy is in helping | and courage to people build | stand up for brighter futures. | what's right. | So, whether you | choose a mug, | pin badge, or | bookmark, you'll | be helping women | and girls around | the world. | You'll find Moomin’s | friendly faces on | wonderful products | in our shops and | online, and each | one sold helps to | break down barriers | that keep women and | girls in poverty. | As Moominpappa says | “The world is full | of great and | So far, over ron Voth ees, te wonderful things | Elmillionhas it Mi. WZ \\iuaie™ for those who are | been raised. 2 ee pee MyeeNe — ready for them.” | © Moomin Characters TM | PERFECT FOR PRESSIES | Our Moomin range is available online and on the highstreet from November 2021 | oxfam.org.uk/moomins | ete ta | FRG i i | OXFAM | INSIDE Wishing you The hunger | The fight for an ethical . pandemic | climate justice Christmas The triple threat | The incredible Thoughtful gifts that’s fuelling | impact of joint we 3 that help people a crisis - one | people power (gy £ and planet family’s story > : | Featured products are subject to availability. Photos: MM Studios/Oxfam ""a uz GSAT | | BUILD BRIGHTER FUTURES | Beating poverty for | good means giving | people support beyond | basics like food and | water. It’s about | breaking down the | barriers that keep | people poor. | Getting more children into school. | Making sure families can get the | healthcare they need. Defending | women’s rights - to things like land | and earning an income. It all starts | with gifts like this one. A decent | education can change everything, | but many of the world’s poorest | children miss out. This is even more | likely if you're a girl. Your gift can help | reset the balance. Gifts like these | make it all possible. | GOT YOU A... 4 | GIFT CARD | A WORLD OF | DIFFERENCE FOR WOMEN | EQUALITY | FOR ALL | EDUCATION | FOR GIRLS | | GOT YOU... | A GIFT THAT PROVIDES | EDUCATION FOR GIRLS £20 | SUPPORT WOMEN ENTREPRENEURS £20 | WOMEN’S LEADERSHIP ELS | CARE FOR CARERS £20 | | GOT YOU AN... | GIFT CARD | EQUAL VOICE | FOR WOMEN | £15 £15 £10 | 4 WWW.OXFAMUNWRAPPED.COM | | GOT YOU A... | KEEP THE | WATER | RUNNING | GIFT CARD | | GOT YOU... | KEEP THE WATER RUNNING £30 | A WONDERFUL WATER PUMP £25 | visit YoUR Loca. OXFAM SHOP | That’s why Oxfam works in some of | the world’s harshest environments, | digging wells and installing | water pumps, taps and toilets so | communities have the water they | need and can protect | themselves against deadly | disease. And it doesn’t stop | there. If people spend less | | time fetching water, they'll | have more time to spend | making a living, and beating | poverty for good. Gifts like | this one make it all happen. | | | GOT YOU Asc) lien | cal. 0300 2001252 | It can give emergency food and | clean, safe water during a crisis. | It can provide shelter and warmth. | And, for people forced to flee their | homes and pushed into poverty, it | can give support, protection - | and the chance of a new start. | Give differently this Christmas, | and help make it all possible. | | GOT YOU A... | HEALTHIER | START | GIFT CARD | | GOT YOU... | A GIFT OF LIGHT | EMERGENCY CASH | A HEALTHIER START | HOPE WITH SOAP | 1GOT YOU A... | meSHELTER | WARMTH | GIFT CARD | | GOT YOU A... | HOPE FORA | REFUGEE | GIFT CARD | od EIR | FAMUNWRAPPED.CO | a.coN = | | GOT YOU A... | GIFT CARD | | GOT YOU... | A GIFT THAT BUILDS FARMS | HELP PEOPLE MAKE A LIVING | HELP FOR FARMERS FACING CLIMATE CHANGE £10 | SUPER SEEDS | PILE OF POO | HEROIC HONEY BEES | 1 GOT YOU A... | GIFT CARD | A LIFE-CHANGING | CHICKEN | | GOT YOU A... | GIFT CARD | FANTASTIC | FARM KIT | Living in poverty, | families can struggle to | put food on the table. | But the chance to earn | a decent income can | change everything - | and help people to beat | poverty for good. | That's why Oxfam provides families | with goats, pigs and chickens | to help them get started. It’s | why we provide training, to help | people grow more crops, raise | more animals and earn more | income. And why we help farmers | with brilliant, poverty-busting | ideas